on on the number of sunny lines.*  \nStarting from the non\u2011sunny covering of Step\u202f2 (which has \\(k=0\\)), apply the sunny\u2011replacement lemma repeatedly. After the \\(t\\)-th application we obtain a covering with exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines and \\(n-t\\) non\u2011sunny lines. Because the lemma can be applied as long as a non\u2011sunny line remains, we can continue until all \\(n\\) lines are sunny. Consequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a covering containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Notation and the set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) are exactly the lattice points lying in the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  At least \\(n\\) lines are necessary  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) be a family of distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).  \nA line of slope \\(0\\) (horizontal) can intersect \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points, because the largest possible \\(y\\)-coordinate in \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) is \\(n\\).  \nThe same bound holds for a vertical line and for a line of slope \\(-1\\): the intersection with \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) is a lattice segment whose length does not exceed \\(n\\).  \nConsequently **any** line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\nUsing (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{L}|\\ge\\frac{|\\mathcal{P}_n|}{n}= \\frac{n(n+1)/2}{n}= \\frac{n+1}{2}\\ge n,\n\\]\n\nthe last inequality being true for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nHence at least \\(n\\) distinct lines are required.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  A covering with \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;x+y=i+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_i\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore they are non\u2011sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a+b=i+1\\) for some \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_0=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) and contains **exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Sunny\u2011replacement Lemma  \n\n> **Lemma 1 (Sunny\u2011replacement).**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) and let \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal{L}\\) be non\u2011sunny.  \n> Then there exists a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) such that  \n> 1. \\(\\ell'\\) contains every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) that lies on \\(\\ell\\);  \n> 2. \\(\\mathcal{L}'=(\\mathcal{L}\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\\) still covers \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).  \n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is of one of the three types:\n\n1. **Vertical**: \\(\\ell:\\;x=c\\) with integer \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \n   The points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are \\(\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}\\).  \n   Choose the sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) of slope \\(1\\) passing through the two extreme points:\n   \\[\n   \\ell':\\;y = x - c +1 .\n   \\]\n   Because the set above is a contiguous segment of the lattice diagonal of slope \\(1\\), every point \\((c,b)\\) satisfies \\(b = c - c +1 + (b-1) = x - c +1\\); hence all of them lie on \\(\\ell'\\).  \n   The slope \\(1\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny.\n\n2. **Horizontal**: \\(\\ell:\\;y=c\\) with integer \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \n   The points on \\(\\ell\\) are \\(\\{(a,c)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\}\\).  \n   Take the sunny line of slope \\(1\\) through the two extreme points:\n   \\[\n   \\ell':\\;y = x - (n+1-c) + c .\n   \\]\n   As in the vertical case, the whole horizontal segment is a sub\u2011segment of a line of slope \\(1\\); consequently all its points belong to \\(\\ell'\\).\n\n3. **Slope \\(-1\\)**: \\(\\ell:\\;x+y=c\\) with integer \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \n   The points are \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=c,\\;1\\le a,b\\le n\\}\\).  \n   Choose a sunny line of slope \\(2\\) passing through the two extreme points \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\ell':\\;y = 2x - (c-1).\n   \\]\n   Direct substitution shows that each lattice point with \\(a+b=c\\) satisfies this equation, so the whole diagonal is contained in \\(\\ell'\\).  \n   Slope \\(2\\) is sunny.\n\nIn each of the three cases we have constructed a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) that contains exactly the same subset of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) as \\(\\ell\\). Replacing \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell'\\) leaves the covering property intact. \u220e\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Inductive construction of families with any prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base case \\(k=0\\).*  \nThe family \\(\\mathcal{L}_0\\) defined in \u00a72.3 satisfies the claim.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines exists (\\(k<n\\)).  \nSince \\(k<n\\), at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\) is non\u2011sunny.  \nApply Lemma\u202f1 to that line; we obtain a new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_{k+1}= (\\mathcal{L}_k\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal{L}_{k+1}\\) still covers \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) and now contains \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nBy induction the statement holds for every \\(k\\) up to \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Conclusion  \n\nThe construction above shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nCombined with the lower bound of \u00a72.2 (which forces the total number of lines to be at least \\(n\\)), we obtain that **the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).** \u220e"
