            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  The set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  At least \\(n\\) lines are necessary  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any family of distinct lines that covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nA vertical line, a horizontal line, or a line of slope \\(-1\\) can intersect \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points, because the longest such lattice segment inside the triangle has length \\(n\\).  \nConsequently **any** line (sunny or not) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nUsing (1),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|\\ge\\frac{|\\mathcal P_n|}{n}= \\frac{n(n+1)/2}{n}= \\frac{n+1}{2}\\ge n\n\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\n\\]\n\nHence at least \\(n\\) distinct lines are required.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  A covering with no sunny line  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;x+y=i+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_i\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore they are non\u2011sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a+b=i+1\\) for a unique \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); thus \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\).  \nHence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Sunny\u2011replacement Lemma  \n\n> **Lemma 1 (Sunny\u2011replacement).**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and let \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) be non\u2011sunny.  \n> Then there exists a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) such that  \n> 1. every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that lies on \\(\\ell\\) also lies on \\(\\ell'\\);  \n> 2. the family \\(\\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\\) still covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is of one of the three types.\n\n1. **Vertical line** \\(\\ell:\\;x=c\\) with integer \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \n   The points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are  \n   \\[\n   S=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}.\n   \\]\n   Choose the sunny line of slope \\(1\\) passing through the two extreme points of \\(S\\):\n   \\[\n   \\ell':\\;y = x-c+1 .\n   \\]\n   For any \\((c,b)\\in S\\) we have \\(b = c-c+1+(b-1)=x-c+1\\); hence \\((c,b)\\in\\ell'\\).  \n   The slope \\(1\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny.  \n   Because \\(\\ell'\\) is not vertical, it cannot coincide with any other vertical line in \\(\\mathcal L\\); it also cannot coincide with any non\u2011vertical line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (otherwise that line would already contain the whole set \\(S\\), contradicting the distinctness of the original family). Thus \\(\\ell'\\) is distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\).\n\n2. **Horizontal line** \\(\\ell:\\;y=c\\) with integer \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \n   The points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are  \n   \\[\n   S=\\{(a,c)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\}.\n   \\]\n   Again take the sunny line of slope \\(1\\) through the two extreme points:\n   \\[\n   \\ell':\\;y = x-(n+1-c)+c .\n   \\]\n   A direct substitution shows that every \\((a,c)\\in S\\) satisfies this equation, so \\(S\\subset\\ell'\\).  \n   The same distinctness argument as in the vertical case applies, proving that \\(\\ell'\\) is new.\n\n3. **Slope \\(-1\\) line** \\(\\ell:\\;x+y=c\\) with integer \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \n   The points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are  \n   \\[\n   S=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=c,\\;1\\le a,b\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n   Let \\(\\ell'\\) be the line of slope \\(2\\) passing through the two extreme points \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\ell':\\;y = 2x-(c-1).\n   \\]\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S\\) we have \\(b = c-a = 2a-(c-1)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell'\\); thus \\(S\\subset\\ell'\\).  \n   Slope \\(2\\) is sunny, and \\(\\ell'\\) cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\) because none of those lines has slope \\(2\\). Hence \\(\\ell'\\) is distinct.\n\nIn each case we have produced a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) that contains all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) formerly covered by \\(\\ell\\) and is distinct from the remaining lines. Therefore \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines. \u220e\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Inductive construction of families with any prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base case \\(k=0\\).*  \nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) constructed in \u00a72.3 satisfies the claim.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, where \\(k<n\\).  \nBecause \\(k<n\\), at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) is non\u2011sunny.  \nApply Lemma\u202f1 to that line; we obtain a new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k+1}= (\\mathcal L_k\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny.  \nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) remain covered, the number of sunny lines increases by one, and the total number of lines stays \\(n\\).  \n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{k+1}\\) satisfies the required properties.  \n\nBy induction, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown:\n\n* At least \\(n\\) lines are necessary to cover the set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\);\n* A covering with \\(n\\) lines and \\(0\\) sunny lines exists;\n* Using the Sunny\u2011replacement Lemma we can increase the number of sunny lines one by one while preserving a covering with exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
