e last line has slope \\(-1\\); consequently none of them is sunny. \u220e  \n\nChoose \\(m=n+1\\).  \nLemma\u202f1 yields a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines that covers \\(T_n\\).  \nHence the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Increasing the number of sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma\u202f2 (Sunny\u2011Insertion Lemma).**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k<n\\) sunny lines.  \nThen there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(k<n\\), at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non\u2011sunny; denote it by \\(\\ell\\).\n\nEach line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\) (the set \\(T_n\\) has \\(\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}>n\\) points).  \nConsequently, by the pigeon\u2011hole principle, some point \\(P\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) lies on another line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) as well.  \nFix such a point \\(P\\).\n\nSelect a slope  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\n\\]\n\nthat is different from the slopes of all sunny lines already present (there are only finitely many forbidden slopes, so such a \\(\\sigma\\) exists).  \nLet \\(\\ell'\\) be the unique line through \\(P\\) with slope \\(\\sigma\\).  \nBy construction \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny and cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (different slope from all sunny lines, and not parallel to the direction of any non\u2011sunny line).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe verify that \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n* Any point of \\(T_n\\) that was on a sunny line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) remains covered, because those lines are unchanged.\n* Any point \\(Q\\in T_n\\) that was on \\(\\ell\\) but not on any other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) would have to be the unique point of \\(\\ell\\) not shared with another line.  \n  However, by the choice of \\(P\\) we know that at least one point of \\(\\ell\\) (namely \\(P\\)) is shared; the remaining points of \\(\\ell\\) are each incident with some other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (otherwise \\(\\ell\\) would be the only line covering them, contradicting the existence of the shared point \\(P\\)).  \n  Hence every such \\(Q\\) is still covered by that other line.\n* The point \\(P\\) itself is now covered by the newly added sunny line \\(\\ell'\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L'\\).  \nThe family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, and its number of sunny lines is \\(k+1\\) (we removed a non\u2011sunny line and added a sunny one). \u220e  \n\nStarting from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) (which has \\(k=0\\) sunny lines) and applying Lemma\u202f2 repeatedly, we obtain for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. No larger value of \\(k\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition contains \\(k>n\\) sunny lines.  \nSince the configuration has only \\(n\\) lines, it cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines\u2014a direct contradiction.  \nTherefore no value \\(k>n\\) is possible. The lower bound \\(k\\ge 0\\) is immediate because the number of sunny lines is a non\u2011negative integer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two given conditions is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\