t is complete and correct. All points of \\(T_n\\) are indeed covered, and none of the lines is sunny (they are horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\)). Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n### 3. Lemma\u202f2 (Sunny\u2011Insertion Lemma)\n\n#### 3.1. Claim that every line contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\)\n- **Quote:** \u201cEach line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\) (the set \\(T_n\\) has \\(\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}>n\\) points).\u201d\n- **Issue:** This is **false**.  \n  The fact that \\(T_n\\) has more points than the number of lines only guarantees that **some** line contains at least two points (by the pigeon\u2011hole principle), not that **every** line does. A line in a covering could intersect \\(T_n\\) in a single point (e.g., a line of slope \\(2\\) passing through only \\((1,1)\\) in \\(T_3\\)). Therefore the statement is incorrect.\n\n#### 3.2. Misapplication of the pigeon\u2011hole principle\n- **Quote:** \u201cConsequently, by the pigeon\u2011hole principle, some point \\(P\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) lies on another line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) as well.\u201d\n- **Issue:** The conclusion does **not** follow from the (incorrect) premise.  \n  Even if at least one line contains two points, it does **not** guarantee that a particular non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\) shares a point with another line. A non\u2011sunny line could be the **only** line covering each of its points (e.g., a horizontal line \\(y=2\\) covering only \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) while the other lines avoid these points). Hence the existence of such a shared point \\(P\\) is not established.\n\n#### 3.3. Construction of the new sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) and preservation of the covering\n- **Quote:** \u201cSelect a slope \\(\\sigma\\) different from the slopes of all sunny lines already present\u2026 Let \\(\\ell'\\) be the unique line through \\(P\\) with slope \\(\\sigma\\)\u2026 We verify that \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still covers \\(T_n\\).\u201d\n- **Issue:** Because the existence of the point \\(P\\) is not guaranteed, the removal of the non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\) may leave points of \\(T_n\\) uncovered. The proof that \u201cevery element of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L'\\)\u201d assumes that each point of \\(\\ell\\) (except possibly \\(P\\)) is also on another line, which was never justified. Consequently the argument that the covering is preserved is unsound.\n\n#### 3.4. Consequence for the overall claim\n- The lemma is intended to show that, starting from a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(k<n\\)), one can always obtain a configuration with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.  \n- Since Lemma\u202f2 is invalid, the inductive step fails. The solution therefore does **not** prove that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is realizable. In fact, for \\(n=3\\) a direct search shows that \\(k=2\\) cannot be achieved, contradicting the claimed result.\n\n### 4. \u201cNo larger value of \\(k\\)\u201d argument\n- **Statement:** \u201cAssume a configuration with \\(k>n\\) sunny lines\u2026 impossible because there are only \\(n\\) lines.\u201d\n- **Verification:** This is a trivial and correct observation; it does not compensate for the flaw in Lemma\u202f2.\n\n### 5. Conclusion\n- The solution correctly handles the cases \\(k=0\\) (Lemma\u202f1) and the trivial upper bound \\(k\\le n\\).  \n- However, the central claim that all intermediate values of \\(k\\) are attainable rests on Lemma\u202f2, which contains a **critical logical error** (false claim about every line containing two points, misuse of the pigeon\u2011hole principle, and an unjustified preservation of the covering after line replacement).  \n- Because of this error, the solution does **not** establish the full set of possible \\(k\\) values and is therefore **invalid**."
