uration exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the impossibility proved by the pigeon\u2011hole argument (4) with the explicit construction for all admissible \\(k\\), we obtain the complete description of the set of possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1   Counting the points of \\(S\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) contributes exactly \\(s-1\\) points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2   How many points can a line contain?\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); i.e. its equation is one of  \n\n\\[\ny=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).  \nFor such a line the intersection with \\(S\\) is a set of lattice points lying on a horizontal, vertical or \\(-1\\)\u2011slope diagonal segment. In each case the number of points is at most \\(n\\); the bound is attained by the lines \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\) and \\(x+y=2\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every non\u2011sunny line contains at most }n\\text{ points of }S.} \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nA **sunny** line has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\). Any two distinct points of the integer lattice determine a line; if the line is sunny then it can contain at most two points of \\(S\\) (otherwise three collinear points would force the slope to be one of the forbidden values). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line contains at most }2\\text{ points of }S.} \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3   Pigeon\u2011hole lower bound for the number of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nAssume that among the required \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny and the remaining  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k\n\\]\n\nare non\u2011sunny.  \nUsing (2.2) and (2.3), the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\ntn+2k = tn+2(n-t)=t(n-2)+2n . \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nSince all points of \\(S\\) must be covered, (2.1) and (2.4) give  \n\n\\[\nt(n-2)+2n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\frac{\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2n}{\\,n-2\\,}\n   =\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-2)}\n   =\\frac{n+1}{2}\\quad\\text{(after taking the ceiling).}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t=n-k\\), inequality (2.5) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nHence **no configuration can have more than \\(\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4   Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k\\qquad\\Bigl(t\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.4.1   Covering \\(S\\) with \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nm=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{t}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nConsider the following \\(t\\) lines:\n\n* the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m ;\n  \\tag{2.7}\n  \\]\n\n* the \\(t-m\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=t-m .\n  \\tag{2.8}\n  \\]\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in S\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(t\\ge\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{either } a\\le m\\quad\\text{or}\\quad b\\le t-m .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on one of the lines (2.7) or (2.8).  \nThus the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines (2.7)\u2013(2.8) **cover all points of \\(S\\