            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1   Number of points to be covered\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) contributes exactly \\(s-1\\) lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2   Maximum number of points a line can contain\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); i.e. its equation is one of  \n\n\\[\ny=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal* (\\(y=c\\)) and *vertical* (\\(x=c\\)) lines intersect the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points; the line \\(x+y=c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) also meets \\(S\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (the line \\(x+y=2\\) attains the bound). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every non\u2011sunny line contains at most }n\\text{ points of }S.} \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nA **sunny** line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). If a sunny line contained three distinct points of \\(S\\), those three points would be collinear with slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), a contradiction. Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line contains at most }2\\text{ points of }S.} \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3   Pigeon\u2011hole lower bound for the number of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nAssume that among the required \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny; the remaining  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k\n\\]\n\nare non\u2011sunny.  \nUsing (2.2) and (2.3), the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\ntn+2k = tn+2(n-t)=t(n-2)+2n . \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S\\) must be covered, so by (2.1) and (2.4)\n\n\\[\nt(n-2)+2n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\frac{\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2n}{\\,n-2\\,}\n   =\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\) is an integer we obtain  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t=n-k\\), inequality (2.5) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can have more than \\(\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4   Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (2.6) and set  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k\\qquad\\Bigl(t\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4.1   Covering \\(S\\) with \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nm=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{t}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nConsider the following \\(t\\) lines:\n\n* the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m ; \\tag{2.7}\n  \\]\n\n* the \\(t-m\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=t-m . \\tag{2.8}\n  \\]\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(t\\ge\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{either } a\\le m\\quad\\text{or}\\quad b\\le t-m .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on one of the lines (2.7) or (2.8).  \nTherefore the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines (2.7)\u2013(2.8) **cover all points of \\(S\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4.2   Adding \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nThe grid formed by the vertical lines (2.7) and the horizontal lines (2.8) contains at least two rows and two columns (because \\(t\\ge\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\ge2\\)). Consequently there exist many unordered pairs of points of \\(S\\) that are **not** aligned horizontally, vertically or on a line of slope \\(-1\\). Choose any \\(k\\) such distinct pairs \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).\n\nFor each pair draw the unique line \\(\\ell_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\). By construction the slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny. The lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct because each contains a different pair of points, and none of them coincides with any of the vertical or horizontal lines already used.\n\nNow we have the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\text{the }t\\text{ lines (2.7)\u2013(2.8)}\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of exactly \\(t+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(S\\) remains covered: a point that originally lay on a vertical or horizontal line is still covered by that line; a point that belongs to a chosen pair \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) is also covered by the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\). Hence the two required conditions are satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5   Final conclusion\n\nThe pigeon\u2011hole argument forces  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThe construction above shows that for **every** integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch a configuration exists. Therefore the set of all admissible non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
