ed}\n&L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\n&L_{n-1}:\\;y=1,\\\\\n&L_{n}:\\;x+y=n .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (one of the \\(L_i\\)).  \n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(y=1\\).  \n* If \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\) and again the point lies on \\(y=1\\).  \n* The remaining possibility is \\(a\\ge n-1\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\); the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a+b=n\\), so the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines, establishing the covering. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Lemma 4 \u2013 Replacing a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny line  \n\n**Lemma 4.**  Let \\(L\\) be any line from the family of Lemma\u202f3.  \nThere exists a sunny line \\(L'\\) that contains **all** points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) that lie on \\(L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set \\(L\\cap\\mathcal T_n\\) contains at least two points (otherwise the line would be superfluous).  \nLet \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\) be the two points of this set with minimal and maximal first coordinate, respectively.  \nThe line through them has slope  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the two points are not on a horizontal line (\\(b_2\\neq b_1\\)), not on a vertical line (\\(a_2\\neq a_1\\)), and not on a line of slope \\(-1\\) (otherwise they would belong to a diagonal line of the forbidden family, which is impossible for a vertical or horizontal line), we have \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nHence the line \\(L'\\) determined by \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\) is sunny.  \nSince both extreme points belong to \\(L\\cap\\mathcal T_n\\) and every other point of this set lies between them, all points of \\(L\\cap\\mathcal T_n\\) lie on \\(L'\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Lemma 5 \u2013 Existence for