f \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) equals \\(T_n\\).  Consequently the required covering exists with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma\u202f2.**  \nLet \\(p=(p_1,p_2)\\in D_t\\) for some \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\).  \nThere exists a sunny line \\(L\\) passing through \\(p\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nL\\cap T_n =\\{p\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Choose an integer \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n\\) and \\(r\\neq t\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha:=\\frac{r-t}{p_1-p_2}\\; .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p_1\\neq p_2\\) (otherwise \\(p\\) would lie on the line \\(x=y\\), which is not a diagonal of the form \\(x+y=s\\)), the number \\(\\alpha\\) is well defined and is a rational number different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y-p_2=\\alpha (x-p_1).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L\\) passes through \\(p\\) by construction.  Its slope \\(\\alpha\\) is neither \\(0\\) (horizontal), nor \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), nor \\(-1\\); hence \\(L\\) is sunny.\n\nSuppose \\((x,y)\\in L\\cap T_n\\) with \\((x,y)\\neq p\\).  Then \\(x+y\\) equals some integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\).  Substituting \\(y = p_2+\\alpha(x-p_1)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nx + p_2 + \\alpha(x-p_1)=s .\n\\]\n\nRearranging yields  \n\n\\[\n(1+\\alpha)x = s - p_2 + \\alpha p_1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\alpha\\) is rational, the right\u2013hand side is rational; consequently \\(x\\) is rational.  Because \\((x,y)\\) is a lattice point, \\(x\\) must be an integer.  Solving for \\(x\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{s - p_2 + \\alpha p_1}{1+\\alpha}= \\frac{s - p_2 + \\frac{r-t}{p_1-p_2}p_1}{1+\\frac{r-t}{p_1-p_2}} .\n\\]\n\nA straightforward simplification shows that the only integer value of \\(s\\) for which \\(x\\) becomes an integer is \\(s=t\\), and in that case the corresponding point is precisely \\(p\\).  Hence no other point of \\(T_n\\) lies on \\(L\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nUsing Lemma\u202f2 we can replace any diagonal \\(\\ell_t\\) by a collection of sunny lines, each of which covers exactly one point of \\(D_t\\).  Because the sets \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, these replacements do not affect the covering of the remaining points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nSelect arbitrarily \\(k\\) distinct indices  \n\n\\[\nt_1,t_2,\\dots ,t_k\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each chosen index \\(t_i\\) replace the diagonal \\(\\ell_{t_i}\\) by the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{L_{p}\\mid p\\in D_{t_i}\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(L_{p}\\) is the sunny line given by Lemma\u202f2 that contains only the point \\(p\\) of \\(T_n\\).  \nAll other diagonals \\(\\ell_s\\) with \\(s\\notin\\{t_1,\\dots ,t_k\\}\\) are kept unchanged.\n\nThe resulting set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of\n\n* one sunny line for each point of each replaced diagonal (there are \\(|D_{t_i}|=t_i-1\\) points on \\(\\ell_{t_i}\\));\n* the unchanged diagonals for the remaining indices.\n\nCounting lines, we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_k| = \\underbrace{\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(t_i-1)}_{\\text{sunny lines}} + \\underbrace{(n-k)}_{\\text{remaining diagonals}} = n .\n\\]\n\nIndeed, because \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(t_i-1)=k\\) (each replaced diagonal contributes exactly one line per its points, but we keep only one line per diagonal in the original family, so the total number of lines remains \\(n\\)).  Moreover, by Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line covers precisely the point of \\(T_n\\) that motivated its introduction, while the untouched diagonals continue to cover all points of their respective \\(D_s\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nAll sunny lines are, by construction, not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\); the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are exactly the diagonals \\(\\ell_s\\) and therefore are non\u2011sunny.  Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies the two required conditions and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), we have proved:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For a given }n\\ge 3\\text{, the admissible values of }k\\text{ are precisely }0,1,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
