   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{k} i = \\frac{k(k+1)}2 ,\n   \\]\n\n   which coincides with (2).  Consequently **all** points whose abscissa belongs to \\(A_2\\) are covered by the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n5. **Verification of the whole construction.**  \n\n   * The \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) are pairwise distinct and non\u2011sunny.  \n   * The \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise distinct (they have different intercepts) and are sunny.  \n   * Every point of \\(S_n\\) either has its abscissa in \\(A_1\\) (hence lies on the corresponding vertical line) or its abscissa in \\(A_2\\) (hence lies on one of the \\(L_i\\) by the construction above).  \n\n   Therefore the family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines satisfies both requirements of the problem.\n\n6. **Extremal cases.**  \n\n   * For \\(k=0\\) we simply take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all of them are non\u2011sunny.  \n   * For \\(k=n\\) we take the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) defined by (3); there are no vertical lines in this case.\n\nSince the construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), these are precisely the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nDefine two subsets of the abscissas:\n\n\\[\nA_1=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\},\\qquad \nA_2=\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Non\u2011sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(a\\in A_1\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\;x=a .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(V_a\\) are distinct, have slope \\(\\infty\\) and therefore are **not** sunny.  \nThere are exactly \\(n-k\\) such lines.\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\in A_1\\) lies on the line \\(V_a\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x-(n-k+i)+i .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of each \\(L_i\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe intercepts \\(- (n-k+i)+i = -n+k\\) are distinct for different \\(i\\), so the lines \\(L_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nWe claim that \\(L_i\\) contains exactly the \\(i\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_{i,j}=(n-k+i+j-1,\\;j),\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,i .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, substituting \\((x,y)=(n-k+i+j-1,j)\\) into (6) gives  \n\n\\[\nj = (n-k+i+j-1)-(n-k+i)+i = j,\n\\]\n\nso the point satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\).  \nConversely, if \\((x,y)\\in S_n\\) lies on \\(L_i\\) then \\(y=x-(n-k+i)+i\\); solving for \\(x\\) yields \\(x=n-k+i+y-1\\).  \nSince \\(y\\ge1\\) and \\(x\\le n\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), we obtain \\(1\\le y\\le i\\), which forces \\((x,y)\\) to be one of the points listed in (7).  \nThus (7) is precisely the set of points of \\(S_n\\) that belong to \\(L_i\\).\n\nThe sets \\(\\{P_{i,j}\\mid 1\\le j\\le i\\}\\) for different \\(i\\) are disjoint, because the abscissas in (7) are \\(n-k+i,\\dots ,n-k+2i-1\\), which do not overlap for distinct \\(i\\).  \nConsequently the total number of points covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} i = \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  All points are covered\n\nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) has \\(a\\in A_1\\) it lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \nIf \\(a\\in A_2\\) then \\(a=n-k+i\\) for a unique \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a = k+1-i\\).  \nHence \\(b\\) is one of the integers \\(1,\\dots ,i\\), and by (7) the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n\nThus **every** point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Counting the lines\n\nThe construction 