S_n\\) into a vertical part (covered by non\u2011sunny lines) and a smaller triangular part (to be covered by sunny lines).  \n   The natural idea is to use \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) to cover the smaller triangle.  \n   However, a detailed check shows that such lines inevitably miss some lattice points of the triangle; the attempted formulas (3)\u2013(4) in the previous answer do not produce a covering when \\(k\\ge 1\\).  \n   Consequently the argument that \u201cevery \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) works\u2019\u2019 cannot be salvaged without a substantially different construction, which I have not found.\n\nThus, at present the only rigorously established admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(n\\). The status of the intermediate integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) remains open.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(S_n\\) is\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_j\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence it is **not** sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(V_a\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nConsequently \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{i+1}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\nWe claim that every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).\n\nIndeed, solve (2) for \\(i\\):\n\\[\nb = \\frac{i}{i+1}a + \\frac{1}{i+1}\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n(b-1)a = i(a-b+1).\n\\]\nSince \\(a,b\\) are positive integers with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the integer\n\\[\ni = \\frac{(b-1)a}{a-b+1}\n\\]\nis well defined, belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), and satisfies the equation above; consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).  \nConversely, if \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (2) for some \\(i\\) then the same computation shows that the above formula yields that same \\(i\\).  \n\nHence the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) cover all points of \\(S_n\\), and exactly \\(k=n\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Remarks on intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\nA natural attempt to obtain intermediate values is to keep \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (covering the columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-k\\)) and to try to cover the remaining \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points of the right\u2011hand triangular region by \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThe simplest idea\u2014using lines of slope \\(1\\) that pass through the \u201cstaircase\u2019\u2019 of points\u2014fails: for \\(k\\ge 1\\) such lines leave uncovered points (for example, when \\(n=5,\\;k=2\\) the point \\((4,2)\\) is missed).  \n\nMore sophisticated arrangements (different slopes, non\u2011parallel families, etc.) have not yielded a complete covering for any \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  Consequently, at present we can only assert rigorously that the admissible values of \\(k\\) include \\(0\\) and \\(n\\); the existence (or impossibility) of the intermediate values remains an open problem."
