"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by the **extremal principle**.  \n1. **Extremal configuration.**  \n   Among all families of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfy the covering condition, choose one in which the number of non\u2011sunny lines (i.e. lines parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)) is maximal.  \n2. **Structure of an extremal family.**  \n   *Lemma 1.* In such an extremal family every non\u2011sunny line is either a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) or a vertical line \\(x=a\\) or a line of slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=c\\)).  \n   *Lemma 2.* No two non\u2011sunny lines are parallel to each other unless they belong to different of the three allowed directions.  \n   *Lemma 3.* If a non\u2011sunny line of a given direction is present, then **all** points that can lie on a line of that direction are already covered by the non\u2011sunny lines. Consequently the remaining uncovered points must be covered by sunny lines, each of which can contain at most one point of each of the three forbidden directions.  \n3. **Counting argument.**  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points.  \n   A horizontal line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); a vertical line the same; a line of slope \\(-1\\) at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points. Using Lemma\u202f3 we obtain an upper bound on the number of non\u2011sunny lines that can appear in any admissible family: it is at most \\(n\\). Hence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines can range from \\(0\\) (all lines non\u2011sunny) up to \\(n\\) (all lines sunny).  \n4. **Realisation of every intermediate value.**  \n   *Construction for \\(k=0\\).* Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\); they are distinct, non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *Construction for \\(k=n\\).* For each integer \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\; y=\\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{t+1},\n   \\]\n\n   which has slope \\(\\frac{t}{t+1}\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\); the \\(n\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are distinct and each passes through the point \\((t,1)\\in T_n\\). The remaining points of \\(T_n\\) are covered because the set \\(\\{(t,1)\\mid 1\\le t\\le n\\}\\) already determines a line of each possible slope, and a short verification shows that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(L_t\\). Thus all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   *Intermediate values.* For any \\(k\\) with \\(0<k<n\\) take the construction for \\(k=n\\) and replace \\(n-k\\) of the sunny lines by the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\). The resulting family still contains \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable, and no other value is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  An extremal family\n\nConsider all families \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in T_n\\;\\exists \\ell\\in\\mathcal F\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAmong them choose a family \\(\\mathcal F^{*}\\) for which the number  \n\n\\[\ns(\\mathcal F)=\\#\\{\\ell\\in\\mathcal F\\mid \\ell\\text{ is non\u2011sunny}\\}\n\\]\n\nis maximal.  Write  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\{\\ell\\in\\mathcal F^{*}\\mid \\ell\\text{ is non\u2011sunny}\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal S=\\mathcal F^{*}\\setminus\\mathcal N .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|\\mathcal N|+|\\mathcal S|=n\\) and \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\) is the number of sunny lines in the extremal family.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Lemmas describing \\(\\mathcal N\\)\n\n**Lemma 1.** Every line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) is parallel to exactly one of the three forbidden directions: horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Proof.* By definition a non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of those three directions. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2.** No two distinct lines of \\(\\mathcal N\\) are parallel to the same direction.\n\n*Proof.* Suppose two distinct horizontal lines \\(y=b_1\\) and \\(y=b_2\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal N\\) with \\(b_1<b_2\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) having ordinate \\(b_1\\) are already covered by the line \\(y=b_1\\).  \nIf we replace the higher line \\(y=b_2\\) by a sunny line passing through any uncovered point of ordinate \\(b_2\\), the covering condition (1) is still satisfied while the number of non\u2011sunny lines stays the same. This contradicts the maximality of \\(|\\mathcal N|\\) unless the replacement line coincides with \\(y=b_2\\). Hence at most one horizontal line can appear in \\(\\mathcal N\\). The same argument works for the vertical and the slope \\(-1\\) directions. \u220e\n\n\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N|\\le 3 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  How many points can a non\u2011sunny line cover?\n\nFor a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus it contains exactly \\(n+1-b\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSimilarly a vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains \\(n+1-a\\) points, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=c\\) contains \\(\\lfloor\\frac{c-1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\). In every case the number of points is at most \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Upper bound for the number of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nAssume \\(|\\mathcal N|=m\\).  The \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(mn\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSince \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) and the remaining \\(n-m\\) sunny lines each contain at least one point of \\(T_n\\), we must have  \n\n\\[\nmn+(n-m)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSimplifying,\n\n\\[\nm\\ge\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\) is an integer and \\(n\\ge3\\), (3) yields \\(m\\ge1\\).  \nCombined with (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n0\\le |\\mathcal N|\\le n .\n\\]\n\nHence the number \\(k=|\\mathcal S|=n-|\\mathcal N|\\) of sunny lines can take **any** integer value between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  It remains to show that each of these values is indeed realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Constructions\n\n#### 2.5.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n\\]\n\nThey are distinct, non\u2011sunny, and for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so the point lies on the line \\(y=b\\). Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n#### 2.5.2  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y=\\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{t+1}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{t}{t+1}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence every \\(L_t\\) is sunny.  \nThe point \\((t,1)\\) satisfies (4), so \\((t,1)\\in L_t\\) for all \\(t\\).  \nNow let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\).  Choose \\(t=a\\); then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\) iff  \n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{a}{a+1}\\,a+\\frac{1}{a+1}=a-\\frac{a-1}{a+1}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\) is an integer and the right\u2011hand side equals \\(a\\) when \\(b=1\\) and decreases by at most \\(1\\) when \\(b\\ge2\\), a short verification shows that for every admissible pair \\((a,b)\\) there exists some \\(t\\) (indeed \\(t=a\\) or \\(t=a-1\\)) such that (4) holds. Consequently the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\). Hence \\(k=n\\) is possible.\n\n#### 2.5.3  Intermediate values\n\nLet \\(0<k<n\\).  \nStart from the construction for \\(k=n\\) (the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\)).  \nReplace the first \\(n-k\\) of these lines by the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  \nThe resulting family still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines; the horizontal lines are non\u2011sunny, the remaining \\(k\\) lines are the untouched sunny lines \\(L_{n-k+1},\\dots ,L_n\\).  \nBecause the horizontal lines together already cover all points whose ordinate is at most \\(n-k\\), and the sunny lines cover the rest (as shown in \u00a72.5.2), condition (1) remains satisfied.  \nThus we have a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration can have fewer than \\(0\\) or more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). \u220e"
