t of \\(L_a\\) between \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a,b)\\) is contained in the line. Consequently \\((a,b)\\) also belongs to \\(L_a\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).\n\n*Construction for intermediate values.*  \nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0<k<n\\).  \nBegin with the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) from the previous construction.  \nReplace the first \\(n-k\\) of them by the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-k .\n\\]\n\nThe resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines: the \\(n-k\\) horizontal ones are non\u2011sunny, the remaining \\(k\\) lines are the untouched sunny lines \\(L_{\\,n-k+1},\\dots ,L_n\\).  \n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-k\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \nIf \\(b>n-k\\) then \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\); in this case \\(a\\le n-(b-1)\\le n-(n-k)=k\\), so \\(a\\le k\\).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(L_{\\,a}\\) (which is still present because \\(a\\le k\\)).  \nHence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n*Extremal observation.*  \nBecause we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\); likewise it cannot be negative. Therefore the only possible values are the integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), all of which have been exhibited.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Impossibility of \\(k>n\\) or \\(k<0\\)\n\nWe must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must belong to the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Realising \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_0=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal F_0\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) satisfies the covering condition (1) and contains no sunny line, i.e. \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Realising \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y=\\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{t+1}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{t}{t+1}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence every \\(L_t\\) is sunny.  \nThe point \\((t,1)\\) satisfies (4), so \\((t,1)\\in L_t\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\(b=1\\)*, then \\((a,1)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge 2\\)*, note that the right\u2011hand side of (4) with \\(t=a\\) equals  \n\n\\[\ny_a(x)=\\frac{a}{a+1}x+\\frac{1}{a+1}.\n\\]\n\nSince the slope \\(\\frac{a}{a+1}\\) is positive, the function \\(y_a(x)\\) is increasing in \\(x\\).  \nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny_a(a)=\\frac{a}{a+1}\\,a+\\frac{1}{a+1}=a-\\frac{a-1}{a+1}\\ge a-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\le a\\) (from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\)) we have \\(b\\le a-1\\) or \\(b=a\\).  \nIn either case \\(b\\le y_a(a)\\), and by monotonicity of the line there exists a point on \\(L_a\\) with abscissa \\(a\\) and ordinate \\(b\\); that point is precisely \\((a,b)\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\in L_a\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\).  Consequently \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Realising an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0<k<n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \nStart from the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \u00a72.3 and replace the first \\(n-k\\) lines by the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-k .\n\\]\n\nDenote the resulting family by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\{\\,y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,L_{\\,n-k+1},\\dots ,L_n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe lines are distinct because the slopes of the \\(L_t\\) are all different from \\(0\\).  \nExactly \\(n-k\\) of them are horizontal (non\u2011sunny) and the remaining \\(k\\) are the sunny lines \\(L_{\\,n-k+1},\\dots ,L_n\\); hence \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe verify the covering condition.  \n\n*If \\(1\\le b\\le n-k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n*If \\(b>n-k\\)*, then \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a\\le k\\) and consequently \\(a\\le n\\).  \nSince \\(a\\le k\\), the sunny line \\(L_a\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) (it belongs to the set \\(\\{L_{\\,n-k+1},\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) because \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) is equivalent to \\(a>n-k\\); however when \\(a\\le k\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\) and \\(a\\) may be less than \\(n-k+1\\). To avoid this difficulty we modify the construction slightly: choose the sunny lines to be \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) instead of the last \\(k\\) lines. The family then is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\{\\,y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}.\n\\]\n\nNow for any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b>n-k\\) we have \\(a\\le k\\) as shown above, so the line \\(L_a\\) is indeed present in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\). By the argument of \u00a72.3 the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  \n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\), and \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains exactly \\