c{1}{t+1}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{t}{t+1}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence every \\(L_t\\) is sunny.  \nThe point \\((t,1)\\) satisfies (4), so \\((t,1)\\in L_t\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\(b=1\\)*, then \\((a,1)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge 2\\)*, note that the right\u2011hand side of (4) with \\(t=a\\) equals  \n\n\\[\ny_a(x)=\\frac{a}{a+1}x+\\frac{1}{a+1}.\n\\]\n\nSince the slope \\(\\frac{a}{a+1}\\) is positive, the function \\(y_a(x)\\) is increasing in \\(x\\).  \nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny_a(a)=\\frac{a}{a+1}\\,a+\\frac{1}{a+1}=a-\\frac{a-1}{a+1}\\ge a-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\le a\\) (from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\)) we have \\(b\\le a-1\\) or \\(b=a\\).  \nIn either case \\(b\\le y_a(a)\\), and by monotonicity of the line there exists a point on \\(L_a\\) with abscissa \\(a\\) and ordinate \\(b\\); that point is precisely \\((a,b)\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\in L_a\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\).  Consequently \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Realising an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0<k<n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \nStart from the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \u00a72.3 and replace the first \\(n-k\\) lines by the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-k .\n\\]\n\nDenote the resulting family by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\{\\,y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,L_{\\,n-k+1},\\dots ,L_n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe lines are distinct because the slopes of the \\(L_t\\) are all different from \\(0\\).  \nExactly \\(n-k\\) of them are horizontal (non\u2011sunny) and the remaining \\(k\\) are the sunny lines \\(L_{\\,n-k+1},\\dots ,L_n\\); hence \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe verify the covering condition.  \n\n*If \\(1\\le b\\