ll_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma\u202f1.**  \n\\[\n\\ell_s\\cap T_n = \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  By definition a point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_s\\) iff \\(a+b=s\\).  \nThe inequalities \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) together with the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are equivalent to \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\).  Hence the intersection of \\(\\ell_s\\) with \\(T_n\\) consists precisely of the integer points whose coordinates sum to \\(s\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0:=\\{\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Every line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) has slope \\(-1\\); therefore none of them is sunny (a sunny line must avoid the slopes \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)).  By Lemma\u202f1 the union of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) equals \\(T_n\\).  Consequently the two requirements of the problem are satisfied with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThus we have rigorously established that **\\(k=0\\) is always attainable** for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n*Remark on the remaining cases.*  \nTo obtain a family with a prescribed number \\(k\\ge 1\\) of sunny lines one would have to replace some of the diagonal lines \\(\\ell_s\\) by sunny lines while preserving the covering of all points of \\(T_n\\).  A sunny line cannot be parallel to the axes nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently its slope must be different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  A single such line can pass through at most two points of the triangular lattice \\(T_n\\), and in many positions it passes through none of them.  Designing a systematic replacement scheme that both (i) keeps the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) an