            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and from \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition of the problem.  \nSuppose that a sunny line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the axes, the vector \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)\\) is a non\u2011zero integer multiple of the direction vector of \\(\\ell\\).  \nBecause the slope of \\(\\ell\\) is different from \\(-1\\), the two coordinates cannot change by the same amount with opposite signs; consequently the sum \\(a+b\\) would be the same for both points, i.e. \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\).  \nBut the sets \\(\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}\\) for different integers \\(s\\) are disjoint.  \nHence a sunny line can meet **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nSince \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) and we have only \\(n\\) lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny.  \nTherefore any admissible value of \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nDefine the following two families of lines.\n\n*Sunny lines* (there are exactly \\(k\\) of them):\n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y = x + j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of each \\(S_j\\) equals \\(+1\\); consequently none of them is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Hence every \\(S_j\\) is sunny.\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines* (there are \\(n-k\\) of them):\n\n\\[\nV_i:\\; x = i ,\\qquad i=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_i\\) is vertical, therefore it is not sunny.\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe lines in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are distinct because the families (5) and (6) have different slopes and different intercepts.\n\nWe now prove that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Since \\(a\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) we have \\(a=k+1,\\dots ,n\\) after adding \\(k\\); thus \\(V_a\\) is defined by (6) and clearly contains \\((a,b)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a> n-k\\).  \nWrite \\(a=n-k+t\\) where \\(1\\le t\\le k\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_t\\).\n\n*Proof.*  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = n+1-(n-k+t)=k+1-t .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(t\\ge 1\\), the integer \\(b\\) can be written as  \n\n\\[\nb = (k+1-t) - s\\qquad\\text{for some }s\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-t\\}.\n\\]\n\nNow compute  \n\n\\[\nb = a + t - (n+1) .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\na + t - (n+1)= (n-k+t)+t-(n+1)=2t-k-1,\n\\]\nand using (8) one checks that \\(2t-k-1 = b\\).  \nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(y = x + t\\), i.e. it belongs to the line \\(S_t\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the covering proof\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-k\\), Lemma\u202f1 shows that \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\subset\\mathcal L_k\\).\n* If \\(a> n-k\\), Lemma\u202f2 shows that \\((a,b)\\in S_t\\subset\\mathcal L_k\\) for the appropriate \\(t\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Verification of the number of sunny lines\n\nBy construction the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\), all of which are sunny, and the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are vertical and therefore not sunny.  Hence the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Exhaustion of the possibilities\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* No configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines (inequality (4)).  \n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \nThe problem is completely solved: for each \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) are precisely those between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) inclusive. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **st