long to the triangle \\(T_n\\), we have  \n\n\\[\na_2-a_1\\le n-1,\\qquad b_2-b_1\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(q\\ge 2\\) then \\(a_2-a_1\\ge q\\ge 2\\); similarly \\(p\\ge 2\\) would give\n\\(b_2-b_1\\ge 2\\).  In either case the two points would lie on a line whose slope\nis either \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(\\ell\\)\nis sunny.  Hence the only possibility is \\(q=1\\) and \\(p=1\\), i.e. \\(m=1\\).\n\nBut a line of slope \\(+1\\) has equation \\(y=x+c\\); for two points on such a line\nthe differences satisfy \\(b_2-b_1 = a_2-a_1\\).  Substituting into (7) yields\n\\(a_2-a_1 = b_2-b_1\\), which together with the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces\n\\(a_2+b_2 > a_1+b_1\\).  Consequently the two points cannot both belong to the\nfinite set \\(T_n\\) (which contains at most one point for each value of the sum\n\\(a+b\\)).  This contradiction shows that a sunny line cannot contain two distinct\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus **any sunny line meets at most one point of \\(T_n\\).**  \n\u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Counting argument\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition.\nSuppose that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are\nnon\u2011sunny (hence vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nFrom Section\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most one point of \\(T_n\\); therefore\nthe sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points.  The set \\(T_n\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\npoints, so the non\u2011sunny lines must cover at least  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 - k\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nA non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\):\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets the triangle in the points \\((c,1),\\dots ,(c,\n  n+1-c)\\), i.e. exactly \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points;\n* a horizontal line \\(y=d\\) meets at most \\(n\\) points;\n* a line of slope \\(-1\\) meets at most \\(n\\) points as well.\n\nHence the total number of points that can be supplied by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny\nlines is at most \\((n-k)n\\).  Consequently we must have  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - k .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (11) gives  \n\n\\[\nkn \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - kn \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n2kn \\ge n(n+1) - 2k \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\,(2n+2) \\ge n(n+1) .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is a non\u2011negative integer and \\(2n+2 > n\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\), the only\nsolution of this inequality is  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThus **any admissible configuration must contain no sunny line at all.**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\,}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThey are pairwise distinct and none of them is sunny (they are vertical).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently the point lies on\nthe line \\(x=a\\) from the family (13).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered,\nand the family consists of exactly \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe counting argument forces \\(k=0\\); the explicit family of vertical lines shows\nthat this value is attainable.  Therefore the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\)\nfor which the required configuration exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
