et the configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Using Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   2k+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\le\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the right\u2013hand side of (3) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\;<\\;n-1 ,\n   \\]\n\n   and a short elementary check shows that the largest integer not exceeding the\n   fraction is exactly \\(n-2\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Construction.*  \n   \u2013 Choose the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   They are non\u2011sunny and together cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first\n   coordinate is at most \\(n-2\\).  \n   \u2013 The remaining points are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(n-1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(n-1,2)\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. at most three points forming a right\u2011angled triangle of side\u2011length\u202f2.\n   Any set of at most three points can be covered by at most two lines.\n   Take any \\(k\\) of the required sunny lines (with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)) and let them be\n   the first \\(k\\) lines of the following list:\n\n   * if \\(k=0\\) we use no sunny line;\n   * if \\(k=1\\) we let the sunny line be the line through \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\)\n     (horizontal, hence not sunny) \u2013 instead we choose a line through\n     \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\) which has slope \\(\\infty\\) (also not sunny);\n     to obtain a sunny line we simply pick any line through a single uncovered\n     point, e.g. the line through \\((n,1)\\) with slope \\(2\\);\n   * if \\(k=2\\) we take the two sunny lines to be the lines through\n     \\((n-1,1)\\)\u2013\\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\)\u2013\\((n,1)\\); both have slopes \\(0\\) and\n     \\