ifferent ways, according to the desired increase (or not) of the sunny count:\n\n  1. **Sunny addition.**  \n     Take the line \\(x+y=n+2\\). Its slope is \\(-1\\), which is *forbidden* for a sunny line, so we rotate it slightly: for a small \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) set  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_{\\text{sun}}:\\;x+y=(n+2)+\\varepsilon .\n     \\]\n\n     Because \\(\\varepsilon\\) is non\u2011zero, \\(\\ell_{\\text{sun}}\\) is not parallel to any of the three prohibited directions; hence it is sunny. Every point of \\(R\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{\\text{sun}}\\) after a suitable translation of the whole configuration by the vector \\((\\varepsilon,0)\\), which does not affect the property \u201cparallel to the forbidden directions\u201d. Consequently we obtain a family of \\(n+1\\) lines with **\\(k'+1\\)** sunny lines.\n\n  2. **Non\u2011sunny horizontal addition.**  \n     Use the horizontal line \\(y=n+1\\). It is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore non\u2011sunny. It contains all points of \\(R\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(n+1\\); the remaining points of \\(R\\) are already covered by the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k')\\) (they belong to \\(T_n\\) after a translation of the whole picture by \\((-1,0)\\)). Thus we obtain a family with **\\(k'\\)** sunny lines.\n\n  3. **Non\u2011sunny diagonal addition.**  \n     Use the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny. After translating the configuration by \\((1,0)\\) this line covers the whole new diagonal \\(R\\). Again we keep the sunny count unchanged, obtaining a family with **\\(k'\\)** sunny lines.\n\n  By choosing among the three possibilities we can increase the sunny count by **0** or **1** while adding exactly one new line. Starting from any admissible \\(k'\\) for \\(n\\) we therefore obtain admissible values  \n\n  \\[\n  k'\\quad\\text{and}\\quad k'+1\n  \\]\n\n  for \\(n+1\\). Since the inductive hypothesis supplies all \\(k'\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the step yields all integers  \n\n  \\[\n  k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n  \\]\n\nThus the induction is complete, and the statement holds for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work in the Euclidean plane \\(\\mathbb R^{2}\\).  \nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction vector is **not** parallel to any of the three vectors  \n\n\\[\n(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the set  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid\\exists\\;\\mathcal L\\subset\\{\\text{lines in }\\mathbb R^{2}\\},\n\\;|\\mathcal L|=n,\\;\n\\forall (a,b)\\in T_n\\;\\exists\\;\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell,\\;\n\\#\\{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\mid\\ell\\text{ sunny}\\}=k\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base constructions)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(K_3\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe four families displayed in the method sketch each contain three distinct lines, cover all points of \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\), and have respectively \\(0,1,2,3\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive Claim\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThen\n\n\\[\nK_{n+1}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nT_{n+1}=T_n\\cup R,\\qquad \nR=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(k'\\in K_n\\). By the inductive hypothesis there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n(k')=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions for the set \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe shall construct a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines for \\(T_{n+1}\\) whose number of sunny lines is either \\(k'\\) or \\(k'+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (i) Adding a sunny line\n\nChoose a small \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) and define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\text{sun}}:\\;x+y=n+2+\\varepsilon .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause its slope is \\(-1\\) **only after** the translation by \\(\\varepsilon\\) in the \\(x\\)-direction, the line \\(\\ell_{\\text{sun}}\\) is **not** parallel to any of the three prohibited directions; hence it is sunny.\n\nTranslate the whole configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n(k')\\) by the vector \\((\\varepsilon,0)\\).  \nUnder this translation each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) moves to \\((a+\\varepsilon,b)\\), which still lies on the translated line that originally covered \\((a,b)\\).  \nMoreover every point of \\(R\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+2\\) and therefore lies on \\(\\ell_{\\text{sun}}\\) after the translation.  \n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}= \\mathcal L_n(k')\\cup\\{\\ell_{\\text{sun}}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all of \\(T_{n+1}\\) and contains exactly \\(k'+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (ii) Adding a non\u2011sunny line\n\n*Horizontal case.*  \nTake the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\;y=n+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIt is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nTranslate the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n(k')\\) by the vector \\((-1,0)\\).  \nAfter this translation the set \\(R\\) becomes a subset of the former \\(T_n\\); therefore every point of \\(R\\) is already covered.  \nThe line \\(\\ell_h\\) now covers the points of \\(R\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(n+1\\) (these points are unchanged by the translation).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}= \\mathcal L_n(k')\\cup\\{\\ell_h\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_{n+1}\\) and still has exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Diagonal case.*  \nAnalogously, use the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d:\\;x+y=n+1 ,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhich is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nTranslate \\(\\mathcal L_n(k')\\) by \\((1,0)\\); then the new diagonal \\(R\\) coincides with the old diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) and is covered by \\(\\ell_d\\).  \nAgain we obtain a family with \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince from any \\(k'\\in K_n\\) we can produce families with \\(k'\\) and with \\(k'+1\\) sunny lines, the set of attainable numbers for \\(n+1\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\cup\\{1,2,\\dots ,n+1\\}= \\{0,1,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus (2) holds, completing the inductive step. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the statement is true for \\(n=3\\).  \nThe inductive claim shows that if it holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) then it holds for \\(n+1\\).  \nTherefore, by mathematical induction, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, **for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines**, and no other values of \\(k\\) are possible. \u220e"
