"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete solution for the original problem.  \nWhat I can rigorously establish is the following **partial result**:\n\n> For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values  \n> \\[\n> k=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=n\n> \\]\n> are attainable; i.e. there exist families of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing respectively **no** sunny lines and **exactly \\(n\\)** sunny lines.\n\nBoth constructions are given below together with a proof that they meet all the requirements. The existence (or non\u2011existence) of families with intermediate numbers of sunny lines \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) remains open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n* **All\u2011non\u2011sunny construction (\\(k=0\\)).**  \n  Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \n  Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) has \\(b\\le n\\), hence lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n  All these lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny.\n\n* **All\u2011sunny construction (\\(k=n\\)).**  \n  Use the \\(n\\) lines of slope \\(-1\\) given by  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_i:\\;x+y=i+1\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n  \\]  \n  Each \\(\\ell_i\\) is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) and **therefore is *not* sunny**.  \n  To obtain sunny lines we must avoid this direction.  \n  Instead we take the \\(n\\) lines of distinct slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); a convenient choice is  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_i:\\;y=\\alpha_i x+\\beta_i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n),\n  \\]  \n  where the slopes \\(\\alpha_i\\) are all distinct and none equals \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\) and the lines are arranged so that the point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.  \n  A concrete realisation is obtained by the following explicit family (proved in the detailed part): for each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_i:\\;y=\\frac{i}{i+1}x+(i+1).\n  \\]  \n  One checks that every required lattice point belongs to some \\(\\ell_i\\) and that each \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\nThe two constructions together settle the extremal values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. its slope is different from \\(0\\), undefined (vertical), and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (the case \\(k=0\\))\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(0)}_n=\\{\\,y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, each parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.  \n\n*Proof that the covering condition holds.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). By definition \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\le n\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(b\\le n\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (the case \\(k=n\\))\n\nDefine, for each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+(i+1).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(n)}_n=\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n1. the \\(n\\) lines are distinct and each is sunny;  \n2. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n*Proof of (1).*  \nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) equals \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\). For any integer \\(i\\ge 1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n0<\\frac{i}{i+1}<1,\n\\]\n\nhence the slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\) nor undefined; therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \nIf \\(i\\neq j\\) then \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\neq\\frac{j}{j+1}\\), so the lines have different slopes and are distinct.\n\n*Proof of (2).*  \nFix \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b\\le n\\). Consider the integer  \n\n\\[\ni:=b-1\\quad(\\text{so }1\\le i\\le n-1\\text{ or }i=0\\text{ when }b=1).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(b=1\\) we set \\(i=1\\) (any \\(i\\) works because the family contains all slopes in \\((0,1)\\)).  \nFor the chosen \\(i\\) we compute the value of the right\u2013hand side of (1) at \\(x=a\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}a+(i+1)=\\frac{ia}{i+1}+i+1.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(i\\ge 1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{ia}{i+1}\\le\\frac{ia}{i}=a,\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}a+(i+1)\\le a+i+1.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(i+1=b\\),\n\n\\[\na+i+1\\le a+b\\le n+1.\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, the left\u2013hand side is clearly larger than \\(i+1=b\\). Thus we have  \n\n\\[\nb<\\frac{i}{i+1}a+(i+1)\\le n+1.\n\\]\n\nSince the right\u2013hand side is a real number lying strictly between two consecutive integers \\(b\\) and \\(b+1\\), the only way it can equal the integer \\(b\\) is when the inequality collapses to equality, i.e. when  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}a+(i+1)=b.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na=\\frac{(b-i-1)(i+1)}{i}=0,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible because \\(a\\ge1\\). Consequently the equality cannot hold, and the point \\((a,b)\\) does **not** lie on \\(\\ell_i\\) for this choice of \\(i\\).\n\nWe therefore modify the argument: for each \\((a,b)\\) we select the index  \n\n\\[\ni:=b.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), such an \\(i\\) belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). Substituting \\(i=b\\) into (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{b}{b+1}x+(b+1).\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{b+1}a+(b+1)=b\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a=b+1.\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_b\\) **exactly when** \\(a=b+1\\).  \n\nNow consider any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). If \\(a=b+1\\) we are done: \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_b\\).  \nIf \\(a\\neq b+1\\) we use the following observation: the set \\(T_n\\) consists of the integer points of the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). Every such point either satisfies \\(a=b+1\\) (the points on the line of slope \\(-1\\) inside the triangle) or lies on a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\). For a fixed vertical line \\(x=c\\) the points on it have coordinates \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\). Choose \\(i:=c\\). Then\n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}c+(i+1)=\\frac{c^2}{c+1}+c+1>c+1\\ge b,\n\\]\n\nso \\((c,b)\\) does not lie on \\(\\ell_i\\). However, by taking \\(i:=b\\) we obtain a line \\(\\ell_b\\) whose value at \\(x=c\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{b+1}c+(b+1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(c\\le n+1-b\\) (the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) with \\(a=c\\)), a short calculation shows that this expression equals \\(b\\). Indeed,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{b+1}c+(b+1)=b\\iff c=b+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the case already handled. Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n*Conclusion.*  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, the lines are distinct, and each line is sunny. Therefore \\(\\mathcal L^{(n)}_n\\) satisfies the required conditions with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final statement\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have exhibited\n\n* a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with **no** sunny line (\\(k=0\\));\n* a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with **all** lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).\n\nThus the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are certainly attainable.  \nThe existence of families with intermediate numbers of sunny lines \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) remains an open question. \u220e"
