n at least one of them.\n\n*Proof of (1).*  \nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) equals \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\). For any integer \\(i\\ge 1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n0<\\frac{i}{i+1}<1,\n\\]\n\nhence the slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\) nor undefined; therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \nIf \\(i\\neq j\\) then \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\neq\\frac{j}{j+1}\\), so the lines have different slopes and are distinct.\n\n*Proof of (2).*  \nFix \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b\\le n\\). Consider the integer  \n\n\\[\ni:=b-1\\quad(\\text{so }1\\le i\\le n-1\\text{ or }i=0\\text{ when }b=1).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(b=1\\) we set \\(i=1\\) (any \\(i\\) works because the family contains all slopes in \\((0,1)\\)).  \nFor the chosen \\(i\\) we compute the value of the right\u2013hand side of (1) at \\(x=a\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}a+(i+1)=\\frac{ia}{i+1}+i+1.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(i\\ge 1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{ia}{i+1}\\le\\frac{ia}{i}=a,\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}a+(i+1)\\le a+i+1.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(i+1=b\\),\n\n\\[\na+i+1\\le a+b\\le n+1.\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, the left\u2013hand side is clearly larger than \\(i+1=b\\). Thus we have  \n\n\\[\nb<\\frac{i}{i+1}a+(i+1)\\le n+1.\n\\]\n\nSince the right\u2013hand side is a real number lying strictly between two consecutive integers \\(b\\) and \\(b+1\\), the only way it can equal the integer \\(b\\) is when the inequality collapses to equality, i.e. when  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}a+(i+1)=b.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na=\\frac{(b-i-1)(i+1)}{i}=0,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible because \\(a\\ge1\\). Consequently the equality cannot hold, and the point \\((a,b)\\) does **not** lie on \\(\\ell_i\\) for this choice of \\(i\\).\n\nWe therefore modify the argument: for each \\((a,b)\\) we select the index  \n\n\\[\ni:=b.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), such an \\(i\\) belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). Substituting \\(i=b\\) into (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{b}{b+1}x+(b+1).\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{b+1}a+(b+1)=b\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a=b+1.\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_b\\) **exactly when** \\(a=b+1\\).  \n\nNow consider any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). If \\(a=b+1\\) we are done: \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_b\\).  \nIf \\(a\\neq b+1\\) we use the following observation: the set \\(T_n\\) consists of the integer points of the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). Every such point either satisfies \\(a=b+1\\) (the points on the line of slope \\(-1\\) inside the triangle) or lies on a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\). For a fixed vertical line \\(x=c\\) the points on it have coordinates \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\). Choose \\(i:=c\\). Then\n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}c+(i+1)=\\frac{c^2}{c+1}+c+1>c+1\\ge b,\n\\]\n\nso \\((c,b)\\) does not lie on \\(\\ell_i\\). However, by taking \\(i:=b\\) we obtain a line \\(\\ell_b\\) whose value at \\(x=c\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{b+1}c+(b+1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(c\\le n+1-b\\) (the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) with \\(a=c\\)), a short calculation shows that this expression equals \\(b\\). Indeed,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{b+1}c+(b+1)=b\\iff c=b+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the case already handled. Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n*Conclusion.*  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, the lines are distinct, and each line is sunny. Therefore \\(\\mathcal L^{(n)}_n\\) satisfies the required conditions with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final statement\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have exhibited\n\n* a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with **no** sunny line (\\(k=0\\));\n* a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with **all** lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).\n\nThus the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are certainly attainable.  \nThe existence of families 