not destroy the covering property.\n\nThe construction therefore yields, for each admissible \\(k\\), a concrete family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\); geometrically it is the set of lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 1.  Three non\u2011sunny lines cover \\(T_n\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\; y=1,\\qquad   \nL_2:\\; x=1,\\qquad   \nL_3:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n* \\(L_1\\) (horizontal) contains all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \n* \\(L_2\\) (vertical) contains all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \n* \\(L_3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies one of the three alternatives:\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_1\\).  \n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_2\\).  \n* If \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) after possibly moving along the integer lattice: indeed the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) passes through the points \\((2,n-1),\\,(3,n-2),\\dots,(n-1,2)\\), which together with the points on the two axes already listed constitute the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\subseteq L_1\\cup L_2\\cup L_3 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe three lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) are pairwise distinct and each of them is **not** sunny, because their slopes are respectively \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 2.  Adding sunny lines while preserving the covering property.\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall construct \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal{L}=\\{M_1,\\dots ,M_n\\}\\) such that\n\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(M_i\\) are sunny,\n* (1) holds for the set \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Case 1: \\(k\\le n-3\\).\n\nTake the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) from Step\u202f1.  \nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines; for instance, for \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y = \\alpha_j x + \\beta_j ,\n\\]\n\nwhere the slopes \\(\\alpha_j\\) are chosen from the set \\(\\{1,2,3,\\dots\\}\\) (any positive integers different from \\(0,-1\\)) and the intercepts \\(\\beta_j\\) are distinct rational numbers chosen so that no two of the \\(S_j\\) coincide and none of them coincides with any of \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).  \nNow define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}= \\{L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-3-k}\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere the remaining \\(n-3-k\\) lines \\(H_i\\) are any further sunny lines distinct from the already listed ones (for example, lines of slope \\(2\\) with different intercepts).  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and by (1) the three non\u2011sunny lines already cover every point of \\(T_n\\). Adding extra lines cannot remove points from the union, so the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 2: \\(k = n-2\\).\n\nNow we need only two non\u2011sunny lines.  \nReplace one of the three lines from Step\u202f1 by a sunny line that still passes through all points that were covered uniquely by the removed line.\n\nObserve that the points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) lie on both \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_3\\) (since for such points \\(a+1\\le n+1\\) they also satisfy \\(x+y\\le n+1\\)).  \nHence we may discard \\(L_1\\) and keep \\(L_2\\) and \\(L_3\\); the remaining points of \\(T_n\\) are still covered.  \nNow add \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines as in Case\u202f1 (choose any \\(n-2\\) distinct sunny lines).  \nThus we obtain a family with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 3: \\(k = n-1\\).\n\nDiscard two of the three non\u2011sunny lines, say \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) lie on \\(L_3\\); the points on the axes \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,b)\\) are also on \\(L_3\\) because for any such point we have \\(a+1\\le n+1\\) or \\(1+b\\le n+1\\).  \nConsequently \\(L_3\\) alone covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).  \nNow take \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines (any choice, e.g. slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) with distinct intercepts).  \nTogether with \\(L_3\\) we have \\(n\\) lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny, and the covering condition holds.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 4: \\(k = n\\).\n\nTake **no** non\u2011sunny line.  \nChoose \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that together cover \\(T_n\\).  \nOne explicit choice is the following family of lines of slope \\(1\\):\n\n\\[\nM_i:\\; y = x + (i-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nHence \\(b-a\\) is an integer between \\(-(n-1)\\) and \\(0\\).  \nSelect \\(i = 1 - (b-a)\\); then \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(y = x + (i-1)\\), i.e. lies on \\(M_i\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{M_i\\}_{i=1}^n\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 3.  Verification of the construction.\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a concrete set \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that\n\n* exactly \\(k\\) members of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) are sunny,\n* every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).\n\nTherefore the required condition is satisfied for every \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\