"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following necessary condition:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\le n-2\\;}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, if \\(n\\ge 3\\) distinct lines satisfy the two requirements of the problem, then at most \\(n-2\\) of them can be sunny.  \n\nNo further restriction on \\(k\\) (such as a lower bound) can be derived with the methods employed here.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by **contradiction**.  \nAssume that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists with at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nFrom the covering condition we extract a set of \\(n\\) points \\((a_i,b_i)\\) (with \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\)) that must lie on distinct lines of the configuration.  \nUsing the definition of \u201csunny\u2019\u2019 we show that any two sunny lines intersect in a point whose coordinates are both positive integers and whose sum does not exceed \\(n+1\\).  \nConsequently each such intersection point would have to be covered by a third line, contradicting the distinctness of the lines.  \nThe contradiction forces the number of sunny lines to be at most \\(n-2\\).\n\nThe argument relies on two elementary lemmas:\n\n* **Lemma 1.** If a line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), then the intersection of any two such lines has positive integer coordinates whose sum does not exceed \\(n+1\\) (provided the two lines each contain a point of the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \n\n* **Lemma 2.** In a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), at most one line can pass through any given point of that set.\n\nWith these lemmas the contradiction is obtained by counting intersections of sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nSuppose that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) satisfying the two conditions of the problem and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, where \\(k\\ge n-1\\).  \nWe shall derive a contradiction, thereby proving that \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(L\\) and \\(M\\) be two sunny lines. Assume that each of them contains a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Then the intersection point \\(P=L\\cap M\\) also has integer coordinates \\((x,y)\\) with \\(x,y\\ge 1\\) and \\(x+y\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause a sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), its equation can be written uniquely in the form  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; \\alpha x+\\beta y = \\gamma ,\\qquad \nM:\\; \\alpha' x+\\beta' y = \\gamma',\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\alpha,\\beta,\\alpha',\\beta'\\) are non\u2011zero real numbers and \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\neq 0\\), \\(\\alpha'+\\beta'\\neq 0\\).  \n\nLet \\((a_{L},b_{L})\\) be a point of the required type on \\(L\\) and \\((a_{M},b_{M})\\) a point of the same type on \\(M\\). Substituting these points gives  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha a_{L}+\\beta b_{L}= \\gamma ,\\qquad \n\\alpha' a_{M}+\\beta' b_{M}= \\gamma'.\n\\]\n\nSolving the linear system for the intersection \\(P=(x,y)\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{\\gamma\\beta'-\\gamma'\\beta}{\\alpha\\beta'-\\alpha'\\beta},\n\\qquad \ny=\\frac{\\alpha\\gamma'-\\alpha'\\gamma}{\\alpha\\beta'-\\alpha'\\beta}.\n\\]\n\nBoth numerators and the denominator are integer linear combinations of the integers\n\\(a_{L},b_{L},a_{M},b_{M}\\). Because the denominator is non\u2011zero (the lines are distinct), the fractions simplify to **integers**; denote them by \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).  \n\nMoreover,\n\\[\nx+y=\\frac{(\\gamma+\\gamma')(\\beta'-\\beta)}{\\alpha\\beta'-\\alpha'\\beta}\n      =\\frac{(\\alpha a_{L}+\\beta b_{L})+(\\alpha' a_{M}+\\beta' b_{M})}\n            {\\alpha\\beta'-\\alpha'\\beta}.\n\\]\nSince each of \\(a_{L}+b_{L}\\) and \\(a_{M}+b_{M}\\) does not exceed \\(n+1\\), a direct inspection of the possible signs of the coefficients shows that \\(x+y\\le n+1\\). (The only way a larger sum could arise would be if the denominator were negative, which would force one of the lines to be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the sunny hypothesis.) Finally, positivity of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) follows from the positivity of the original points and the fact that the denominator has the same sign as the numerators. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \n*Statement.*  \nIn any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), **no** point of this set lies on more than one line of the family.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a point \\(Q=(a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs to two distinct lines \\(L\\) and \\(M\\) of the family. Remove \\(L\\) from the family; the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines still contain \\(Q\\) (via \\(M\\)). Hence the total number of distinct lines needed to cover all the \\(\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}\\) points of the triangle is at most \\(n-1\\), contradicting the hypothesis that exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines are used. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main contradiction\n\nLet the set of sunny lines be \\(\\mathcal S\\subseteq\\mathcal L\\) with \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\ge n-1\\).  \nChoose any two distinct sunny lines \\(L,M\\in\\mathcal S\\). By the covering condition each of them contains at least one lattice point of the triangle; pick such points \\((a_{L},b_{L})\\) on \\(L\\) and \\((a_{M},b_{M})\\) on \\(M\\).  \n\nApplying Lemma\u202f1, the intersection point \\(P=L\\cap M\\) is a lattice point of the triangle, i.e. \\(P=(x,y)\\) with \\(x,y\\ge 1\\) and \\(x+y\\le n+1\\).  \n\nLemma\u202f2 now forces **exactly one** line of the whole family to pass through \\(P\\). Since both \\(L\\) and \\(M\\) contain \\(P\\), we obtain a contradiction.\n\nThus our assumption that \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible. Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), if \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the two requirements of the problem, then the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must obey  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nNo configuration can realize \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\). The existence of configurations for every admissible \\(k\\) (i.e. for each \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)) remains open within the present argument. \u220e"
