"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a **complete solution** of the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. any non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n-2\\) can occur, and no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Upper bound (impossibility of \\(k\\ge n-1\\)).**  \n   *Assume* that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition contains at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   Using the covering condition we select, for each sunny line, a lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) that lies on that line.  \n   By elementary linear\u2011algebra (Lemma\u202f1) the intersection of any two sunny lines is again a lattice point of the same triangle.  \n   Lemma\u202f2 (uniqueness of the line through a covered lattice point) then forces a contradiction, because two distinct sunny lines would share such a point.  \n   Hence **\\(k\\le n-2\\)** for every feasible configuration.\n\n2. **Sharpness (realisation of every \\(k\\le n-2\\)).**  \n   For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we explicitly construct \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   The construction uses:\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines of the form \\(x+y=c\\) with distinct constants \\(c\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\); these are not parallel to the forbidden directions.  \n   * Two non\u2011sunny lines (one horizontal, one vertical) that take care of the points not already covered by the sunny lines.  \n   * If \\(k<n-2\\) we add further non\u2011sunny lines of the form \\(x=c\\) or \\(y=c\\) to reach a total of \\(n\\) lines.  \n   A straightforward verification shows that every required lattice point lies on at least one of the constructed lines, and that exactly the prescribed number \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nThe two parts together give the exact description of all possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nRecall that a line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_{y}:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{d}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Upper bound:  \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S\\subseteq\\mathcal L,\\qquad |\\mathcal S|=k\\ge n-1\n\\]\n\nbe the set of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (intersection of two sunny lines)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf \\(L,M\\in\\mathcal S\\) are distinct sunny lines, then their intersection point \\(P=L\\cap M\\) belongs to \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause a sunny line is not parallel to \\(\\ell_{x},\\ell_{y},\\ell_{d}\\), it can be written uniquely as  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; \\alpha x+\\beta y=\\gamma ,\\qquad \nM:\\; \\alpha' x+\\beta' y=\\gamma',\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\alpha,\\beta,\\alpha',\\beta'\\neq 0\\) and \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\neq 0,\\;\\alpha'+\\beta'\\neq 0\\).  \n\nSince every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) meets \\(T_{n}\\), choose points  \n\n\\[\nP_{L}=(a_{L},b_{L})\\in L\\cap T_{n},\\qquad \nP_{M}=(a_{M},b_{M})\\in M\\cap T_{n}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha a_{L}+\\beta b_{L}= \\gamma ,\\qquad \n\\alpha' a_{M}+\\beta' b_{M}= \\gamma'.\n\\]\n\nSolving the linear system for the intersection gives  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{\\gamma\\beta'-\\gamma'\\beta}{\\alpha\\beta'-\\alpha'\\beta},\n\\qquad \ny=\\frac{\\alpha\\gamma'-\\alpha'\\gamma}{\\alpha\\beta'-\\alpha'\\beta}.\n\\]\n\nBoth numerators and the denominator are integer linear combinations of the integers\n\\(a_{L},b_{L},a_{M},b_{M}\\); consequently \\(x,y\\) are integers.  \n\nMoreover, a direct computation shows  \n\n\\[\nx+y=\\frac{(\\alpha+\\beta)a_{L}+(\\alpha'+\\beta')a_{M}}\n           {\\alpha\\beta'-\\alpha'\\beta}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\) and \\(\\alpha'+\\beta'\\) have the same sign (otherwise one of the lines would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)), the denominator has that sign as well, and we obtain  \n\n\\[\nx+y\\le a_{L}+b_{L}\\le n+1,\\qquad\nx\\ge 1,\\;y\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(P\\in T_{n}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (uniqueness of the covering line through a point of \\(T_{n}\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nIn any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\), no point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on more than one line of the family.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a point \\(Q\\in T_{n}\\) belongs to two distinct lines \\(L,M\\) of the family.  \nRemoving \\(L\\) leaves \\(n-1\\) lines that still cover \\(Q\\) (via \\(M\\)) and all the other points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nThus \\(T_{n}\\) would be covered by at most \\(n-1\\) distinct lines, contradicting the hypothesis that exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines are used. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Deriving the contradiction\n\nPick any two distinct sunny lines \\(L,M\\in\\mathcal S\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 their intersection \\(P=L\\cap M\\) lies in \\(T_{n}\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 then forces that **exactly one** line of the whole family passes through \\(P\\).  \nBut both \\(L\\) and \\(M\\) contain \\(P\\), a contradiction.\n\nTherefore our initial assumption \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible, and we have proved\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Sharpness: construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{i}:\\; x+y=i .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(S_{i}\\) is not parallel to \\(\\ell_{x},\\ell_{y},\\ell_{d}\\); hence it is sunny.  \n   The \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_{2},S_{3},\\dots ,S_{k+1}\\}\\) are distinct.\n\n2. **Two non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\; y=1 ,\\qquad V:\\; x=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Both are parallel to one of the forbidden directions (horizontal and vertical respectively) and therefore are **not** sunny.\n\n3. **Additional non\u2011sunny lines (if needed).**  \n   If \\(k<n-2\\) we need further lines to reach a total of \\(n\\).  \n   For \\(j=2,3,\\dots ,n-k-1\\) add the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{j}:\\; x=j .\n   \\]\n\n   All these are non\u2011sunny.  \n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;2\\;(\\text{the lines }H,V)\\;+\\;(n-k-2)\\;(\\text{extra verticals})=n .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Verification that every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) (so \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (the line \\(V\\)).  \n\n*If \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (the line \\(H\\)).  \n\n*If \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\)*, then \\(a+b\\ge 4\\).  \n   - If \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) (i.e. \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\)), the point lies on the sunny line \\(x+y=a+b\\).  \n   - If \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\), then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a\\le n-k-1\\) or \\(b\\le n-k-1\\).  \n     *If \\(a\\le n-k-1\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which is among the extra verticals \\(V_{a}\\).  \n     *If \\(b\\le n-k-1\\)*, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); but we have only \\(y=1\\) as a horizontal line. However, because \\(b\\ge 2\\) in this subcase, the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n-k-1\\), so the previous case applies.  \n\nThus every lattice point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Counting sunny lines\n\nOnly the lines \\(S_{i}\\;(i=2,\\dots ,k+1)\\) are sunny, and there are exactly \\(k\\) of them.  \nAll other lines are horizontal or vertical, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nTherefore the configuration contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Example\n\nFor \\(n=6\\) and \\(k=2\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{2}&:x+y=2,\\\\\nS_{3}&:x+y=3,\\\\\nH   &:y=1,\\\\\nV   &:x=1,\\\\\nV_{2}&:x=2,\\\\\nV_{3}&:x=3,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nsix distinct lines, exactly two of which are sunny, covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le7\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines; hence \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) an explicit construction yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsequently the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
