well; thus \\(M\\le n\\).\n\n  The total number of incidences (point\u2013line pairs) is at most\n  \\[\n  k\\cdot n+(n-k)\\cdot n = n^{2}.\n  \\]\n  On the other hand every point of \\(S\\) must be incident to at least one line,\n  so we need at least \\(|S|\\) incidences:\n  \\[\n  n^{2}\\ge |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  n\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n  \\]\n  This inequality is always true, but it becomes **strict** when the\n  non\u2011sunny lines are forced to use their maximal capacity \\(n\\).  In the worst\n  case each non\u2011sunny line indeed covers exactly \\(n\\) points, leaving\n  \\[\n  |S|- (n-k)n = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} - (n-k)n = \\frac{n}{2} - kn\n  \\]\n  points still uncovered.  These remaining points must be covered by the\n  \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of which can contribute at most \\(n\\) points, so we\n  require\n  \\[\n  kn \\ge \\frac{n}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\ge\\frac12 .\n  \\]\n  Since \\(k\\) is an integer, we obtain the **pigeon\u2011hole lower bound**\n  \\[\n  k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n  \\]\n\n* **Conclusion of the argument.**  \n  The pigeon\u2011hole principle forces any admissible configuration to contain at\n  least \\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  No upper bound on \\(k\\) follows from\n  this counting argument alone.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S\\) is obtained by summing, for each possible value\n\\(c=a+b\\) (with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)), the number of integer solutions of\n\\(a+b=c\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\).  For a fixed \\(c\\) there are \\(c-1\\) such solutions,\nhence  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1  \nA line that is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is \\(y=b\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), then the admissible\n\\(a\\)-coordinates satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus the line meets at most\n\\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points of \\(S\\).  The case of a line \\(x=a\\) (parallel to the\n\\(y\\)-axis) is symmetric.  If the line is \\(x+y=c\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)),\nthe condition \\(a+b=c\\) together with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) yields exactly \\(c-1\\)\npoints, and \\(c\\le n+1\\) gives \\(c-1\\le n\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line (i.e. a line not parallel to any of the three forbidden\ndirections) also contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause a sunny line is not parallel to any of the three directions, its\nintersection with the lattice points of \\(S\\) cannot be confined to a single\nhorizontal, vertical or anti\u2011diagonal line.  Nevertheless, the set \\(S\\) lies\ninside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),\\,(n,1),\\,(1,n)\\); any\nstraight line can intersect this triangle in at most one of its sides, and\neach side contains at most \\(n\\) lattice points.  Hence the line can meet at\nmost \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n### Proposition (Pigeon\u2011hole lower bound)  \nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(S\\).\nIf exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote by \\(s=n-k\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  By Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011sunny\nline covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\); by Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line also\ncovers at most \\(n\\) points.  Consequently the total number of incidences\n(point\u2013line pairs) is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot n + s\\cdot n = n^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(S\\) must be incident to at least one line, so the number of\nincidences is at least \\(|S|\\).  Using (1),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}\\ge |S| = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n2n \\ge n+1 .\n\\]\n\nEquality in (2) can hold only if every line actually attains its maximal\ncapacity \\(n\\).  In that extremal situation the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines already\ncover \\(sn\\) points, leaving  \n\n\\[\n|S|-sn = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} - sn = \\frac{n}{2} - kn\n\\]\n\npoints still uncovered.  These must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines, each\ncontributing at most \\(n\\) points, whence  \n\n\\[\nkn \\ge \\frac{n}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\ge\\frac12 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer and the bound must hold for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), a\nsharper estimate is obtained by observing that the non\u2011sunny lines can be of\nat most three different directions.  By the pigeon\u2011hole principle at least\n\\(\\lceil s/3\\rceil\\) of them share the same direction; each such direction can\ncover at most \\(n\\) points, so we need  \n\n\\[\n\\lceil s/3\\rceil n \\ge |S| = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(n\\) and solving for \\(s=n-k\\) yields  \n\n\\[\ns \\ge \\frac{n+1}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk \\le n-\\frac{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n### Corollary  \nNo configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition can\ncontain fewer than \\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\nThe pigeon\u2011hole argument therefore establishes a **necessary** condition on\n\\(k\\).  Determining whether this condition is also sufficient, or whether\nadditional restrictions on \\(k\\) exist, remains open.  The present work\nconcludes with the rigorous lower bound above."
