ght line can intersect this triangle in at\nmost one of its three sides, and each side contains exactly \\(n\\) lattice\npoints (the hypotenuse contains fewer).  Consequently a line can meet at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(S\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition (Pigeon\u2011hole lower bound)  \nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers every point\nof \\(S\\).  If exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\nLet \\(s=n-k\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  By Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011sunny line\ncovers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\); by Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line also covers at\nmost \\(n\\) points.  Hence the total number of incidences (point\u2013line pairs) is\nbounded above by  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{k\\cdot n}_{\\text{sunny}}+\\underbrace{s\\cdot n}_{\\text{non\u2011sunny}}\n= n^{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(S\\) must be incident to at least one line, so the number of\nincidences is at least \\(|S|\\).  The cardinality of \\(S\\) is obtained by\nsumming, for each possible sum \\(c=a+b\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)), the number of\nsolutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\):\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n2n\\ge n+1,\n\\]\nwhich is always true.  To extract a non\u2011trivial restriction we use the fact\nthat the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines can have only three distinct directions\n(horizontal, vertical, or anti\u2011diagonal).  By the pigeon\u2011hole principle at\nleast  \n\n\\[\nt:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{s}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nof them share the same direction.  All lines of a common direction are\nparallel, so they intersect the set \\(S\\) in disjoint subsets; each such line\ncovers at most \\(n\\) points (Lemma\u202f1).  Therefore the \\(t\\) parallel\nnon\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(tn\\) points of \\(S\\).  Since all\npoints of \\(S\\) must be covered, we need  \n\n\\[\ntn\\;\\ge\\;|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(n\\) and substituting \\(t=\\lceil s/3\\rceil\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-k}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe smallest integer \\(k\\) satisfying this inequality is obtained by\nreversing the inequality:\n\n\\[\nn-k\\;\\le\\;3\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\;\\ge\\;n-3\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least\n\\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Corollary  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\ncovers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the number\n\\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe argument above uses only counting and the pigeon\u2011hole principle; it\nestablishes a necessary lower bound on the number of sunny lines.  Determining\nwhether this bound is also sufficient, or whether additional constraints on\n\\(k\\) exist, remains an open problem.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
