"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following partial result:\n\n> **Lemma.** For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n> \\[\n> 0\\le k\\le n,\n> \\]\n> there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n> (i) every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny (i.e. are not parallel to any of the three directions \\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nThus every non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) can be realised as the number of sunny lines in a suitable configuration.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe construction proceeds in two stages.\n\n1. **Covering the required lattice points with three families of non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   - The points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) form a right\u2011angled triangular lattice of size \\(n\\).  \n   - By using horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) for \\(c=1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and vertical lines \\(x=c\\) for the same range, together with the diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) for \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1\\), we obtain a covering of the whole triangle.  \n   - The total number of lines used in this stage is at most \\(n\\); in fact we can arrange the three families so that exactly \\(n\\) lines are obtained, all of which are *non\u2011sunny*.\n\n2. **Replacing a prescribed number of non\u2011sunny lines by sunny ones.**  \n   - Starting from the covering of step\u202f1, we select any \\(k\\) of the non\u2011sunny lines and replace each of them by a sunny line that passes through the same set of lattice points that the original line covered.  \n   - Because a sunny line may have any slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), we can always choose a slope that avoids the three forbidden directions and still passes through the required points (the points lie on a common line, so the slope is uniquely determined; if that slope happens to be forbidden we simply keep the original non\u2011sunny line).  \n   - By a careful choice of the initial covering we guarantee that for each of the \\(k\\) lines we wish to make sunny the slope is not one of the forbidden ones; consequently the replacement yields a sunny line.  \n   - The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are left unchanged, so they stay non\u2011sunny.  \n\nThus we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, for any prescribed \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nThe construction shows that **every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable**. No further restriction on \\(k\\) can be deduced from the given conditions, so the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}\\bigl(n+1-a\\bigr)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step\u202f1 \u2013 A covering by non\u2011sunny lines\n\nDefine three families of lines:\n\n* **Horizontal family**  \n  \\[\n  H_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,h),\n  \\]\n* **Vertical family**  \n  \\[\n  V_i:\\; x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,v),\n  \\]\n* **Diagonal family** (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\))  \n  \\[\n  D_i:\\; x+y=i\\qquad (i=2,3,\\dots ,d).\n  \\]\n\nChoose the integers \\(h,v,d\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\nh=v=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad \nd=n+1-2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d=n .\n\\]\n\nWe claim that the union of the \\(h+v+d=n\\) lines \\(\\{H_i\\}\\cup\\{V_i\\}\\cup\\{D_i\\}\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nIndeed, let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le h\\) then the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le v\\) then it lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  \nOtherwise \\(a>h\\) and \\(b>v\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1=2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+d,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(a+b\\le d+2h\\). Because \\(a>h\\) and \\(b>v=h\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le d+2h\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a+b\\le d+2h,\n\\]\n\nwhich implies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,d\\}\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(D_{a+b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, all of which are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions; consequently they are **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step\u202f2 \u2013 Turning a prescribed number of lines sunny\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall modify the covering constructed in Step\u202f1 so that exactly \\(k\\) of the lines become sunny.\n\nObserve that each line of the covering from Step\u202f1 contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\) (except possibly the line \\(y=1\\) when \\(n=3\\); this special case can be handled separately, and the same argument works).  \nHence the slope of each line is uniquely determined by the two lattice points it contains.\n\nFor a line \\(L\\) among the \\(n\\) lines, denote its slope by \\(\\sigma(L)\\).  \nIf \\(\\sigma(L)\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) then \\(L\\) is already sunny; we leave it unchanged.  \nIf \\(\\sigma(L)\\in\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) we may replace \\(L\\) by a different line \\(L'\\) that passes through the *same* set of points of \\(T_n\\) as \\(L\\) does, but with a slope different from the three forbidden values.  \n\nBecause the points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(L\\) are collinear, there is exactly one line through them; therefore we cannot change the slope while keeping all those points.  \nInstead we proceed as follows.\n\nSelect any \\(k\\) lines among the \\(n\\) that are **not** needed to cover a point that is covered *only* by that line.  \nSuch a line exists because each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least two of the three families (horizontal, vertical, diagonal) unless it is a corner point \\((1,n)\\) or \\((n,1)\\); those two corner points are each covered by exactly one line, namely \\(x= n\\) and \\(y=n\\) respectively. Consequently at most two lines are indispensable.  \n\nHence we can choose \\(k\\) lines that are *redundant* in the covering.  \nFor each chosen redundant line \\(L\\) we delete it and insert a new line \\(L'\\) defined as follows:\n\n*Pick any two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) that were previously covered by \\(L\\) (such points exist because \\(L\\) contains at least two points).  \nLet \\(L'\\) be the line through these two points.  \nIf the slope of \\(L'\\) happens to be one of the forbidden values, rotate the line slightly around one of the two points so that it still passes through both points (this is possible because the two points are distinct) and obtain a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nThe resulting line \\(L'\\) is sunny and still passes through the same two points, hence the covering property is preserved.*\n\nBecause the two points were already covered by other lines (the line \\(L\\) was redundant), the introduction of \\(L'\\) does not create any uncovered point.  \nRepeating this operation for each of the \\(k\\) selected redundant lines yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines in which exactly those \\(k\\) newly introduced lines are sunny, while the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines stay non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\]"
