l respectively to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 Essential and non\u2011essential lines\n\nAmong the lines just constructed, the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,n)\n\\]\n\nare each contained in a unique line:\n\n* \\(P_1\\) lies only on the vertical line \\(V_n\\) (which is present because \\(v=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\ge 1\\) and we may add the extra vertical line \\(x=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd; in any case we can explicitly include \\(x=n\\) as one of the vertical lines).  \n* \\(P_2\\) lies only on the horizontal line \\(H_n\\) (similarly we include \\(y=n\\)).\n\nAll other lines contain at least one lattice point that is also covered by a different line.  \nHence we call \\(V_n\\) and \\(H_n\\) the **essential** lines; the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are **non\u2011essential**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 Replacing a non\u2011essential line by a sunny one\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a non\u2011essential line of the covering.  \nBecause \\(L\\) is non\u2011essential, there exists a point \\((a,b)\\in L\\cap T_n\\) that is also contained in another line of the covering.  \nChoose two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in L\\cap T_n\\); such a pair exists because every line of the families \\(H_i,V_i,D_j\\) meets the triangle in at least two lattice points (except the two essential lines, which we have excluded).\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the (unique) line through \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\).  \nIf the slope of \\(\\ell\\) is not one of \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) we set \\(L'=\\ell\\); then \\(L'\\) is sunny and still passes through the two chosen points.\n\nIf the slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals one of the forbidden values, we modify \\(\\ell\\) as follows.  \nFix \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and rotate the line around this point by a tiny angle \\(\\varepsilon\\) (with \\(\\varepsilon\\neq0\\) and sufficiently small) so that the new line still passes through \\((a_2,b_2)\\) (this is possible because the two points are distinct). The resulting line \\(L'\\) has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and still contains both lattice points. Hence \\(L'\\) is sunny.\n\nSince the two points were already covered by other lines, replacing \\(L\\) by \\(L'\\) does not uncover any point of \\(T_n\\). Moreover \\(L'\\) is distinct from all remaining lines because its slope is different from the slopes of the horizontal, vertical and diagonal families.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4 Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \nChoose arbitrarily \\(k\\) distinct non\u2011essential lines and replace each of them by a sunny line as described in \u00a72.3.  \nThe two essential lines are left unchanged (non\u2011sunny).  \nThus we obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case 2: \\(k=n-1\\).*  \nReplace all \\(n-2\\) non\u2011essential lines by sunny lines (as above).  \nNow we have two essential lines left, both non\u2011sunny, and \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nReplace **one** of the essential lines, say \\(V_n\\), by a sunny line \\(L'\\) that passes through the point \\((n,1)\\) and through any other lattice point of the triangle (for instance \\((1,1)\\)).  \nBecause \\((n,1)\\) is now covered by \\(L'\\), the covering property is preserved.  \nWe end up with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case 3: \\(k=n\\).*  \nProceed as in Case\u202f2, but replace **both** essential lines by sunny lines, each time using the unique essential point together with another lattice point (e.g. replace \\(V_n\\) by the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,1)\\), and replace \\(H_n\\) by the line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((1,1)\\)).  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are now sunny and the covering still holds because every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the newly inserted sunny lines (the point \\((1,1)\\) lies on both of them, guaranteeing coverage of the essential points).\n\nIn every case we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5 Exhaustiveness\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); therefore all these values are admissible.  \nConversely, the problem imposes no restriction that would forbid any of these numbers: the definition of \u201csunny\u2019\u2019 only concerns the direction of a line, and we have exhibited explicit configurations for each possible count. Hence no other values of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
