"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following two statements  \n\n*   **Lemma\u202f1.**  For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is admissible; i.e. there exist \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\n*   **Lemma\u202f2.**  For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is **not** admissible; i.e. it is impossible to cover the required set of points with \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nThese lemmas give non\u2011trivial partial information about the set of possible \\(k\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by an elementary algebraic analysis of the three families of non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n*   vertical lines \\(x=c\\);\n*   horizontal lines \\(y=c\\);\n*   diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nAll other lines are sunny.  \n\n*Lemma\u202f1* is proved by an explicit construction using only vertical (or only horizontal) lines; the algebraic verification is that each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) satisfies the equation of the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n\n*Lemma\u202f2* is proved by contradiction. Assuming that \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all required points, we write the equation of each line in the form \\(y=m_i x+q_i\\) with \\(m_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\). By evaluating the covering condition at the points \\((1,1), (2,1),\\dots,(n,1)\\) we obtain a system of linear equations in the unknowns \\(q_i\\). The Vandermonde\u2011type matrix that appears is nonsingular, forcing each point to lie on a distinct line. This uses the fact that the slopes are all different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\). Consequently at least \\(n\\) distinct lines are needed just to cover the \\(n\\) points \\((i,1)\\), contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(n\\) lines are available (the same \\(n\\) lines would have to cover also the point \\((1,n)\\), which is impossible because no sunny line can pass through both \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) for any \\(i\\)). Hence a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\nThe lemmas together show that the admissible set of \\(k\\) is non\u2011empty (it contains \\(0\\)) and does not contain the maximal value \\(n\\). Determining the exact set of admissible \\(k\\) remains open.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines that covers \\(T_n\\). Consequently \\(k=0\\) is admissible.\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is non\u2011sunny (its slope is \\(\\infty\\)).  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). By definition \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\le n\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)). Hence the point satisfies the equation \\(x=a\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(L_a\\). Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\). The lines are distinct because their \\(x\\)-intercepts are distinct. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) it is impossible to cover \\(T_n\\) with \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines. Hence \\(k=n\\) is not admissible.\n\n**Proof.**  \nAssume, for contradiction, that there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=m_i x+q_i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nwith slopes \\(m_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (the case of a vertical line is excluded because a vertical line is non\u2011sunny).  \nSince the lines are distinct, the ordered pairs \\((m_i,q_i)\\) are distinct.\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nAll these points belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(i+1\\le n+1\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\) there exists at least one line \\(\\ell_{j(i)}\\) that contains \\(P_i\\); thus\n\n\\[\n1=m_{j(i)}\\,i+q_{j(i)}\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause the \\(n\\) points are distinct, the indices \\(j(i)\\) must be distinct as well: if two different points \\(P_{i}\\) and \\(P_{i'}\\) lay on the same line \\(\\ell_{j}\\), then \\(\\ell_{j}\\) would contain the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i',1)\\) with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, forcing its slope to be \\(0\\), contradicting the sunny hypothesis. Hence the map \\(i\\mapsto j(i)\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nRe\u2011indexing the lines if necessary we may assume \\(j(i)=i\\); then (1) becomes\n\n\\[\n1=m_i\\,i+q_i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEquation (2) is a linear system for the unknowns \\(q_i\\). Its coefficient matrix is the \\(n\\times n\\) Vandermonde\u2011type matrix  \n\n\\[\nV=\\begin{pmatrix}\n1 & 1 & \\dots & 1\\\\\n1 & 2 & \\dots & 2\\\\\n\\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots\\\\\n1 & n & \\dots & n\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\nwhich has determinant  \n\n\\[\n\\det V = \\prod_{1\\le i<j\\le n}(j-i)\\neq 0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the system (2) has a unique solution, namely  \n\n\\[\nq_i = 1-m_i i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nThus each line \\(\\ell_i\\) is uniquely determined by its slope \\(m_i\\).\n\nNow consider the point  \n\n\\[\nQ=(1,n)\\in T_n .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(Q\\) lies on some line \\(\\ell_{i_0}\\), we must have  \n\n\\[\nn = m_{i_0}\\cdot 1 + q_{i_0}= m_{i_0}+ (1-m_{i_0} i_0)=1+m_{i_0}(1-i_0). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (3) gives  \n\n\\[\nm_{i_0}= \\frac{n-1}{1-i_0}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(i_0\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\), the right\u2013hand side of (4) is either \\(0\\) (when \\(i_0=1\\)) or \\(-1\\) (when \\(i_0=n\\)). Both possibilities are forbidden for a sunny line. Consequently no sunny line among \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) can pass through \\(Q\\), contradicting the covering requirement.\n\nTherefore a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines does not exist. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion of the partial solution**  \n\n*   The value \\(k=0\\) is always attainable (Lemma\u202f1).  \n*   The value \\(k=n\\) is never attainable (Lemma\u202f2).\n\nThe determination of the remaining admissible values of \\(k\\) (in particular whether any \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) can occur) is left open."
