ble \\(k\\) (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n   *Idea.*  Use exactly \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, one from each of the families \\(\\mathcal F_{x},\\mathcal F_{y},\\mathcal F_{d}\\) as far as possible, and fill the remaining points with sunny lines of distinct slopes.  \n   *Step\u2011by\u2011step.*  \n   - Choose integers \\(c_{1},\\dots ,c_{n-k}\\) such that the vertical lines \\(x=c_{i}\\) (if any) together with the horizontal lines \\(y=c_{i}\\) (if any) and the diagonal lines \\(x+y=c_{i}\\) (if any) cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) except a set \\(S\\) of size at most \\(k\\).  \n   - For each point of \\(S\\) draw a distinct sunny line passing through it; because the points of \\(S\\) are pairwise non\u2011collinear with respect to the three forbidden directions, the resulting sunny lines are all distinct and none of them is parallel to the axes or to \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   - The total number of lines is \\((n-k)+(k)=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny by construction.\n\n   The explicit choice of the constants \\(c_{i}\\) is as follows.  \n   Let \\(m=n-k\\). Write \\(m=3q+r\\) with \\(0\\le r<3\\).  \n   - Take the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,q\\).  \n   - Take the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,q\\).  \n   - Take the diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,2+q-1\\).  \n   - If \\(r\\ge1\\) add the vertical line \\(x=q+1\\); if \\(r=2\\) also add the horizontal line \\(y=q+1\\).  \n\n   One checks directly that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on one of these \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines, except possibly the points \\((q+1,q+1),\\,(q+2,q),\\,(q,q+2)\\) (at most three points).  Since \\(k\\ge\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\) we have \\(k\\ge r\\), so we can use the remaining \\(k\\) sunny lines to cover the uncovered points.  The construction yields distinct lines because the slopes of the sunny lines are chosen, for instance, as the successive rational numbers \\(\\frac{p}{p+1}\\) for \\(p=1,2,\\dots ,k\\).\n\nThus Lemma\u202f2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\(\\bigl[\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil ,\\,n\\bigr]\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nConsequently a non\u2011sunny line belongs to exactly one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{x}=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal F_{y}=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal F_{d}=\\{x+y=c\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (lower bound)\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, at least  \n\n\\[\nk_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nof the lines are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(t_{x},t_{y},t_{d}\\) be the numbers of vertical, horizontal and diagonal (i.e. \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)) lines among the \\(n\\) lines; thus  \n\n\\[\nt_{x}+t_{y}+t_{d}+k=n,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(k\\) is the number of sunny lines.  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) can contain at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) for each value of \\(b\\); because \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the possible values of \\(b\\) are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\). Hence a vertical line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\). The same bound holds for a horizontal line and for a diagonal line (the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) only for \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) points).  \n\nTherefore the total number of points that can be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n(t_{x}+t_{y}+t_{d})\\;n\\le (n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nSince all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) must be covered, (1) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le (n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(n\\;(>0)\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n+1}{2}\\le n-k\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer,  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (realisation)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\;k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid \n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\;\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nPut \\(m=n-k\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines).  Write  \n\n\\[\nm=3q+r,\\qquad 0\\le r<3 .\n\\]\n\nWe shall construct \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines as follows.\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,q\\) take the line \\(x=i\\).  \nIf \\(r\\ge1\\) also take the line \\(x=q+1\\).\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,q\\) take the line \\(y=i\\).  \nIf \\(r=2\\) also take the line \\(y=q+1\\).\n\n*Diagonal lines.*  \nFor \\(i=2,\\dots ,q+1\\) take the line \\(x+y=i\\).\n\nThus we have exactly \\(q\\) vertical, \\(q\\) horizontal and \\(q\\) diagonal lines, plus possibly one extra vertical and/or one extra horizontal line, giving a total of  \n\n\\[\n3q+r=m\n\\]\n\nnon\u2011sunny lines, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Claim 1  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the above \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines, except possibly the points  \n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(q+1,q+1),\\qquad \nP_{2}=(q+2,q),\\qquad \nP_{3}=(q,q+2).\n\\]\n\n*Proof of Claim.*  \nConsider a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le q\\)*, then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to our list) contains the point.  \n*If \\(b\\le q\\)*, then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains the point.  \n*If \\(a,b>q\\)*, then \\(a+b\\ge 2q+2\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1=3q+r+1\\), we have \\(a+b\\le 3q+r+1\\).  \nIf \\(a+b\\le q+1\\) the point would already be covered by a diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\) (which is among the diagonals we chose).  \nHence the only possibilities left are the three points listed above, which correspond to the three ways in which the inequality \\(a+b\\le 3q+r+1\\) can be tight while both coordinates exceed \\(q\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause \\(k\\ge\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{3q+r+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\ge r,\n\\]\n\nso the number of uncovered points (at most three) does not exceed the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines that we are allowed to use.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction of the sunny lines\n\nFor each uncovered point we draw a distinct sunny line passing through it.  \nChoose the slopes of these sunny lines to be the rational numbers  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2},\\;\\frac{2}{3},\\;\\frac{3}{4},\\dots ,\\frac{k}{k+1},\n\\]\n\nand let the line of slope \\(\\frac{t}{t+1}\\) pass through the \\(t\\)-th uncovered point (if fewer than \\(k\\) points remain uncovered, the remaining sunny lines can be taken arbitrarily, e.g. any line of the same slope passing through a point already covered; this does not affect the covering property).  \n\nAll these lines are sunny because their slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nThey are distinct because they have distinct slopes, and none of them coincides with any of the previously constructed non\u2011sunny lines (the latter have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\nConsequently we have produced exactly  \n\n\\[\nm\\;(\\text{non\u2011sunny})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, covering every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\), and precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that any admissible configuration must have at least \\(\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f2 exhibits, for every integer \\(k\\) in the interval  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n,\n\\]\n\na concrete configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\n\\left\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\;\\bigg|\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\right\\}} .\n\\]"
