und holds for a horizontal line and for a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (the admissible values of \\(c\\) are \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\)). Consequently the total number of points that can be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n(t_{x}+t_{y}+t_{d})\\;n\\le (n-k)n . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) must be covered, so by (1) and (3)\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le (n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(n>0\\) gives \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\le n-k\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is integral,  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\qquad\\Box\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (sufficiency)\n\n**Statement.**  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid \n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\,\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Proof.**  \nPut \\(m=n-k\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines).  Write  \n\n\\[\nm=3q+r,\\qquad 0\\le r<3 .\n\\]\n\nWe construct \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines as follows.\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,q\\) take \\(x=i\\).  \nIf \\(r\\ge1\\) also take \\(x=q+1\\).\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,q\\) take \\(y=i\\).  \nIf \\(r=2\\) also take \\(y=q+1\\).\n\n*Diagonal lines.*  \nFor \\(i=2,\\dots ,q+1\\) take \\(x+y=i\\).\n\nThe numbers of lines are \\(q\\) vertical, \\(q\\) horizontal and \\(q\\) diagonal, plus possibly one extra vertical and/or one extra horizontal line; altogether  \n\n\\[\nq+q+q+r = 3q+r = m\n\\]\n\nnon\u2011sunny lines, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Claim 1  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the above \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines, except possibly the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(q+1,q+1),\\qquad \nP_{2}=(q+2,q),\\qquad \nP_{3}=(q,q+2