ough the same set of points.  Because a sunny line can be chosen to contain any prescribed finite set of points (as long as the set is not collinear with a forbidden direction), we can replace exactly \\(k\\) of the horizontal lines by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines while preserving the covering property.  When \\(k=n\\) we simply take \\(n\\) arbitrary sunny lines, each passing through a distinct point of the required set; the covering condition is then satisfied trivially because each point lies on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\nThe detailed proof below spells out these constructions and verifies that they meet all the requirements.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nRecall that a line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\; y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_y:\\; x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{-1}:\\; x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus a non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal, vertical, or has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\n*Construction.*  \nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  The lines are distinct, so we have exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Verification that the covering condition holds.*  \nFix a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  By definition \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n\\).  Consequently the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which is one of the lines in (3)) passes through \\((a,b)\\).  Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\nThus the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) satisfies both requirements with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (realising any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nWe show that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  This is Lemma\u202f1.\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\).*  \nChoose any \\(n\\) distinct points of \\(S_n\\); for definiteness take  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) let \\(M_i\\) be a line passing through \\(P_i\\) whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\) (for instance, the line of slope \\(1\\) through \\(P_i\\)).  Because the slopes are all equal to \\(1\\) the lines are parallel, but they are **not** parallel to any of the forbidden directions, so each \\(M_i\\) is sunny.  The \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{M_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) are distinct (they have different intercepts) and each contains the point \\(P_i\\); consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them (indeed, each point of \\(S_n\\) is one of the \\(P_i\\)).  Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n*Intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).*  \nStart from the configuration of Lemma\u202f1 (the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\)).  Select any subset \\(H\\subset\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|H|=n-k\\).  Replace each horizontal line \\(y=h\\) for \\(h\\in H\\) by a **sunny** line that still passes through all the points that were originally covered by \\(y=h\\).  This can be done as follows.\n\nFix \\(h\\in H\\).  The set of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(y=h\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_h=\\{(a,h)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-h\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|\\mathcal{P}_h|\\ge 2\\) for every \\(h\\le n-1\\), we can choose a line \\(M_h\\) of slope \\(1\\) (or any slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) that contains **all** points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_h\\).  Indeed, the points of \\(