tion, that there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=m_i x+q_i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nwith slopes \\(m_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (a vertical line would have slope \\(\\infty\\) and therefore be non\u2011sunny, so all lines are of the above form).  \nSince the lines are distinct, the ordered pairs \\((m_i,q_i)\\) are distinct.\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nAll these points belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(i+1\\le n+1\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\) there exists at least one line \\(\\ell_{j(i)}\\) that contains \\(P_i\\); thus\n\n\\[\n1=m_{j(i)}\\,i+q_{j(i)}\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf two different points \\(P_i\\) and \\(P_{i'}\\) (\\(i\\neq i'\\)) lay on the same line \\(\\ell_{j}\\), then that line would contain two points with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, forcing its slope to be \\(0\\), contradicting the sunny hypothesis. Hence the indices \\(j(i)\\) are all distinct; after a possible re\u2011ordering we may assume \\(j(i)=i\\). Consequently (1) becomes\n\n\\[\n1=m_i\\,i+q_i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEquation (2) determines each intercept uniquely:\n\n\\[\nq_i = 1-m_i i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nNow examine the point  \n\n\\[\nQ=(1,n)\\in T_n .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(Q\\) lies on some line \\(\\ell_{i_0}\\), substituting \\((x,y)=(1,n)\\) into the equation of \\(\\ell_{i_0}\\) and using (3) gives\n\n\\[\nn = m_{i_0}\\cdot 1 + q_{i_0}\n   = m_{i_0} + \\bigl(1-m_{i_0} i_0\\bigr)\n   = 1 + m_{i_0}(1-i_0). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSolving (4) for \\(m_{i_0}\\) yields\n\n\\[\nm_{i_0}= \\frac{n-1}{1-i_0}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i_0\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le i_0\\le n\\), the denominator \\(1-i_0\\) is either \\(0\\) (when \\(i_0=1\\)) or a negative integer.  \n\n* If \\(i_0=1\\), (5) would give \\(m_{1}\\) undefined; however (4) with \\(i_0=1\\) reduces to \\(n=1\\), which contradicts \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n\n* If \\(i_0=n\\), then \\(1-i_0 = 1-n\\) and (5) gives \