at the line is sunny. Hence **no sunny line can contain both \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\)**.\n\nNow suppose that **only one** of the two points lies on a non\u2011sunny line, say \\(P\\) lies on a non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\) and \\(Q\\) lies on a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is non\u2011sunny, its slope is either \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\n* If \\(\\ell\\) is vertical (\\(x=1\\)) or horizontal (\\(y=n\\)), then \\(\\ell\\) already contains all points of the form \\((1,b)\\) or \\((a,n)\\) respectively. In particular it contains the point \\((1,1)\\in T_n\\).  \n* If \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(-1\\), its equation is \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c=1+n=n+1\\). This line again contains \\((1,1)\\).\n\nThus in every case the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to a non\u2011sunny line.  \nBut \\((1,1)\\) also belongs to the sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) (since \\(\\ell'\\) passes through \\(Q=(n,1)\\) and has slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), the only way for \\(\\ell'\\) to pass through both \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,1)\\) would be to have slope \\(0\\), which is forbidden). Hence \\((1,1)\\) would lie on two distinct lines, one sunny and one non\u2011sunny, contradicting the requirement that the \\(n\\) lines be *distinct* (the same point may lie on several lines, but the lines themselves must be distinct; this is not a contradiction).  \n\nA more direct argument is as follows. Because \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) cannot share a sunny line, each of them must be covered by a non\u2011sunny line. Therefore **at least two** of the \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nDefine the following family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n1. **Two fixed non\u2011sunny lines**\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;x=1 ,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **\\(k\\) sunny lines**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) choose a distinct slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (for instance \\(m_i=i\\)).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y=m_i(x-(i+1))+(i+1) .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S_i\\) passes through the point \\((i+1,i+1)\\) and, because its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\), it is sunny. Distinct choices of the slopes guarantee that the \\(S_i\\) are pairwise distinct and also distinct from \\(L_1,L_2\\).\n\n3. **\\(n-2-k\\) non\u2011sunny diagonal lines**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   3\\le c\\le n+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad c\\neq 2,3,\\dots ,k+2,\n   \\]\n\n   introduce the line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\;x+y=c .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   These lines have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore non\u2011sunny. Their constants are all different, so the lines are distinct from each other and from the lines already listed.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n2\\;(\\text{vertical and horizontal})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-2-k)\\;(\\text{diagonal})\\;=\\;n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(L_1\\).  \n*If \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(L_2\\).\n\nHence we may assume \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \nThen \\(c:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(4\\le c\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(c\\) equals one of the numbers \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+2\\)*, we have \\(c=i+1\\) for some \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \nIn this case \\((a,b)=(i+1,i+1)\\) because both coordinates are at least \\(2\\) and sum to \\(c\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\) defined in (2).\n\n*If \\(c\\) is not among \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+2\\)*, then the diagonal line \\(D_c\\) defined in (3) is present in the family, and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=c\\); hence it lies on \\(D_c\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Counting sunny lines\n\nBy construction exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are sunny; all the other \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny. Therefore the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) realises the prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n* Lemma\u202f1 shows that any admissible configuration must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f2 provides, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
