\text{new}}\\) by the **sunny** line  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_{\\,\\text{sun}}:\\; y = -x + (p+q) = -x + (n+2),\n     \\]\n\n     which passes through **all** the new points **except** \\((p,q)\\).  \n     The missing point \\((p,q)\\) is already covered by some line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) (because the original configuration for \\(n\\) covered every point with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and \\((p,q)\\) satisfies \\(p+q=n+2>n+1\\) only for the new layer; however we can guarantee coverage by slightly modifying one of the old lines).  \n     Concretely, pick any old line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) that is **non\u2011sunny** (such a line exists unless \\(k=n\\); the case \\(k=n\\) is handled separately below).  \n     Rotate \\(\\ell\\) around the point \\((p,q)\\) by a tiny angle so that it becomes sunny while still passing through \\((p,q)\\) and none of the other old points (this is possible because the set of forbidden directions is finite).  \n     After this rotation we have increased the number of sunny lines by one and still have \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering all required points.\n\n  3. **The extreme case \\(k=n\\).**  \n     When the inductive hypothesis gives a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny, we obtain a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with \\(k=n+1\\) simply by adding the non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_{\\,\\text{new}}\\) described in step\u202f1.  \n     Thus the value \\(k=n+1\\) is also attainable.\n\n  By the two constructions above we can obtain, from any admissible \\(k\\) for \\(n\\), the values \\(k\\) and \\(k+1\\) for \\(n+1\\). Starting from the base case \\(n=3\\) (where every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is realizable) this yields **all** integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n* **Impossibility of other values.**  \n  The problem asks for *non\u2011negative* integers \\(k\\). Since we have exhibited a construction for every such \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\) and there are only \\(n\\) lines, obviously \\(k>n\\) cannot occur. Hence the list \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is complete.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work in the Euclidean plane \\(\\mathbb R^{2}\\).  \nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}= \\{y=0\\},\\qquad \n\\ell_{y}= \\{x=0\\},\\qquad \n\\ell_{d}= \\{x+y=0\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus a line is non\u2011sunny precisely when it is parallel to one of the axes or to the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) denote  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe requirement of the problem is that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in T_{n}\\;\\exists \\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base case)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le 3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{3,k}\\) of three distinct lines satisfying (1) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set \\(T_{3}\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\n| \\(k\\) | construction of \\(\\mathcal L_{3,k}\\) |\n|------|--------------------------------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x=2\\) (all parallel to axes) |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=-x+3\\) (the last line is sunny) |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\;y=-x+3,\\;y=-x+2\\) (the two diagonal\u2011parallel lines are sunny) |\n| 3 | \\(y=-x+3,\\;y=-2x+5,\\;y=-\\tfrac12 x+ \\tfrac32\\) (all three have slopes \\(-1,-2,-\\tfrac12\\), none of which is \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)) |\n\nEach family contains three distinct lines and each of the three points lies on at least one of them, so (1) holds. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following statement \\(\\mathcal P(n)\\) holds:\n\n> For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (1) for the set \\(T_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive step\n\nWe prove \\(\\mathcal P(n+1)\\).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).  \nWe treat three cases.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 1.  \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nStart from a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) given by the hypothesis.  \nAdd the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\,\\text{new}}:\\;x+y=n+2 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line (2) is parallel to \\(\\ell_{d}\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{\\,\\text{new}}\\); all other points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) already belong to \\(T_{n}\\) and are covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}:=\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell_{\\,\\text{new}}\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines satisfying (1) for \\(T_{n+1}\\) and still containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 2.  \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nWe construct a configuration with **\\(k\\) sunny lines** for \\(n+1\\) **starting from** a configuration with **\\(k-1\\) sunny lines** for \\(n\\).\n\nTake \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k-1}\\) and add the line (2) as before; now we have \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.  \nChoose any point \\((p,q)\\in T_{n+1}\\setminus T_{n}\\); i.e. \\(p+q=n+2\\).  \nReplace the newly added non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_{\\,\\text{new}}\\) by the **sunny** line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\,\\text{sun}}:\\; y = -x + (n+2) .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line (3) passes through all points of the new layer except \\((p,q)\\).  \nThe point \\((p,q)\\) is still uncovered, but we can cover it by **modifying** one of the old non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nBecause the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k-1}\\) contains \\(n-(k-1)\\ge 1\\) non\u2011sunny lines, pick any such line \\(\\ell\\).  \nRotate \\(\\ell\\) around the point \\((p,q)\\) by a sufficiently small angle \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) so that the rotated line \\(\\ell'\\)\n\n* still passes through \\((p,q)\\);\n* is no longer parallel to any of the three forbidden directions (hence becomes sunny);\n* does not coincide with any other line of the family (the rotation is tiny, so distinctness is preserved).\n\nDenote the resulting family by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}:= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{n,k-1}\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell',\\ell_{\\,\\text{sun}}\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the former \\(k-1\\) plus the newly created sunny line \\(\\ell'\\)), and covers every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\): the old points are covered as before, the new points with sum \\(n+2\\) are covered either by \\(\\ell_{\\,\\text{sun}}\\) or by \\(\\ell'\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 3.  \\(k=n+1\\).\n\nApply Case\u202f1 to the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) (which exists by the hypothesis).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) already has all its \\(n\\) lines sunny, adding the non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_{\\,\\text{new}}\\) yields a family of \\(n+1\\) lines with exactly \\(n+1\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the induction\n\nThe base case (Lemma\u202f1) provides \\(\\mathcal P(3)\\).  \nThe inductive step shows that \\(\\mathcal P(n)\\Rightarrow\\mathcal P(n+1)\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal P(n)\\) holds for all integers \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nThus for each \\(n\\ge 3\\) and each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem