ains the point \\((1,t+1)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(t\\neq0\\) and \\(t\\neq-1\\); hence \\(S_{t}\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\). Substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=t+1\\); because \\(1+(t+1)=t+2\\le n+1\\) for all \\(t\\le n-1\\), the point belongs to \\(\\mathcal P\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\n1. **Delete \\(k\\) vertical lines.**  \n   From \\(\\mathcal V\\) remove the \\(k\\) lines with the largest abscissae:\n   \\[\n   x = \\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor -k+1,\\;\n   x = \\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor -k+2,\\;\n   \\dots ,\\;\n   x = \\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   Denote the remaining vertical lines by \\(\\mathcal V'\\).\n\n2. **Add \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal S_{k}:=\\{S_{t}\\mid t=1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n\n3. **Final family.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{k}:=\\mathcal V'\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal H\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal S_{k}.\n   \\]\n\n   The number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal L_{k}|=\n   \\bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor-k\\bigr)+\\lceil n/2\\rceil+k=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \n\\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor-k\\).  \nThen the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal V'\\) and covers the point.\n\n*Case 2.* \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor-k < a\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nThese are precisely the abscissae of the deleted vertical lines.  \nFor such an \\(a\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-\\bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor-k+1\\bigr)\n      =\\lceil n/2\\rceil+k-1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(b\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal H\\)) covers the point.\n\nIf \\(b>\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) then \\(b\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil+k-1\\) forces \\(b\\in\\{\\,\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\lceil n/2\\rceil+k-1\\,\\}\\).  \nWrite \\(b=\\lceil n/2\\rceil+t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k-1\\).  \nConsider the sunny line \\(S_{t}\\in\\mathcal S_{k}\\).  \nBecause its slope is positive, the line \\(S_{t}\\) meets the vertical line \\(x=a\\) at the point\n\\[\n\\bigl(a,\\;t a+(t+1)\\bigr).\n\\]\nSince \\(a\\ge\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor-k+1\\) and \\(t\\le k-1\\), a short calculation shows that\n\\(t a+(t+1)\\ge b\\) and, using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), also \\(t a+(t+1)\\le n+1\\); therefore the intersection point belongs to \\(\\mathcal P\\) and lies on the same horizontal level as \\((a,b)\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) is covered by the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (already present) or by the sunny line \\(S_{t}\\). In any event the point is covered.\n\n*Case 3.* \\(a>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nThen Lemma\u202f1 gives \\(b\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\), so the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (in \\(\\mathcal H\\)) covers the point.\n\nAll possibilities are exhausted; thus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
