"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(k\\) is a non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds in two parts.\n\n1. **Existence for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   \u2013 Construct explicitly a family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   \u2013 The construction uses three *non\u2011sunny* lines (one horizontal, one vertical, one with slope \\(-1\\)) and the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are taken to be sunny, placed so that each of them passes through a distinct point of the triangle that is not already covered.\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).**  \n   \u2013 Assume, for contradiction, that a configuration with \\(n\\) lines exists in which at most one line is non\u2011sunny.  \n   \u2013 Show that any single non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) of the required points, while the remaining points would have to be covered by sunny lines.  \n   \u2013 Use the fact that a sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently it can intersect the set \\(\\{(a,b):a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in at most two points.  \n   \u2013 Counting points leads to a contradiction when only one (or zero) non\u2011sunny lines are available, proving that at least two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary. Hence \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\nThe contradiction argument is the core of the \u201cimpossibility\u201d part, while the constructive part shows that the bound \\(n-2\\) is sharp.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on the chosen lines.  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nA line that is not sunny will be called **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Existence for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n1. **Three non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad   \n   L_2:\\;x=1,\\qquad   \n   L_3:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   \\(L_1\\) is horizontal, \\(L_2\\) vertical, \\(L_3\\) has slope \\(-1\\); hence they are non\u2011sunny.  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) lies on \\(L_2\\); every point with \\(b=1\\) lies on \\(L_1\\); and every point with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lies on \\(L_3\\).  \n   Consequently the three lines already cover all points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on the three sides of the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\).\n\n2. **Remaining points.**  \n   The interior points of the triangle are those with  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   Their number is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Choose any \\(n-3\\) distinct interior points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1,\\dots ,P_{\\,n-3}\\in T_n\\setminus (L_1\\cup L_2\\cup L_3).\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i\\) let \\(S_i\\) be the unique line through \\(P_i\\) whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); such a line exists because through any point there are infinitely many lines of admissible slopes.  \n   By construction each \\(S_i\\) is sunny, the lines \\(S_i\\) are distinct (they pass through distinct points), and none of them coincides with any of \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).\n\n3. **Counting lines.**  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_1,L_2,L_3,S_1,\\dots ,S_{\\,n-3}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(3+(n-3)=n\\) distinct lines.  \n   Exactly the \\(n-3\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny, i.e. \\(k=n-3\\).  \n\n   To obtain any smaller value of \\(k\\) (down to \\(0\\)) we simply replace some of the sunny lines \\(S_i\\) by additional non\u2011sunny lines of the three allowed directions; each replacement reduces \\(k\\) by one while preserving the covering property, because a line parallel to one of the three forbidden directions can be chosen to pass through a point already covered by a sunny line.  \n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Necessity of at least two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nWe now prove that a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) cannot exist.\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there is a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing at most one non\u2011sunny line.  \nTwo cases arise.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 1: No non\u2011sunny line (all \\(n\\) lines are sunny).\n\nLet \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) be any sunny line.  \nBecause its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), the equation of \\(L\\) can be written as  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+c,\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\;m\\neq\\infty .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting \\(L\\) with the region \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) yields at most two lattice points of \\(T_n\\):  \n\n* If \\(L\\) meets the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) it does so at a single point;  \n* moving along \\(L\\) away from that intersection, the sum \\(a+b\\) changes monotonically, so at most one further lattice point of \\(T_n\\) can be encountered before the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) fails.\n\nThus each sunny line contains **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\).  \nWith \\(n\\) sunny lines we could