"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to prove that every non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\ncan be realised, nor that no larger \\(k\\) is possible.  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following *necessary* inequality for any admissible \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is contained in the interval \\([0,\\lfloor n(n-1)/(2n-4)\\rfloor]\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof uses the pigeon\u2011hole principle together with elementary bounds on how many lattice points of the set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ncan lie on a single line of each of the three prohibited directions (vertical, horizontal, slope \\(-1\\)) and on a line of any other direction (a *sunny* line).\n\n* Lemma 1.  Any line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n* Lemma 2.  Any sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nLet the required configuration consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \nCounting the points of \\(T_n\\) (there are \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) of them) and using Lemmas\u202f1 and\u202f2 gives the inequality  \n\n\\[\n2k+n(n-k)\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA simple algebraic manipulation of (3) yields the bound (1).  \nThus (1) is a necessary condition for the existence of a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAssume that we have \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them, and that exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nDenote by \\(S\\) the set of sunny lines and by \\(N\\) the set of the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines; thus \\(|S|=k\\) and \\(|N|=n-k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), then \\(\\ell\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\)).  \nPoints of \\(T_n\\) on it have the form \\((c,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(c+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\).  \nThus at most \\(n\\) such points exist (the maximum is attained for \\(c=1\\)).  \n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\) is analogous.  \n\n*Line of slope \\(-1\\)*, i.e. \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  \nPoints of \\(T_n\\) on it satisfy \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 1\\); consequently \\(c\\ge 2\\) and the admissible pairs are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\).  \nSince we also need \\(c\\le n+1\\), the number of points is \\(c-1\\le n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, then \\(\\ell\\) contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose, to the contrary, that a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\).  \nBecause the three points are collinear, the slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals the slope of the segment joining any two of them.  \nIf two of the points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the line would be vertical, contradicting sunniness.  \nIf two have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, the line would be horizontal, again a contradiction.  \nIf the sum of the coordinates of two points is equal, i.e. \\(a_i+b_i=a_j+b_j\\) for some \\(i\\neq j\\), then the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), also impossible.  \n\nHence for any pair of points among the three, the three quantities  \n\n\\[\na_i-a_j,\\qquad b_i-b_j,\\qquad (a_i+b_i)-(a_j+b_j)\n\\]\n\nare all non\u2011zero.  Consequently the three points cannot be collinear unless the line is parallel to one of the prohibited directions, which it is not.  This contradiction shows that a sunny line can meet \\(T_n\\) in at most two points. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting argument\n\nEach sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (Lemma\u202f2), and each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points (Lemma\u202f1).  \nTherefore the total number of incidences between the \\(n\\) lines and the set \\(T_n\\) is bounded above by  \n\n\\[\n2k + n\\,(n-k).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) must lie on at least one of the lines, the number of incidences is at least \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n2k + n\\,(n-k)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Deriving the bound on \\(k\\)\n\nRewrite (4):\n\n\\[\n2k + n^2 - nk \\ge \\frac{n^2+n}{2}\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n4k + 2n^2 - 2nk \\ge n^2 + n\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n4k \\ge n + n^2 - 2n^2 + 2nk\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n4k \\ge n(2k - n + 1).\n\\]\n\nBring the term containing \\(k\\) to the left:\n\n\\[\n4k - 2kn \\ge -n^2 + n\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n2k(2-n) \\ge -n(n-1).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\), the factor \\((2-n)\\) is negative; multiplying the inequality by \\(-1\\) reverses the direction:\n\n\\[\n2k(n-2) \\le n(n-1).\n\\]\n\nFinally,\n\n\\[\nk \\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer, we obtain the necessary condition\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), if there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of those lines are sunny, then \\(k\\) must satisfy inequality (5).  In other words, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is contained in  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThis completes the rigorous part of the solution.  (The converse \u2013 that every integer in this range can actually be realised \u2013 remains open.) \u220e"
