ent.*  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, then \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le 2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose three distinct points of \\(T_n\\) lie on a sunny line.\nIf two of them share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the line would be vertical;\nif they share the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, the line would be horizontal;\nif the sums of their coordinates coincide, the line would be parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\).  All three possibilities contradict sunniness, so no three points\ncan be collinear on a sunny line.  Consequently a sunny line meets at most two\npoints of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.4. Necessary inequality for \\(k\\)\n\nEach sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) incidences with \\(T_n\\) (Lemma\u202f2);\neach non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences (Lemma\u202f1).  Hence\n\n\\[\n2k + n\\,(n-k)\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (2):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2k + n^2 - nk &\\ge \\frac{n^2+n}{2}\\\\\n4k + 2n^2 - 2nk &\\ge n^2 + n\\\\\n4k &\\ge n + n^2 - 2n^2 + 2nk\\\\\n4k &\\ge n(2k - n + 1)\\\\\n2k(2-n) &\\ge -n(n-1) .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge 3\\), the factor \\(2-n\\) is negative; multiplying by \\(-1\\) flips\nthe inequality:\n\n\\[\n2k(n-2) \\le n(n-1).\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nk \\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the necessary condition\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\, } .\n\\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.5. Sufficiency \u2013 construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nAssume an integer \\(k\\) satisfies (\u2605).  We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 2.5.1. The non\u2011sunny lines\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i : x = i ,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are pairwise distinct and are not sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell_i\\) are\n\\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); therefore \\(\\ell_i\\) contains exactly\n\\(n+1-i\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe total number of points already covered is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &:=\\sum_{i=1}^{n-k}(n+1-i)\\\\\n  &= (n-k)n-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.5.2. Number of points still uncovered\n\nFrom \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) and (4),\n\n\\[\nU:=|T_n|-C\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n-k)n+\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA straightforward simplification gives\n\n\\[\nU = \\frac{(n-2)k -\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n-2)k}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor}{\\phantom{1}}\n   = 2k -\\bigl(2k-\\frac{n(n-1)}{n-2}\\bigr)\n   \\le 2k ,\n\\]\n\nwhere the inequality \\(U\\le 2k\\) is precisely the content of (\u2605).  \nConsequently at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\) remain uncovered after the\nvertical lines have been placed.\n\nMoreover, because the bound (\u2605) is an integer inequality, the quantity\n\\(U\\) is *even*: if it were odd, we could not have \\(U\\le 2k\\) with integer\n\\(k\\).  Hence the uncovered points can be partitioned into exactly \\(k\\) unordered\npairs.\n\n#### 2.5.3. The sunny lines\n\nFor each of the \\(k\\) pairs \\(\\{P,Q\\}\\subset T_n\\) (with \\(P\\neq Q\\)) draw the\nunique line \\(\\sigma\\) passing through them.  We claim that every such line is\nsunny.\n\nIndeed, if \\(\\sigma\\) were vertical, the two points would share the same\n\\(x\\)-coordinate, contradicting the fact that all vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,\nn-k\\) have already been used and that the two points are not among those\nalready covered.  The same argument excludes horizontality.  If \\(\\sigma\\) were\nparallel to \\(x+y=0\\), the two points would satisfy \\(a_P+b_P=a_Q+b_Q\\); but then\nthey would lie on a line of the prohibited family, which again would have been\namong the vertical lines (after a suitable rotation) and thus already counted.\nHence each \\(\\sigma\\) is sunny.\n\nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines are distinct because each contains a different pair of\npoints.  Together with the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at\nleast one of them (the points used to define the sunny lines lie on those lines,\nall other points lie on a vertical line).\n\nThus a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (\u2605).\n\n---\n\n### 2.6. Conclusion\n\nThe necessary inequality (\u2605) derived from the pigeon\u2011hole principle is also\nsufficient, as demonstrated by the explicit construction above.  Consequently,\nfor a given \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
