points).  \n  Hence the total capacity of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most\n  \\((n-k)n\\).\n\n* The covering is possible only if the capacity bound dominates the required\n  number of points:\n  \\[\n  (n-k)n\\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k .\n  \\]\n  Rearranging gives\n  \\[\n  2kn\\; \\ge\\; n^{2}+n-4k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  k\\,(2n+4)\\;\\ge\\; n^{2}+n .\n  \\]\n  Solving for \\(k\\) yields the inequality\n  \\[\n  k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2n+4}= \\frac{n}{2} -\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{2n+4}.\n  \\]\n\n  Since \\(k\\) is an integer, the smallest integer satisfying this inequality is\n  \\(k\\ge n-1\\).  Therefore, if \\(k=n\\) or \\(k=n-1\\) the inequality fails, i.e.\n  the required number of points cannot be covered.\n\nThus any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to lie on at least one of the \\(n\\)\nlines.  Its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nand we denote this number by \\(N\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Geometry of sunny and non\u2011sunny lines\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is **not** parallel to any of the three\ndirections\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}: y=0\\;(x\\text{-axis}),\\qquad\n\\ell_{2}: x=0\\;(y\\text{-axis}),\\qquad\n\\ell_{3}: x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently a sunny line has a slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n*Lemma 2.1.*  \nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2}), (a_{3},b_{3})\\in\\mathcal{P}\\).\nIf two of them have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, then \\(L\\) is vertical,\ncontradicting sunniness.  If two have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, then \\(L\\)\nis horizontal, again a contradiction.  If two satisfy \\(a_{i}+b_{i}=a_{j}+b_{j}\\),\nthen \\(L\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), also impossible.  Since among three\npoints at least one of these three equalities must hold (pigeonhole\nprinciple on the three possible invariants \\(x\\), \\(y\\), \\(x+y\\)), we obtain a\ncontradiction.  \u220e\n\nThus each sunny line contributes at most two covered points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Capacity of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nA non\u2011sunny line must be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\nHence it is of one of the following forms:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{vertical: }x=c,\\qquad 1\\le c\\le n,\\\\\n&\\text{horizontal: }y=c,\\qquad 1\\le c\\le n,\\\\\n&\\text{diagonal: }x+y=c,\\qquad 2\\le c\\le n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(c\\) the intersection of the line with the triangular region\n\\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x\\ge 1,\\;y\\ge 1,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}\\) consists of at most \\(n\\) lattice\npoints:\n\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets the region in the points \\((c,b)\\) with\n  \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) points;\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) is analogous;\n* a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) meets the region in the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with\n  \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\), again at most \\(n\\) points because \\(c\\le n+1\\).\n\nHence **every non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Counting argument\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny\n(\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).  Let the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non\u2011sunny.\n\n* The sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points (Lemma\u202f2.1).\n* Consequently the non\u2011sunny lines must cover at least  \n\n  \\[\n  N-2k=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  points.\n\n* Since each non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points, the total\n  capacity of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\((n-k)n\\).\n\nTherefore a necessary condition for the existence of such a configuration is\n\n\\[\n(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Solving the inequality\n\nRewrite (4):\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-kn\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}-2k\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\; 2n^{2}-2kn\\;\\ge\\; n^{2}+n-4k\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\; n^{2}+n\\;\\le\\; 2kn+4k\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\; n^{2}+n\\;\\le\\; k(2n+4)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\; k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2n+4}\n= \\frac{n}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{2n+4}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer, the right\u2011hand side of (5) is strictly larger than\n\\(\\frac{n}{2}-\\frac12\\).  The smallest integer satisfying (5) is therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently, if \\(k=n\\) or \\(k=n-1\\) the inequality (4) fails, i.e. the\nrequired set of points cannot be covered by \\(n\\) distinct lines with that\nmany sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved that for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\nin a feasible configuration must satisfy\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nIn particular, configurations with \\(k=n\\) or \\(k=n-1\\) are impossible.  This\nprovides a necessary condition on \\(k\\); the determination of whether every\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is actually attainable remains open. \u220e"
