sufficient, completing the\ncharacterisation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Notation and the set of points to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\;\\Big|\\;a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;\na+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the lattice points that must lie on at least one of the\n\\(n\\) lines.  The cardinality of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|\n =\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)\n =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\;=:N. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Geometry of sunny and non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}: y=0\\;(x\\text{-axis}),\\qquad\n\\ell_{2}: x=0\\;(y\\text{-axis}),\\qquad\n\\ell_{3}: x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently a sunny line has a slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2}), (a_{3},b_{3})\\in\\mathcal P\\).\nAmong three numbers there must be a repetition of one of the three\nquantities \\(a\\), \\(b\\) or \\(a+b\\); i.e. either\n\\(a_{i}=a_{j}\\), or \\(b_{i}=b_{j}\\), or \\(a_{i}+b_{i}=a_{j}+b_{j}\\) for some\n\\(i\\neq j\\).  \nIf \\(a_{i}=a_{j}\\) the line is vertical, parallel to \\(\\ell_{2}\\);  \nif \\(b_{i}=b_{j}\\) the line is horizontal, parallel to \\(\\ell_{1}\\);  \nif \\(a_{i}+b_{i}=a_{j}+b_{j}\\) the line is parallel to \\(\\ell_{3}\\).  \nAll three possibilities contradict the definition of a sunny line.\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.2  \nA line that is parallel to one of \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\) (i.e. a\nnon\u2011sunny line) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuch a line is of one of the forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n),\\qquad\ny=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n),\\qquad\nx+y=c\\;(2\\le c\\le n+1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n* For \\(x=c\\) the admissible points are \\((c,b)\\) with\n  \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them.\n* For \\(y=c\\) the situation is symmetric.\n* For \\(x+y=c\\) the admissible points are \\((a,c-a)\\) with\n  \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); because \\(c\\le n+1\\) we again have at most \\(n\\) points.\n\nThus any non\u2011sunny line meets \\(\\mathcal P\\) in at most \\(n\\) points. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny\n(\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).  Let the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non\u2011sunny.\n\n* By Lemma\u202f2.1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of\n  \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n* Hence the non\u2011sunny lines must cover at least  \n\n  \\[\n  N-2k=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  points.\n* By Lemma\u202f2.2 each non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points, so the\n  total capacity of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\((n-k)n\\).\n\nTherefore a necessary condition for the existence of such a configuration is  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Solving the inequality  \n\nRewrite (4):\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-kn\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}-2k\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\;2n^{2}-2kn\\;\\ge\\;n^{2}+n-4k\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\;n^{2}+n\\;\\le\\;2kn+4k\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\;(2n+4)\\;\\ge\\;n^{2}+n .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2n+4}\n      =\\frac{n}{2}-\\frac12+\\frac{2}{2n+4}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe right\u2013hand side of (5) is strictly larger than \\(\\frac{n}{2}-\\frac12\\);\nthe smallest integer satisfying (5) is therefore \\(k\\ge n-1\\).\nConsequently, if \\(k=n\\) or \\(k=n-1\\) inequality (4) is violated, i.e. the\nrequired covering is impossible.\n\nThus any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now show that (6) is also sufficient.\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }m\\ge 2).\n\\]\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny lines.**  \nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}: x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,m). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny, and they are\npairwise distinct.\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) with \\(a\\le m\\) lies on exactly one of the\nlines \\(L_{i}\\).  Consequently the points that are *not* yet covered are those\nwith  \n\n\\[\na\\ge m+1=n-k+1,\\qquad b\\ge 1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 counting the uncovered points.**  \nFrom (8) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(m+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(b\\) can take the values \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\).  For each such \\(b\\) the\ncorresponding \\(a\\) is forced to be \\(a=n+1-b\\), which satisfies \\(a\\ge m+1\\).\nHence the set of uncovered points is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q=\\{(n+1-b,b)\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\cup\n           \\{(m+b,b)\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,k\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. it consists of \\(2k\\) points, two for each \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n\n**Step 3 \u2013 sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) join the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_{b}^{(1)}=(m+b,b),\\qquad P_{b}^{(2)}=(n+1-b,b)\n\\]\n\nby the unique line \\(S_{b}\\) passing through them.  Because the two points have\ndifferent \\(x\\)-coordinates, different \\(y\\)-coordinate