rac{a+b-1}{a+b}\\,a + \\frac{1}{a+b}\n          = \\frac{a(a+b-1)+1}{a+b}\n          = \\frac{a^2+ab-a+1}{a+b}\n          = b,\n   \\]\n   where the last equality follows from the elementary identity\n   \\(a^2+ab-a+1 = b(a+b)\\) that holds precisely because \\(i=a+b-1\\).\n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{a+b-1}\\).  Consequently every admissible\n   integer point is contained in at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nThese two constructions settle the extreme values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of integer points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (All lines non\u2011sunny, \\(k=0\\))\n\n*Construction.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i : x=i .\n\\]\n\n*Proof that the construction works.*  \n\n*   Each \\(\\ell_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence it is not sunny.\n*   The lines are distinct because they have different \\(x\\)-intercepts.\n*   Take any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Since \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), the point satisfies\n    \\(x=a\\) and therefore lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\nThus the \\(n\\) vertical lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) and contain\nexactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (All lines sunny, \\(k=n\\))\n\n*Construction.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i :\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof that the construction works.*  \n\n1. **Sunny property.**  \n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\).  This number is never\n   \\(0\\) (because \\(i\\ge1\\)), never infinite (the line is not vertical), and\n   never \\(-1\\) (since \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}>0\\)).  Hence each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n2. **Distinctness.**  \n   If \\(i\\neq j\\) then \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\neq\\frac{j}{