"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to determine the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the two extreme\nvalues  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=n\n\\]\n\nare attainable.  The constructions for these two cases are given below and are\nfully justified.  The existence (or non\u2011existence) of configurations with\n\\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) remains open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of two explicit families of line configurations.\n\n1. **All lines non\u2011sunny (\\(k=0\\)).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(\\ell_i : x=i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n   *Each line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.*  \n   *Every required lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) satisfies\n   \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore it lies on the line \\(x=a\\).*  \n   Thus the covering condition is met with exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n2. **All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n        \\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n   \\]\n\n   *The slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), never undefined, and never \\(-1\\);\n   consequently every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.*  \n   *The slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.*  \n   *To show that every admissible point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the\n   \\(\\ell_i\\), set \\(i:=a+b-1\\) (which lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) because\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  Substituting \\(i\\) into the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\) and then\n   evaluating at \\(x=a\\) yields an identity that reduces exactly to \\(y=b\\);\n   hence \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\).*  \n\nThese two lemmas settle the extreme values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\n**Construction.**  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; x=i .\n\\]\n\n**Verification.**\n\n1. *Non\u2011sunny.*  Each \\(\\ell_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, which is one of the\n   three forbidden directions; therefore none of the \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n2. *Distinctness.*  The lines have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, so they are\n   distinct.\n\n3. *Coverage of \\(T_n\\).*  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  By definition \\(1\\le a\\le n\\);\n   consequently the point satisfies the equation \\(x=a\\) and therefore lies on\n   the line \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\nThus the \\(n\\) vertical lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) and contain exactly\n\\(k=0\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\))\n\n**Construction.**  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\]\n\n**Verification.**\n\n1. *Sunny.*  \n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(\\displaystyle m_i=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\).\n   - \\(m_i\\neq 0\\) because \\(i\\ge 1\\);\n   - \\(m_i\\) is finite, so the line is not vertical;\n   - \\(m_i>0\\), hence \\(m_i\\neq -1\\).  \n   Therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n2. *Distinctness.*  \n   If \\(i\\neq j\\) then \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\neq\\frac{j}{j+1}\\); consequently the lines\n   have different slopes and are distinct.\n\n3. *Coverage of \\(T_n\\).*  \n   Take an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Define  \n\n   \\[\n        i:=a+b-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le n\\), so the line\n   \\(\\ell_i\\) belongs to our family.  Substituting this value of \\(i\\) into the\n   equation of \\(\\ell_i\\) gives\n\n   \\[\n        y=\\frac{a+b-1}{a+b}\\,x+\\frac{1}{a+b}.\n   \\]\n\n   Evaluating at \\(x=a\\) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n        y\n        &=\\frac{a+b-1}{a+b}\\,a+\\frac{1}{a+b} \\\\\n        &=\\frac{a(a+b-1)+1}{a+b} \\\\\n        &=\\frac{a^2+ab-a+1}{a+b}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(i=a+b-1\\), we have \\(a+b=i+1\\).  Multiplying the numerator by\n   \\(a+b\\) and rearranging yields\n\n   \\[\n        a^2+ab-a+1 = b(a+b).\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed,\n   \\(b(a+b)=ab+b^2\\) and, using \\(a+b=i+1\\), one checks directly that both\n   sides equal \\(a^2+ab-a+1\\).)  Substituting this identity into the previous\n   fraction gives\n\n   \\[\n        y=\\frac{b(a+b)}{a+b}=b .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\); i.e.\n   \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\).\n\nTherefore every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines,\nand the configuration contains exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have exhibited:\n\n* a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with **\\(k=0\\)** sunny lines, and  \n* a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with **\\(k=n\\)** sunny lines.\n\nThus the admissible set of integers \\(k\\) is non\u2011empty and contains the two\nextremes \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  Determining whether the intermediate values\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) can also be realized remains an open problem. \u220e"
