formed by induction on \\(n\\).  \n   *Base case* \\(n=3\\): explicit drawings show that \\(k=0,2,3\\) are realizable, while \\(k=1\\) is impossible by part\u202f1.  \n   *Inductive step*: assuming a configuration for \\(n-1\\) with any admissible \\(k'\\), we add the point \\((n,1)\\) (the new point appearing when the bound \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is increased) together with a new line.  \n   - If we wish to keep the number of sunny lines unchanged, we add a non\u2011sunny line passing through \\((n,1)\\) (possible because \\((n,1)\\) is not on any of the three forbidden families already used).  \n   - If we wish to increase the number of sunny lines by one, we add a sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) that does not coincide with any existing line.  \n   This shows that, starting from the base case, every \\(k\\neq 1\\) can be achieved for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nThe two parts together give the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \n\\(|T_n|=\\displaystyle\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a **non\u2011sunny** line is of one of the three types  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_c &: y=c \\qquad\\text{(horizontal)},\\\\\nV_c &: x=c \\qquad\\text{(vertical)},\\\\\nD_c &: x+y=c \\qquad\\text{(diagonal of slope }-1\\text{)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Impossibility of a single sunny line)\n\n*For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there is no family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) in which exactly one line is sunny.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nAssume such a family exists. Let \\(L_s\\) be the unique sunny line and let the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines be of the three types in (1).  \n\nEach horizontal line \\(H_c\\) can contain at most one point of \\(T_n\\) because points of \\(T_n\\) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates ranging from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\).  \nSimilarly each vertical line \\(V_c\\) contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nA diagonal line \\(D_c\\) contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\): if \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(D_c\\) then \\(a+b=c\\); the only other lattice point on the same diagonal with positive coordinates is \\((a',b')\\) with \\(a'=b,\\;b'=a\\), and both satisfy \\(a'+b'=c\\). Because \\(a\\neq b\\) for all points of \\(T_n\\) except possibly \\((\\frac{c}{2},\\frac{c}{2})\\), at most two points of \\(T_n\\) lie on a given diagonal.\n\nHence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{horizontal/vertical}}+ \\underbrace{2}_{\\text{one diagonal at most}} \\le n+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n(The bound \\(2\\) for diagonals is crude; in fact at most one diagonal can be used because two distinct diagonals are parallel and would coincide with the sunny line\u2019s slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden.)\n\nSince \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n+1\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), the points of \\(T_n\\) cannot all be covered, a contradiction. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Base configurations)\n\n*For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,2,3\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\n* \\(k=0\\): take the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\). Each of the six points lies on one of them.\n\n* \\(k=2\\): use the sunny lines \\(y=2x\\) and \\(y=\\frac12 x\\) together with the non\u2011sunny line \\(x=3\\). One checks that every point of \\(T_3\\) belongs to at least one of the three lines.\n\n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines \\(y=2x,\\;y=\\frac12 x,\\;y=-\\frac13 x+ \\frac{4}{3}\\) already cover all six points.\n\nThe case \\(k=1\\) is excluded by Lemma\u202f1. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Inductive step)\n\n*Assume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for a given integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n,\\;k\\neq 1\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThen for the same \\(k\\) (if \\(k\\le n\\)) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and for \\(k+1\\) (provided \\(k+1\\le n+1\\)) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\) covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe new points that appear when passing from \\(T_n\\) to \\(T_{n+1}\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nP_{n+1}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+2,\\;1\\le a,b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nPick any point \\((a_0,b_0)\\in P_{n+1}\\) that is not already on a non\u2011sunny line of the existing configuration (such a point exists because each non\u2011sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(P_{n+1}\\), while \\(|P_{n+1}|=n+1\\)).  \n\n*To keep the number of sunny lines equal to \\(k\\):* add the non\u2011sunny line \\(x=a_0\\) (or \\(y=b_0\\) or \\(x+y=a_0+b_0\\)) that passes through \\((a_0,b_0)\\) and is not parallel to any line already present. This line is distinct from all previous ones, and all points of \\(P_{n+1}\\) are now covered (the added line covers \\((a_0,b_0)\\); the remaining points of \\(P_{n+1}\\) were already covered by the original \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) because they belong to \\(T_n\\)). Thus we obtain \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\).\n\n*To increase the number of sunny lines by one:* choose a slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\) that is different from the slopes of all sunny lines already present. The line \\(y=m(x-a_0)+b_0\\) passes through \\((a_0,b_0)\\) and is sunny. Adding it to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) yields a family of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have explicit configurations for \\(n=3\\) and all admissible \\(k\\) (i.e. \\(k\\neq 1\\)).  \nApplying Lemma\u202f3 repeatedly, we can increase \\(n\\) while preserving any admissible \\(k\\) or raising it by one, never creating the forbidden value \\(k=1\\). Consequently, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n,\\qquad k\\neq 1,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTogether with Lemma\u202f1, which rules out \\(k=1\\), this yields the full description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
