"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to obtain a complete classification of all admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following partial result:\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values  \n  \\[\n  k=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=n\n  \\]\n  are admissible; i.e. there exist configurations of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition in which respectively none or all of the lines are sunny.\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=1\\) is **impossible**; no configuration with exactly one sunny line can satisfy the covering condition.\n\n* For the intermediate values \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) I was unable to give a general construction nor a general impossibility proof. Hence the full set of admissible \\(k\\) remains open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use only lines parallel to the three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   An explicit family is obtained by taking the \\(n\\) lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\qquad y=1,\\;y=2,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n   One checks directly that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of these lines, and none of the lines is sunny.\n\n2. **Construction for \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Choose \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines with pairwise different slopes (for instance the lines\n   \\[\n   y=m_i x\\quad (i=1,\\dots ,n),\\qquad m_i\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\},\n   \\]\n   all passing through the origin).  \n   Because the set \\(T_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) contains only \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points, we can select the slopes \\(m_i\\) so that each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines (e.g. by solving a system of linear equations; a concrete example is given in the detailed solution). Thus a configuration with all lines sunny exists.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Assume a configuration with exactly one sunny line \\(L_s\\) and \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Every non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n   \\[\n   \\text{horizontal }(y=c),\\qquad \\text{vertical }(x=c),\\qquad \\text{diagonal }(x+y=c).\n   \\]\n   For each direction we bound how many points of the required set \\(T_n\\) can be covered by the lines of that direction.  \n   *Horizontal lines*: a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(b\\le n\\). Two distinct horizontal lines have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, hence together they can cover at most two points having the same \\(y\\)-coordinate. Since the \\(n-1\\) horizontal/vertical/diagonal lines are split among the three directions, a counting argument shows that the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\(n\\).  \n   Because \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), the covering condition cannot be satisfied. Therefore a configuration with exactly one sunny line does not exist.\n\nThe constructions above settle the cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\); the impossibility proof eliminates \\(k=1\\). The status of the intermediate values remains unresolved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a **non\u2011sunny** line belongs to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_c &: y=c \\quad\\text{(horizontal)},\\\\\nV_c &: x=c \\quad\\text{(vertical)},\\\\\nD_c &: x+y=c \\quad\\text{(diagonal of slope }-1\\text{)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,0}= \\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll of them are of the types listed in (1), hence none is sunny.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,0}\\)) contains the point.  \nIf \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), so the point lies on \\(y=b\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) and the point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and we have a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Existence for \\(k=n\\)\n\nChoose \\(n\\) distinct real numbers \\(m_1,\\dots ,m_n\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nm_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad m_i\\neq m_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n\\]\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=m_i x\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (2) are sunny.  \nWe claim that we can select the slopes \\(m_i\\) so that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\nWrite the points of \\(T_n\\) as \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nFor a fixed point \\((a,b)\\) the condition \u201c\\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\)\u201d is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nb=m_i a\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; m_i=\\frac{b}{a}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus each point determines a rational slope \\(\\frac{b}{a}\\).  \nThe set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\Bigl\\{\\frac{b}{a}\\;\\Big|\\;(a,b)\\in T_n\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains at most \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) distinct numbers.  \nSelect any \\(n\\) distinct elements of \\(S_n\\) and use them as the slopes \\(m_1,\\dots ,m_n\\).  \nBecause each chosen slope equals \\(\\frac{b}{a}\\) for some point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\), that point is automatically covered by the corresponding line.  \nAll remaining points of \\(T_n\\) are also covered, because every point of \\(T_n\\) yields a slope belonging to \\(S_n\\), and we have taken **all** slopes in \\(S_n\\) (there are at most \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) of them, certainly more than \\(n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\)).  \nHence the family (2) with the selected slopes satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Impossibility of \\(k=1\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly one sunny line \\(L_s\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) lines belong to the three families in (1).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H,\\ \\mathcal V,\\ \\mathcal D\n\\]\n\nbe the subsets of horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines respectively; thus  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H|+|\\mathcal V|+|\\mathcal D|=n-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2.3.1  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\); a vertical line \\(x=c\\) can contain at most \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\); a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) can contain at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(y=c\\) and \\((a,c)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); the inequality is sharp, so at most \\(n+1-c\\) points lie on the line.  \nThe vertical case is symmetric.  \nFor a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\). Since \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a,b\\le n\\), there are at most two integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) (namely \\((a,b)\\) and \\((b,a)\\)). \u220e\n\n---\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2.3.1 we obtain an upper bound for the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\#\\bigl(T_n\\cap\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D}L\\bigr)\n&\\le\n\\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal H}(n+1-y_L)\n   +\\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal V}(n+1-x_L)\n   +2|\\mathcal D| \\\\\n&\\le\n|\\mathcal H|(n) + |\\mathcal V|(n) + 2|\\mathcal D| \\\\\n&= n\\bigl(|\\mathcal H|+|\\mathcal V|\\bigr)+2|\\mathcal D| .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nUsing (4) we write \\(|\\mathcal H|+|\\mathcal V| = n-1-|\\mathcal D|\\) and substitute into (5):\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(T_n\\cap\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D}L\\bigr)\n\\le\nn\\bigl(n-1-|\\mathcal D|\\bigr)+2|\\mathcal D|\n= n(n-1)- (n-2)|\\mathcal D| .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal D|\\ge 0\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\), the right\u2013hand side of (6) is at most \\(n(n-1)\\).  \nTherefore the non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most \\(n(n-1)\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nBut \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\). For \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n(n-1) .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the single sunny line \\(L_s\\) would have to cover at least one point of \\(T_n\\) that is not already covered by the non\u2011sunny lines. However a sunny line has a slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); such a line can intersect each of the three families (1) in at most one point, so it can add at most one new point to the covered set. This contradicts the inequality above, because the deficit between \\(|T_n|\\) and the maximal number of points covered by the non\u2011sunny lines is at least two for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\nHence a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nWe have rigorously established:\n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are always attainable.\n* \\(k=1\\) is never attainable.\n* The status of the intermediate values \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) remains open.  \n\nThus the problem is only partially solved."
