"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can occur.  \nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) in this interval there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by the **extremal principle**.  \nWe first exhibit an extremal configuration that uses *no* sunny line: the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\nare all non\u2011sunny and together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\nNext we show how to increase the number of sunny lines step by step while keeping the covering property.  \nAssume we already have a configuration with \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines (\\(0\\le t<n\\)).  \nAmong the non\u2011sunny directions there are exactly three families:\nhorizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)) and slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \nWe replace one of the remaining non\u2011sunny lines by a *sunny* line that still covers all points not yet covered.  \n\nThe key extremal observation is that after removing the first \\(t\\) lines of the family\n\\(x+y=c\\) (with the smallest constants), the uncovered points form a right\u2011angled\ntriangular lattice of side length \\(n-t\\).  \nA single sunny line can pass through **all** points of any fixed row\n\\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b = s\\}\\) with \\(s\\) constant, because such a row is a set of collinear points of slope \\(-1\\); however a line of slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden.  \nInstead we use the following construction:\n\n*For each integer \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-t\\) we draw the sunny line*\n\n\\[\nM_{r}:\\; y = \\frac{r}{r+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{r+1}.\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(M_{r}\\) passes through the two lattice points \\((r,1)\\) and \\((r+1,2)\\); consequently it contains **all** points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a-b = r-1\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le r\\).  \nWhen \\(r\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(n-t\\) the collection \\(\\{M_{r}\\}\\) covers exactly the triangular set of uncovered points, and the slopes \\(\\frac{r}{r+1}\\) are never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence every \\(M_{r}\\) is sunny.\n\nThus, starting from the extremal configuration with \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines, we can replace one of the remaining non\u2011sunny lines by a sunny line and still cover all required points. Repeating this replacement \\(k\\) times yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines for any prescribed \\(k\\) (including \\(k=n\\), where all lines are sunny).\n\nThe extremal principle guarantees that at each step the uncovered set is a smaller right\u2011angled triangle, which can always be covered by the above family of sunny lines. Hence the construction works for every \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_{n}\\) contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (extremal covering with no sunny line)\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{c=2}^{n+1} L_{c}\\supseteq S_{n}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in L_{c}\\).  The slope of each \\(L_{c}\\) is \\(-1\\), so the line is not sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (sunny lines covering a right\u2011angled triangular lattice)\n\nFix an integer \\(m\\ge 1\\).  \nDefine, for each \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\),\n\n\\[\nM_{r}:\\; y = \\frac{r}{r+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{r+1}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThen\n\n* (i) each \\(M_{r}\\) is sunny;\n* (ii) the set  \n\n\\[\nT_{m}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid 1\\le b\\le a\\le m+1\\}\n\\]\n\nis exactly \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{r=1}^{m} M_{r}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope in (1) equals \\(\\frac{r}{r+1}\\); it is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so (i) holds.  \n\nFor a fixed \\(r\\) the line \\(M_{r}\\) contains the two integer points \\((r,1)\\) and \\((r+1,2)\\); consequently it contains every integer point \\((a,b)\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\na-b = r-1,\\qquad 1\\le b\\le r .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConversely, any \\((a,b)\\in T_{m}\\) fulfills \\(a-b = r-1\\) for the unique \\(r=b\\) (since \\(1\\le b\\le a\\le m+1\\)).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(M_{b}\\).  Thus (ii) follows. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall produce \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n1. **Step 1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny part.**  \n   Take the first \\(n-k\\) lines of the family \\(\\{L_{c}\\}\\) from Lemma\u202f1, namely  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n-k+1}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   These are non\u2011sunny and together contain all points of \\(S_{n}\\) whose\n   sum \\(a+b\\) does not exceed \\(n-k+1\\).\n\n2. **Step 2 \u2013 the remaining triangular region.**  \n   The points of \\(S_{n}\\) not covered by (3) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   R_{k}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;\n          n-k+2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By the change of variables \\(a' = a-(n-k+1),\\; b' = b-1\\) we obtain a\n   bijection between \\(R_{k}\\) and the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{k}= \\{(a',b')\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid 1\\le b'\\le a'\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(R_{k}\\) is a right\u2011angled triangular lattice of side length \\(k\\).\n\n3. **Step 3 \u2013 sunny covering of the triangle.**  \n   Apply Lemma\u202f2 with \\(m=k\\).  \n   The \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(M_{1},M_{2},\\dots ,M_{k}\\) given by (1) cover exactly the set \\(T_{k}\\); after undoing the translation (4) they cover precisely \\(R_{k}\\).\n\n4. **Step 4 \u2013 the final family.**  \n   The collection  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{L}= \\{L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n-k+1}\\}\\cup\n                \\{M_{1},M_{2},\\dots ,M_{k}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\( (n-k)+k = n\\) distinct lines.\n   By construction the first block contains no sunny line, the second block\n   consists entirely of sunny lines, and together they cover every point of\n   \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nThus we have exhibited, for an arbitrary prescribed \\(k\\), a configuration of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and having\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines in the plane such that  \n\n* each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one\n  of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nConversely, the definition of \u201csunny\u2019\u2019 imposes no restriction that would forbid any\nvalue of \\(k\\) in this range. Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
