(m\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(n-t\\), the strips (3) partition the whole set\n   \\(R_{t}\\).  Hence the family\n   \\[\n   \\{S_{1},S_{2},\\dots ,S_{\\,n-t}\\}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   covers the entire triangle \\(R_{t}\\) and consists solely of sunny lines.\n\n4. **Increasing the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from the extremal configuration of Lemma\u202f1 (which has \\(t=0\\)),\n   replace the non\u2011sunny line \\(x+y=n-t+2\\) by the sunny line \\(S_{\\,n-t}\\)\n   given in (4).  After this replacement the new family still covers all\n   required points and now contains \\(t+1\\) sunny lines.  \n   Repeating the replacement step \\(k\\) times yields a configuration with\n   exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n5. **Extremal principle justification.**  \n   At each iteration the uncovered region is a strictly smaller right\u2011angled\n   triangle, so the process must terminate after at most \\(n\\) steps.  Because\n   we can perform the replacement as long as a non\u2011sunny line remains, we can\n   obtain **any** number \\(k\\) of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\nThus, by an extremal\u2011stepwise replacement of non\u2011sunny lines with the sunny\nlines \\(S_{m}\\), we obtain the desired configuration for every admissible\n\\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (extremal covering with no sunny line)\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{c=2}^{n+1} L_{c}\\supseteq S_{n}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(c=a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\