            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (extremal covering with no sunny line)\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{c=2}^{n+1} L_{c}\\supseteq S_{n}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(c=a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); hence\n\\((a,b)\\in L_{c}\\).  Each \\(L_{c}\\) has slope \\(-1\\), so it is not sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (a sunny line covering a diagonal strip)\n\nFor each integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{m}:\\; y = \\frac{m}{m+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{m+1}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThen\n\n* (i) \\(S_{m}\\) is sunny;\n* (ii) \\(S_{m}\\) contains exactly the lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with  \n\n  \\[\n  a-b = m-1,\\qquad 1\\le b\\le m .\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope in (5) equals \\(\\frac{m}{m+1}\\); it is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or\n\\(-1\\), so (i) holds.  \n\nMultiplying (5) by \\(m+1\\) gives \\((m+1)y = mx+1\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nmx-(m+1)y+1=0 .\n\\]\n\nFor an integer point \\((a,b)\\) this is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nm a = (m+1)b-1 \\Longleftrightarrow a-b = \\frac{b-1}{m}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b\\) are integers, the right\u2011hand side must be an integer; thus\n\\(m\\mid (b-1)\\).  Writing \\(b-1 = m t\\) with \\(t\\ge 0\\) we obtain\n\\(a-b = t\\).  The condition \\(b\\le m\\) forces \\(t\\le m-1\\), and \\(b\\ge 1\\)\ngives \\(t\\ge 0\\).  Hence the integer solutions are precisely those described\nby (6). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (the uncovered region after removing the largest non\u2011sunny line)\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of lines that covers \\(S_{n}\\) and that\ncontains exactly \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all of which belong to the set\n\\(\\{L_{c}\\}\\) from Lemma\u202f1.  Let \\(L_{c^{*}}\\) be the line among them with the\nlargest constant term \\(c^{*}\\).  Then the set of points of \\(S_{n}\\) not\ncovered by \\(\\mathcal{F}\\setminus\\{L_{c^{*}}\\}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nR_{t}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;\n          n-t+2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe lines \\(L_{c}\\) with \\(c\\le n-t+1\\) are still present, so every point with\n\\(a+b\\le n-t+1\\) remains covered.  The removed line is \\(x+y=n-t+2\\); therefore\npoints whose sum is at least \\(n-t+2\\) may become uncovered.  Because the\noriginal family covered all of \\(S_{n}\\), any point with sum larger than\n\\(n-t+2\\) is covered either by a remaining non\u2011sunny line (which has a smaller\nconstant term) or by a sunny line.  Removing only the line with the largest\nconstant term eliminates precisely those points whose sum lies in the interval\n\\([\\,n-t+2,\\;n+1\\,]\\) and that were covered **only** by that line.  Hence the set\nof uncovered points is exactly (7). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nWe build a family \\(\\mathcal{L}_{k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the\nrequirements and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny part.**  \n   Take the first \\(n-k\\) lines of the family \\(\\{L_{c}\\}\\):\n   \\[\n   L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n-k+1}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   These are non\u2011sunny and together cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\) whose sum is\n   at most \\(n-k+1\\).\n\n2. **Remaining triangular region.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f3 with \\(t=n-k\\) the points of \\(S_{n}\\) not covered by (8) are\n   precisely the set \\(R_{\\,n-k}\\) defined in (7) with \\(t=n-k\\); i.e.\n   \\[\n   R_{\\,n-k}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;\n               k+2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Write a point \\((a,b)\\in R_{\\,n-k}\\) as \\((a,b)=(b+m,b)\\) where\n   \\(m=a-b\\ge 0\\).  From \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\) we obtain \\(b\\ge 1\\) and\n   \\(m\\le k-1\\).  Thus every point of \\(R_{\\,n-k}\\) satisfies\n   \\[\n   a-b = m\\quad\\text{with}\\quad 0\\le m\\le k-1,\\qquad 1\\le b\\le k-m .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sunny covering of the triangle.**  \n   For each integer \\(m\\) with \\(0\\le m\\le k-1\\) set \\(r=m+1\\) and consider the\n   sunny line \\(S_{r}\\) from Lemma\u202f2, i.e.\n   \\[\n   S_{r}:\\; y = \\frac{r}{r+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{r+1}.\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n   By Lemma\u202f2, \\(S_{r}\\) contains exactly the lattice points satisfying\n   \\[\n   a-b = r-1 = m,\\qquad 1\\le b\\le r = m+1 .\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n   Condition (12) is precisely the description (10) of those points of\n   \\(R_{\\,n-k}\\) whose difference \\(a-b\\) equals \\(m\\).  Consequently, the\n   collection\n   \\[\n   \\{S_{1},S_{2},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n   covers **all** points of \\(R_{\\,n-k}\\).\n\n4. **Final family.**  \n   Define\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{L}_{k}= \\{L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n-k+1}\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                    \\{S_{1},S_{2},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}.\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n   The set \\(\\mathcal{L}_{k}\\) contains \\( (n-k)+k = n\\) lines.\n   The first block consists of non\u2011sunny lines, the second block consists of\n   sunny lines (Lemma\u202f2(i)), and the two blocks are disjoint because the slopes\n   are different (\\(-1\\) versus \\(\\frac{r}{r+1}\\)).  \n   By steps\u202f1\u20133 the union of the lines in \\(\\mathcal{L}_{k}\\) contains every\n   point of \\(S_{n}\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal{L}_{k}\\) satisfies both conditions of the\n   problem and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\),\nthe set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
