egative; in that case we simply set \\(r=0\\) and will add extra non\u2011sunny lines later; the argument below still works because we never need to pick a negative number of points.)\u201d\n\n*Issue.*  \nWhen \\(k<3\\) the construction **sets \\(r=0\\)**, i.e. it creates **no sunny lines**. Yet the problem requires **exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines**. For \\(k=1\\) or \\(k=2\\) the construction therefore fails to meet the specification. The subsequent claim that the final number of sunny lines equals \\(k\\) relies on the identity \\(r=k\\), which is false when \\(k<3\\).\n\n*Classification:* **Critical Error** \u2013 the construction does not produce the required number of sunny lines for \\(k=1,2\\).\n\n> \u201cWhen \\(r\\ge 0\\) pick \\(r\\) distinct points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_r\\) from \\(I_n\\).  \n> This is possible because \\(|I_n|\\ge r\\) by (8) and the fact that \\(r\\le n-3\\le |I_n|\\) for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\u201d\n\n*Correct* for the case \\(r\\ge0\\) (i.e. \\(k\\ge3\\)). The inequality \\(|I_n|\\ge n-3\\) holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\) (see Lemma\u202f2).\n\n> \u201cFor each \\(P_i\\) choose a rational slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) satisfying  \n> \\[\n> \\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n> \\tag{11}\n> \\]  \n> The line  \n> \\[\n> S_i:\\; y-\\;b_i = \\alpha_i\\,(x-a_i) \\qquad\\text{if }P_i=(a_i,b_i)\n> \\tag{12}\n> \\]  \n> passes through \\(P_i\\) and is sunny.  \n> Because the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(S_i\\) are pairwise distinct.  \n> Insert all the \\(S_i\\) into \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n> At this stage \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\(3+r = n-s\\) lines, of which exactly \\(r\\) are sunny.\u201d\n\n*Correct* for \\(k\\ge3\\). Rational slopes distinct from the forbidden three exist in abundance, and each line is indeed sunny.\n\n#### 5.3. Adding the remaining non\u2011sunny lines\n\n> \u201c3. **Add the remaining non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n> We still need \\(s\\) further lines, all of which must be non\u2011sunny.