s provided.\n\n*Classification:* **Justification Gap** \u2013 the covering argument is incomplete.\n\n#### 5.4. Counting sunny lines\n\n> \u201c4. **Count sunny lines.**  \n> By construction the number of sunny lines equals  \n> \\[\n> r = n-3-s = n-3-(n-k)=k .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Issue.*  \nWhen \\(k<3\\) we have set \\(r=0\\) (see step\u202f2). Substituting \\(r=0\\) into the formula gives \\(0=k\\), which is false for \\(k=1,2\\). Hence the equality \\(r=k\\) does **not** hold in those cases, and the construction does not yield the required number of sunny lines.\n\n*Classification:* **Critical Error** \u2013 the counting step relies on an incorrect handling of the case \\(k<3\\).\n\n#### 5.5. Verifying the covering property\n\n> \u201c5. **Covering property.**  \n> * The three lines (6) cover every boundary point of \\(T_n\\) (Lemma\u202f1).  \n> * Each interior point either was selected as one of the \\(P_i\\) (hence lies on a sunny line) or lies on one of the extra non\u2011sunny lines added in step\u202f3 (by the choice of those lines).  \n> Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\u201d\n\n*Analysis.*  \n- The first bullet is correct.  \n- The second bullet repeats the insufficient justification identified in step\u202f5.3: it assumes that the extra non\u2011sunny lines indeed cover **all** remaining interior points, which has not been proved. Consequently the covering property is not established rigorously.\n\n*Classification:* **Justification Gap** \u2013 the argument that all interior points are covered is not substantiated.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Theorem (final answer)\n\n> \u201cFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Analysis.*  \nThe theorem\u2019s conclusion depends on Lemma\u202f3 being valid for **every** \\(k\\) in the stated range. Because Lemma\u202f3 fails for \\(k=1,2\\) (critical error) and contains gaps in