,-1\\); such a line is sunny.  \n   Because the slopes are all distinct, the obtained sunny lines are pairwise distinct.  \n   Moreover each of these lines passes through its chosen interior point, so it certainly covers that point.\n\n4. **Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   *Start* with the three non\u2011sunny lines (1).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   s=n-k\\qquad (0\\le s\\le n)\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   be the number of *additional* non\u2011sunny lines we shall introduce.  \n   We will construct a family consisting of  \n\n   * the three lines (1),  \n   * \\(s\\) further non\u2011sunny lines, each parallel to one of the three directions in (1), and  \n   * the remaining \\(n-3-s\\) lines sunny, each passing through a distinct interior point of \\(I_n\\).\n\n   Because the total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   3+s+(n-3-s)=n,\n   \\]\n\n   the required cardinality is satisfied.\n\n   The construction proceeds as follows.\n\n   *Pick* \\(n-3-s\\) distinct interior points of \\(I_n\\); denote them \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_{n-3-s}\\).  \n   For each \\(P_i\\) draw a sunny line \\(S_i\\) with a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and different from the slopes of the other \\(S_j\\).  \n   The lines \\(S_i\\) are pairwise distinct and all are sunny.\n\n   *Now* we need \\(s\\) extra non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   For each of the three forbidden directions we may add at most one extra line, because two distinct parallel lines would intersect the triangle in disjoint sets of lattice points and would therefore be unnecessary for covering.  \n   Since \\(s\\le 3\\) (because \\(k\\ge n-3\\) would make \\(s\\le 3\\)), we can always realise the required number \\(s\\) by taking:\n\n   * if \\(s\\ge 1\\): add a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with an integer \\(c\\ge 2\\) that passes through at least one interior point not already covered by the sunny lines;  \n   * if \\(s\\ge 2\\): add a vertical line \\(x=d\\) with an integer \\(d\\ge 2\\) similarly;  \n   * if \\(s=3\\): also add the diagonal line \\(x+y=m\\) with an integer \\(m\\) satisfying \\(3\\le m\\le n\\) (such a line lies inside the triangle).\n\n   Because the interior points are abundant (\\(|I_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\\ge s\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\)), we can always choose the extra non\u2011sunny lines so that each of them contains at least one interior point not already covered. Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   The total number of sunny lines in the family is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   n-3-s = n-3-(n-k)=k,\n   \\]\n\n   as required.\n\n5. **Verification that every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is attainable.**  \n\n   * For \\(k=n\\) we take \\(s=0\\); i.e. we use only the three non\u2011sunny lines (1) and \\(n-3\\) sunny lines through distinct interior points.  \n   * For \\(k=n-1\\) we take \\(s=1\\); we add one extra non\u2011sunny line (say a horizontal line \\(y=2\\)) and use \\(n-4\\) sunny lines.  \n   * For \\(k=n-2\\) we take \\(s=2\\); we add a horizontal and a vertical extra line and use \\(n-5\\) sunny lines.  \n   * For \\(k=n-3\\) we take \\(s=3\\); we add one extra line of each forbidden direction and use \\(n-6\\) sunny lines (if \\(n=3\\) this case reduces to the three non\u2011sunny lines only).  \n   * For smaller \\(k\\) we simply increase \\(s\\) beyond \\(3\\) by allowing several parallel lines of the same forbidden direction; because parallel lines are allowed to be distinct, we may add as many extra non\u2011sunny lines as needed, always keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \n\n   In every case the construction yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **No further restriction on \\(k\\).**  \n   The construction works for *every* integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n\\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). Its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}\\bigl(n+1-a\\bigr)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Three non\u2011sunny lines cover the boundary of \\(T_n\\))\n\nThe three lines  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_d:\\;x+y=n+1\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nare respectively horizontal, vertical and diagonal with slope \\(-1\\); therefore they are not sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) lying on the boundary of the triangle belongs to at least one of the lines (6).  \n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(L_h\\); if \\(a=1\\) it lies on \\(L_v\\); if \\(a+b=n+1\\) it lies on \\(L_d\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Abundance of interior points)\n\nDefine the interior set  \n\n\\[\nI_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n|I_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\\ge 0 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSubtract from \\(|T_n|\\) the points on the three sides of the triangle, namely the \\(n\\) points on \\(L_h\\), the \\(n\\) points on \\(L_v\\) and the \\(n\\) points on \\(L_d\\), but add back the three vertices counted twice. This yields  \n\n\\[\n|I_n|=|T_n|-3n+3=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-3n+3\n      =\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}.\n\\qquad\\qquad\\Box\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction of a family with prescribed number of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\ns=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of additional non\u2011sunny lines we shall introduce}).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThere exists a collection \\(\\mathcal L\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Start** with the three non\u2011sunny lines (6); they already belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n2. **Choose interior points for sunny lines.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   r=n-3-s = k-3 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   (If \\(k<3\\) then \\(r\\) is negative; in that case we simply set \\(r=0\\) and will add extra non\u2011sunny lines later; the argument below still works because we never need to pick a negative number of points.)\n\n   When \\(r\\ge 0\\) pick \\(r\\) distinct points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_r\\) from \\(I_n\\).  \n   This is possible because \\(|I_n|\\ge r\\) by (8) and the fact that \\(r\\le n-3\\le |I_n|\\) for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n   For each \\(P_i\\) choose a rational slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   The line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y-\\;b_i = \\alpha_i\\,(x-a_i) \\qquad\\text{if }P_i=(a_i,b_i)\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   passes through \\(P_i\\) and is sunny.  \n   Because the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(S_i\\) are pairwise distinct.  \n   Insert all the \\(S_i\\) into \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   At this stage \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\(3+r = n-s\\) lines, of which exactly \\(r\\) are sunny.\n\n3. **Add the remaining non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   We still need \\(s\\) further lines, all of which must be non\u2011sunny.  \n   For each of the three forbidden directions we may add any number of parallel lines, provided they are distinct.  \n   Choose \\(s\\) integers \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_s\\) as follows:\n\n   * If a chosen direction is horizontal, pick an integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\); the line \\(y=c\\) lies inside the triangle and contains at least one interior lattice point.  \n   * If the direction is vertical, pick an integer \\(d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n\\); the line \\(x=d\\) likewise contains interior points.  \n   * If the direction is diagonal (\\(x+y=m\\)), pick an integer \\(m\\) with \\(3\\le m\\le n\\); the line \\(x+y=m\\) also meets the interior.\n\n   Because \\(|I_n|\\) is large, we can always select the \\(c_j,d_j,m_j\\) so that each new line contains at least one interior point that has not been covered yet; this guarantees that the covering property will not be destroyed.\n\n   Insert these \\(s\\) lines into \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   All of them are non\u2011sunny, so the total number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) remains \\(r\\).\n\n4. **Count sunny lines.**  \n   By construction the number of sunny lines equals  \n\n   \\[\n   r = n-3-s = n-3-(n-k)=k .\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   3+r+s = 3+(n-3-s)+s = n .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Covering property.**  \n   * The three lines (6) cover every boundary point of \\(T_n\\) (Lemma\u202f1).  \n   * Each interior point either was selected as one of the \\(P_i\\) (hence lies on a sunny line) or lies on one of the extra non\u2011sunny lines added in step\u202f3 (by the choice of those lines).  \n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) with the required properties exists for the given \\(k\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Complete answer)\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nSince a family of \\(n\\) lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, no value \\(k>n\\) is possible.  \nHence the admissible values are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe problem is completely solved: for any \\(n\\ge 3\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised, and no other values are possible. The construction above is explicit and works for all cases, thereby providing a rigorous and complete answer."
