h \\(L_d\\) when \\(s=n-1\\)), contains **exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines**, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\n5. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n\n   *Boundary points* are on \\(L_h\\), \\(L_v\\) or \\(L_d\\) (Lemma\u202f1).  \n\n   *Interior points* fall into three disjoint classes:\n\n   * (i) points equal to one of the chosen \\(P_i\\) \u2013 they lie on the sunny line \\(S_i\\);\n   * (ii) points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) but different from any \\(P_i\\) \u2013 they lie on the diagonal line \\(L_d\\);\n   * (iii) the remaining interior points \u2013 by construction they have a \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=c\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal N'\\); hence they are covered.\n\n   Thus **every** point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n6. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   The only sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\); all other lines are among \\(L_h,L_v,L_d\\) or the horizontal lines of \\(\\mathcal N'\\), each of which has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \n   Hence the number of sunny lines equals \\(k\\), as required.\n\nSince the construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), these are precisely the admissible values.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Boundary covering)\n\nThe three lines  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_d:\\;x+y=n+1\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nare respectively horizontal, vertical and diagonal of slope \\(-1\\); therefore they are **not** sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) that lies on the boundary of the triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) belongs to at least one of the line