use they contain different points \\(P_c\\).\n\n4. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).*  \n\n   *Step\u202f4.1 \u2013 choose the sunny lines.*  \n   Take the first \\(k\\) integers among \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); denote this set by  \n\n   \\[\n   C_k=\\{\\,c_1,c_2,\\dots ,c_k\\,\\}\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]  \n\n   (When \\(k=n-1\\) we take all numbers \\(2,\\dots ,n-1\\); when \\(k=n\\) we also add \\(c=1\\) defined below.)  \n   For each \\(c\\in C_k\\) include the sunny line \\(S_c\\) of (3) in our family.\n\n   *Step\u202f4.2 \u2013 the remaining non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Start from the template \\(\\mathcal B_n\\) (1).  \n   For every \\(c\\in C_k\\) **remove** the horizontal line \\(y=c\\) from \\(\\mathcal B_n\\) (it is replaced by the sunny line \\(S_c\\)).  \n   After these removals we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl|\\mathcal B_n\\setminus\\{y=c\\mid c\\in C_k\\}\\bigr|=(n-1)-k+1=n-k .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining lines are all non\u2011sunny (horizontal lines with \\(c\\notin C_k\\) and the vertical line \\(x=1\\)).\n\n   *Step\u202f4.3 \u2013 the case \\(k=n\\).*  \n   When \\(k=n\\) we cannot remove more than the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines, so we also replace the vertical line \\(x=1\\) by a sunny line through the boundary point \\((1,1)\\):  \n\n   \\[\n   S_1:\\;y-1=\\beta(x-1),\\qquad \\beta\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]  \n\n   Thus for \\(k=n\\) the family consists of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f4.4 \u2013 the final family.*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\bigl\\{S_c\\mid c\\in C_k\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl(\\mathcal B_n\\setminus\\{y=c\\mid c\\in C_k\\}\\bigr)\n   \\quad\\text{if }k\\le n-1,\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]  \n\n   and  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_n=\\{S_c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]  \n\n   By construction \\(|\\mathcal L_k