hcal L\\cup\\{\\ell\\}\\) covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le m+2\\).*  \n  The proof of the lemma uses the fact that the new points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=m+2\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=m+2\\); by taking a line of the required slope that passes through any one of these points we guarantee that the whole new diagonal is covered, while the previously covered points remain covered because the old lines are left unchanged.\n\nUsing the lemma we can increase the number of lines from \\(n\\) to \\(n+1\\) while controlling the number of sunny lines, thereby establishing the inductive step for all \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the integer lattice points  \n\\[\nP_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\nFor a given \\(n\\ge 3\\) we must exhibit, for each admissible \\(k\\), a set \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(P_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) are *sunny* (i.e. their slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma (Cover\u2011Extension Lemma)\n\n*Let \\(m\\ge 3\\) and let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of \\(m\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(P_{m}\\).  \nLet \\(\\sigma\\in\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) be any prescribed slope (the value \\(\\sigma=\\infty\\) denotes a vertical line).  \nThen there exists a line \\(\\ell\\) of slope \\(\\sigma\\) such that \\(\\mathcal L\\cup\\{\\ell\\}\\) covers all points of \\(P_{m+1}\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe new points that appear in \\(P_{m+1}\\) but not in \\(P_{m}\\) are precisely those with \\(a+b=m+2\\); they lie on the line\n\\[\nd_{m+1}:\\;x+y=m+2 .\n\\]\nChoose any lattice point \\((a_{0},b_{0})\\) on \\(d_{m+1}\\) (for instance \\((1,m+1)\\)).  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the unique line of slope \\(\\sigma\\) passing through \\((a_{0},b_{0})\\).  \nBy construction \\(\\ell\\) contains \\((a_{0},b_{0})\\) and therefore meets the new diagonal \\(d_{m+1}\\).  \nAll points of \\(P_{m}\\) remain covered because the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are unchanged.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L\\cup\\{\\ell\\}\\) covers \\(P_{m+1}\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(P_{3}\\) consists of the points\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\n*Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the three vertical lines\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad x=2,\\qquad x=3 .\n\\]\nEach of them is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nThe points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) lie on \\(x=1\\); the point \\((2,1)\\) lies on \\(x=2\\).  All three points of \\(P_{3}\\) are covered, and exactly \\(0\\) lines are sunny.\n\n*Construction for \\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the lines\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}: y=1\\quad(\\text{horizontal, non\u2011sunny}),\\qquad\n\\ell_{2}: x=1\\quad(\\text{vertical, non\u2011sunny}),\\qquad\n\\ell_{3}: y=2x-1\\quad(\\text{slope }2,\\ \\text{sunny}).\n\\]\nThe point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}\\); \\((1,2)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{3}\\); \\((2,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{1}\\).  Thus all points are covered and exactly one line (\\(\\ell_{3}\\)) is sunny.\n\nHence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\) and for \\(k=0,1\\) (note that \\(n-2=1\\) when \\(n=3\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive step\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have already constructed a family\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}=\\{\\ell^{(n)}_{1},\\dots ,\\ell^{(n)}_{n}\\}\n\\]\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition for \\(P_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe shall produce, for each integer \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le (n+1)-2=n-1\\), a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k'}\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(P_{n+1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet \\(k'\\) be given.\n\n*Case 1: \\(k'\\le n-2\\).*  \nTake the already constructed \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k'}\\).  \nApply the Cover\u2011Extension Lemma with the prescribed slope **non\u2011sunny** (choose, for instance, slope \\(0\\)).  \nThus we obtain a new line \\(\\ell\\) parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nSet\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k'}:=\\mathcal L_{n,k'}\\cup\\{\\ell\\}.\n\\]\nThe number of sunny lines is unchanged (still \\(k'\\)), the total number of lines is \\(n+1\\), and by the lemma the new family covers all points of \\(P_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(k'=n-1\\).*  \nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n-2}\\), which contains \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nApply the Cover\u2011Extension Lemma with a **sunny** slope (any real number different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \nDenote the obtained line by \\(\\ell_{\\text{sunny}}\\).  \nNow we have a family of \\(n+1\\) lines containing \\(n-1\\) sunny lines:\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,n-1}:=\\mathcal L_{n,n-2}\\cup\\{\\ell_{\\text{sunny}}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le n-1\\) we have produced a suitable family for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion of the induction\n\nThe base case supplies the families for \\(n=3\\) and for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le 1=n-2\\).  \nThe inductive step shows that if the statement holds for a given \\(n\\) then it also holds for \\(n+1\\).  \nConsequently, by mathematical induction, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2\n\\]\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Remarks on the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction above never yields families with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).  \nThe present method does not settle whether such families exist; the problem of deciding the admissibility of the two largest values of \\(k\\) remains open. \u220e"
