"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution of the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Construction (existence).**  \n   Using mathematical induction on \\(n\\) we build, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that  \n   * covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);  \n   * contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   The induction step relies on the **Cover\u2011Extension Lemma**: from a family covering the points up to diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) we can add one more line of any prescribed admissible slope (sunny or non\u2011sunny) and obtain a family that covers the points up to diagonal \\(x+y=n+2\\).  \n   Starting from the explicit constructions for \\(n=3\\) (which realise \\(k=0,1\\)), the lemma allows us to increase \\(n\\) while preserving the number of sunny lines or increasing it by one, thereby producing all required values of \\(k\\).\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   We prove that any family of \\(n\\) lines satisfying the covering condition must contain **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   The argument is combinatorial: the points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lie on the extreme diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   If a line is sunny, its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently a sunny line can intersect the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) in at most one lattice point.  \n   Since there are \\(n-1\\) lattice points on that diagonal, covering them with at most \\(n\\) lines forces at least two of the lines to be non\u2011sunny (they can each contain many of those points because they are parallel to the axes or to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Hence the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-2\\).\n\nCombining the constructive part and the impossibility result yields the exact set of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Notation and the covering set\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_{n}:=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\;\\Big|\\;a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition of the problem is that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in P_{n}\\; \\exists \\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Cover\u2011Extension Lemma\n\n> **Lemma 1 (Cover\u2011Extension Lemma).**  \n> Let \\(m\\ge 3\\) and let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of \\(m\\) distinct lines satisfying (1) for the set \\(P_{m}\\).  \n> Let \\(\\sigma\\in\\mathbb R\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\\) be any prescribed slope (sunny or non\u2011sunny).  \n> Then there exists a line \\(\\ell\\) of slope \\(\\sigma\\) such that \\(\\mathcal L\\cup\\{\\ell\\}\\) satisfies (1) for \\(P_{m+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe new points that appear in \\(P_{m+1}\\) but not in \\(P_{m}\\) are exactly those with \\(a+b=m+2\\); they lie on the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nd_{m+1}:\\;x+y=m+2 .\n\\]\n\nPick any lattice point \\((a_{0},b_{0})\\) on \\(d_{m+1}\\) (for instance \\((1,m+1)\\)).  \nThere is a unique line \\(\\ell\\) of slope \\(\\sigma\\) passing through \\((a_{0},b_{0})\\).  \nBy construction \\(\\ell\\) contains a point of the new diagonal, hence all points of \\(P_{m+1}\\) are covered: the old points are still covered by the unchanged lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\), and the new diagonal is covered because \\(\\ell\\) meets it. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(P_{3}\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\n*Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the three vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad x=2,\\qquad x=3 .\n\\]\n\nAll are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n\\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) lie on \\(x=1\\); \\((2,1)\\) lies on \\(x=2\\).  \nThus (1) holds and exactly \\(0\\) lines are sunny.\n\n*Construction for \\(k=1\\).*  \nConsider  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}: y=1\\quad(\\text{horizontal, non\u2011sunny}),\\qquad\n\\ell_{2}: x=1\\quad(\\text{vertical, non\u2011sunny}),\\qquad\n\\ell_{3}: y=2x-1\\quad(\\text{slope }2,\\ \\text{sunny}).\n\\]\n\n\\((1,1)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}\\); \\((1,2)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_{3}\\); \\((2,1)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_{1}\\).  \nHence (1) holds and exactly one line (\\(\\ell_{3}\\)) is sunny.\n\nSince \\(n-2=1\\) when \\(n=3\\), the base case establishes the statement for all admissible \\(k\\) when \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Inductive construction\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2\n\\]\n\nwe have already constructed a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}=\\{\\ell^{(n)}_{1},\\dots ,\\ell^{(n)}_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (1) for \\(P_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe shall produce, for the integer \\(n+1\\), families \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k'}\\) for all  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k'\\le (n+1)-2=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.4.1  The case \\(0\\le k'\\le n-2\\)\n\nTake the already existing family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k'}\\).  \nApply Lemma\u202f1 with a **non\u2011sunny** prescribed slope,