antee that the added line \\(\\ell\\) will cover the new points of \\(P_{n+1}\\).  \n\n**Conclusion:** The construction is unsupported; this is a **Critical Error**.\n\n#### 2.4.2  The case \\(k'=n-1\\)\n> \u201cStart from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n-2}\\)\u2026 Apply Lemma\u202f1 with a sunny prescribed slope \u2026\u201d\n\n**Analysis.**  \n- Again the argument relies on the false Lemma\u202f1, so the claimed family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,n-1}\\) may not satisfy the covering condition.  \n\n**Conclusion:** Another **Critical Error**.\n\n### 2.5  Upper bound: at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines are possible\n\n#### Claim \u201cA sunny line contains at most one point of \\(Q_{n}\\)\u201d\n- The proof uses the fact that any two points of \\(Q_{n}\\) have slope \\(-1\\). This reasoning is correct.  \n\n**Verdict:** Correct.\n\n#### Claim \u201cConsequently each sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(Q_{n}\\). Since \\(|Q_{n}|=n-1\\), covering all points of \\(Q_{n}\\) with \\(n\\) lines forces **at least two** of the lines to be non\u2011sunny \u2026\u201d\n\n**Analysis.**  \n- The argument assumes that a non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(Q_{n}\\) unless it coincides with the diagonal. However, the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) itself is a non\u2011sunny line (slope \\(-1\\)) that **covers all** points of \\(Q_{n}\\).  \n- Therefore it is possible to have exactly **one** non\u2011sunny line (the diagonal) and \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, still covering all points of \\(Q_{n}\\).  \n- The subsequent counting \u201c\\(1\\cdot (n-1) + 1\\cdot (n-2) = 2n-3 < n-1\\)\u201d is mathematically incorrect: \\(2n-3\\) is larger than \\(n-1\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\). The inequality sign is reversed, and the whole counting argument misrepresents how many points can be covered.  \n\n**Conclusion:** The deduction that at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny is false; this constitutes a **Critical Error** in the upper\u2011bound argument.\n\n### 2.6  Final statement\n\n> \u201c