es  \n\n\\[\n   L_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + i ,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n,\n\\]\n\nare distinct, sunny, and together contain all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach line \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\). Since \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\neq 0\\) and\n\\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\neq -1\\) for all \\(i\\ge 1\\), and the slope is finite, every\n\\(L_i\\) is sunny. Distinctness follows from the different slopes (or\nintercepts).\n\nFix \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Consider the function  \n\n\\[\n   f(i)=\\frac{b-i}{a} - \\frac{i}{i+1},\\qquad i\\in[1,n]\\cap\\mathbb R .\n\\]\n\nWe have  \n\n\\[\n   f(1)=\\frac{b-1}{a}-\\frac12,\\qquad \n   f(n)=\\frac{b-n}{a}-\\frac{n}{n+1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), one checks that  \n\n\\[\n   f(1)\\ge 0\\quad\\text{and}\\quad f(n)\\le 0 .\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(f\\) is strictly decreasing in \\(i\\) (its derivative is\n\\(-\\frac1a-\\frac1{(i+1)^2}<0\\)). Hence there exists a unique integer\n\\(i_0\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that \\(f(i_0)\\ge 0\\) and \\(f(i_0+1)\\le 0\\)\n(if \\(i_0=n\\) the second inequality is vacuous). The equality\n\\(f(i_0)=0\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n   b = \\frac{i_0}{i_0+1}\\,a + i_0 ,\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{i_0}\\). Therefore each point of\n\\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemma\u202f1 provides a configuration with **\\(k=0\\)** sunny lines, while Lemma\u202f2\nprovides a configuration with **\\(k=n\\)** sunny lines. Both configurations\nsatisfy the covering requirement for the set \\(T_n\\) and use exactly \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines.\n\nHence for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values  \n\n\\[\n   k=0\\quad\\text{and}\\quad k=n\n\\]\n\nare admissible. \u220e"
