"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution. I have rigorously proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis admissible; i.e. there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, that covers all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe problem asks for the possible numbers \\(k\\) of \u201csunny\u2019\u2019 lines among a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\). Consequently a *non\u2011sunny* line must be horizontal, vertical, or have slope \\(-1\\).\n\nThe proof consists of a single explicit construction:\n\n1. **Construction with \\(k=0\\).**  \n   - Take the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   - Take the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   - For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) take the line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n   These \\(n\\) lines are pairwise distinct and each is non\u2011sunny.  \n   By a case analysis on a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) we show that it lies on at least one of the chosen lines:\n   * if \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(x=1\\);\n   * if \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(y=1\\);\n   * otherwise \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\), whence \\(c:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) and the point lies on \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nThus the required covering is achieved with zero sunny lines.\n\nThe method demonstrates that the extreme value \\(k=0\\) is always attainable. Determining whether other values of \\(k\\) (in particular \\(k=n\\) or intermediate values) can occur remains open.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* \\(L_1:\\; x=1\\) (vertical);\n* \\(L_2:\\; y=1\\) (horizontal);\n* For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) let  \n  \\[\n     L_{c+1}:\\; x+y=c .\n  \\]\n\nAll these lines are distinct:\n* \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) have different directions (vertical vs. horizontal);\n* each line \\(x+y=c\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and a different intercept, so they are distinct from each other and from \\(L_1,L_2\\).\n\nMoreover none of them is sunny, because their slopes are respectively\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical), \\(0\\) (horizontal) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification that the lines cover \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x=1\\), so it lies on \\(L_1\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\neq1\\) but \\(b=1\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(y=1\\), hence it lies on \\(L_2\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\neq1\\) and \\(b\\neq1\\).*  \nSince \\(a,b\\ge1\\) this forces \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\).  \nDefine \\(c:=a+b\\). Because \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and\n\\(a,b\\ge2\\) gives \\(c\\ge4\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n2\\le c=a+b\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(c\\) is an integer in the range \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\), and the point\n\\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x+y=c\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line\n\\(L_{c+1}\\).\n\nIn every possible case the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the\n\\(n\\) constructed lines. Therefore the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers\nall points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for an arbitrary integer \\(n\\ge3\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct\nnon\u2011sunny lines that together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence the number of sunny lines can be\n\\(k=0\\).\n\nThus **\\(k=0\\) is always an admissible value** for the problem. \u220e"
