 called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.  \nThus a non\u2011sunny line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal: }y=c,\\qquad\n\\text{vertical: }x=c,\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal: }x+y=c .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (necessary number of non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nEach line of type (1) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nConsequently, if a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_{n}\\) and contains \\(s\\) sunny lines, then the number \\(t=n-s\\) of non\u2011sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|T_{n}|}{n}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(T_{n}\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such points.  \nThe same bound holds for vertical and diagonal lines by symmetry.  \nSince the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines together can cover at most \\(tn\\) points, we need \\(tn\\ge |T_{n}|\\), which yields (2). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (coverage capacity of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(R\\) be any subset of rows \\(\\{y=b\\}\\), columns \\(\\{x=a\\}\\) and diagonals \\(\\{x+y=c\\}\\) that still contain uncovered points after the non\u2011sunny lines have been placed.  \nIf \\(|R|=r\\), then three sunny lines can cover at most \\(3r\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) lying in those rows, columns or diagonals.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three families (1); therefore it meets each member of \\(R\\) in at most one point.  \nWith three sunny lines the total number of points that can be hit inside the union of the members of \\(R\\) is at most \\(3r\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (lower bound on the number of still uncovered points)\n\nAssume a covering family contains exactly three sunny lines.  \nLet \\(t=n-3\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(t\\ge\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil-3\\).  \nConsequently the number \\(U\\) of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that are **not** covered by the non\u2011sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nU\\ge |T_{n}|-tn\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\bigl(\\lceil\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\rceil-3\\bigr)n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points, so the total number covered by the \\(t\\) such lines is at most \\(tn\\).  \nSubtracting from \\(|T_{n}|\\) yields (3). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Contradiction\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil-3 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(r\\) be the number of rows, columns and diagonals that contain at least one of the \\(U\\) uncovered points.  \nEvery uncovered point lies on exactly one row, one column and one diagonal, hence  \n\n\\[\nr\\le U .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 the three sunny lines can cover at most \\(3r\\) of those points, while by Lemma\u202f3 at least \\(U\\) points remain to be covered.  \nThus we must have  \n\n\\[\n3r\\ge U .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5) and (6) gives \\(3U\\ge U\\), i.e. \\(U\\le 0\\).  \nBut (3) shows that for every \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\nU\\ge\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n^{2}}{2}-\\frac{3n}{2}+3>0, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[4pt]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n^{2}}{2}-\\frac{3n}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}>0, & n\\text{ odd},\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\na direct calculation.  \nHence \\(U>0\\), contradicting (6).  \n\nTherefore a covering family of \\(n\\) lines cannot contain three or more sunny lines.  \nConsequently any feasible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\nIt remains to show that the three values \\(k=0,1,2\\) are indeed attainable.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Use only non\u2011sunny lines: take the \\(\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) and the remaining \\(n-\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots\\).  \n  One checks that every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on either its row\u2011line or its column\u2011line, and all lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Start from the construction for \\(k=0\\) and replace one vertical line \\(x=c\\) by the sunny line \\(y = -x + (c+1)\\).  \n  This new line still passes through all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c+1\\) that were previously covered by the removed vertical line, while the other vertical lines continue to cover the remaining points.  \n  Hence the covering uses exactly one sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \n  Begin with the \\(k=0\\) construction again.  \n  Replace two vertical lines \\(x=c_{1},x=c_{2}\\) (with \\(c_{1}\\neq c_{2}\\)) by the two sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=-x+(c_{1}+1),\\qquad y=-x+(c_{2}+1).\n  \\]\n\n  As before each of these sunny lines covers precisely the points of the diagonal \\(x+y=c_{i}+1\\) that were formerly covered by the corresponding vertical line, and the rest of the covering is unchanged.  \n  Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly two sunny lines.\n\nAll three constructions use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines and satisfy the required covering condition.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nWe have proved that for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) can contain **at most two** sunny lines, and that the numbers \\(k=0,1,2\\) are indeed realizable.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is contained in \\(\\{0,1,2\\}\\) and actually equals this set. \u220e"
