ts \\(m=n-3\\) and\n   \\(m=n\\).  Hence (2) holds **iff**  \n\n   \\[\n   n-3\\le m\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(m=n-k\\), this is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le 3 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus no configuration with \\(k\\ge 4\\) sunny lines can exist.\n\n5. **Construction for the admissible values of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\).  Every required point\n   \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\); none of these lines is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) (they cover all points with\n   \\(a\\ge 2\\)).  The remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\); they are\n   collinear on the line \\(y=x+0\\) which has slope \\(1\\) and is sunny.  Together\n   we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of them sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  \n   Take the vertical lines \\(x=3,\\dots ,x=n\\) (covering all points with\n   \\(a\\ge 3\\)).  The points with \\(a=1,2\\) form two arithmetic progressions:\n   \\((1,b)\\) and \\((2,b)\\) for \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le n-1\\) respectively.\n   They are covered by the two sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{1}:\\; y = x-1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n   \\ell_{2}:\\; y = x+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Both have slope \\(1\\) and are not parallel to any of the forbidden directions.\n   Hence we have \\(n\\) lines, exactly two of them sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Use the vertical lines \\(x=4,\\dots ,x=n\\) (covering all points with\n   \\(a\\ge 4\\)).  The remaining points are those with \\(a=1,2,3\\).  They are covered\n   by the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y = x-2,\\qquad y = x,\\qquad y = x+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Again each has slope \\(1\\) and is sunny.  Thus we obtain a family of \\(n\\)\n   lines with exactly three sunny ones.\n\n   In each case the lines are distinct and together contain every lattice point\n   \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   Therefore the values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are attainable, and by the pigeonhole\n   argument no larger \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nand we are asked to cover \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which\nexactly \\(k\\) are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis,\nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, it meets each vertical line\n\\(x=c\\) in at most one point; similarly it meets each horizontal line\n\\(y=c\\) and each line \\(x+y=c\\) in at most one point.  Consequently a sunny\nline can contain at most one point with a given \\(x\\)-coordinate, at most one\nwith a given \\(y\\)-coordinate and at most one with a given value of \\(x+y\\).\nIf three distinct points of \\(T_n\\) lay on the same sunny line, two of them\nwould share either the same \\(x\\), the same \\(y\\) or the same \\(x+y\\), a\ncontradiction.  Hence at most two points can lie on a sunny line. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nAmong all non\u2011sunny lines, the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_m=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers the largest possible number of points of \\(T_n\\) for a given number\n\\(m\\) of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  The line\n\\(x=i\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-i\\) points of \\(T_n\\); the line \\(y=j\\) contains\n\\(n+1-j\\) points, and the line \\(x+y=c\\) contains \\(c-1\\) points (for\n\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)).  The multiset \\(\\{n,n-1,\\dots ,1\\}\\) is the set of all these\nnumbers, and the \\(m\\) largest of them are precisely \\(n,n-1,\\dots ,n+1-m\\),\nwhich are attained by the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Pigeonhole bound)  \nIf a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines covers\n\\(T_n\\), then  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le 3 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\nBy Lemma\u202f2 the \\(m=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nC(m)=(n+1)m-\\frac{m(m+1)}2\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\npoints.  Hence\n\n\\[\n2k+C(m)\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(k=n-m\\) and using (6) we obtain\n\n\\[\n2(n-m)+(n+1)m-\\frac{m(m+1)}2\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) and simplifying yields\n\n\\[\nm^{2}-(2n-3)m+(n^{2}-3n)\\le 0 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe quadratic polynomial on the left has discriminant  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=(2n-3)^{2}-4(n^{2}-3n)=9,\n\\]\n\nso its roots are  \n\n\\[\nm_{1}=n-3,\\qquad m_{2}=n .\n\\]\n\nInequality (8) holds exactly for \\(n-3\\le m\\le n\\).  Because \\(m=n-k\\),\nthis is equivalent to \\(0\\le k\\le 3\\), proving (5). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\),\na family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering property\nand containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and therefore lies on the\nline \\(x=a\\).  None of these lines is sunny.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1=\\{x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\cup\\{\\ell\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell\\) is the line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \nThe vertical lines cover all points with \\(a\\ge 2\\).  The remaining points are\n\\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\); they all satisfy \\(y=x\\) after translating by\n\\((1,0)\\), i.e. they lie on \\(\\ell\\).  Thus \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and\ncontains exactly one sunny line.\n\n*Case \\(k=2\\).*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_2=\\{x=3,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\},\n\\]\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x-1,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;y=x+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe vertical lines cover all points with \\(a\\ge 3\\).  The points with\n\\(a=1\\) are \\((1,b),\\;1\\le b\\le n\\) and satisfy \\(y=x+0\\); they lie on\n\\(\\ell_2\\) after shifting by \\((0,1)\\).  The points with \\(a=2\\) are\n\\((2,b),\\;1\\le b\\le n-1\\) and satisfy \\(y=x-1\\); they lie on \\(\\ell_1\\).\nHence \\(\\mathcal L_2\\) covers all of \\(T_n\\) and has exactly two sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=3\\).*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_3=\\{x=4,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_{-2},\\ell_{0},\\ell_{2}\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{t}:\\;y=x+t\\qquad(t=-2,0,2).\n\\]\n\nThe vertical lines cover all points with \\(a\\ge 4\\).  For \\(a=1,2,3\\) we have\nthe three families  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\na=1 &: (1,b),\\;1\\le b\\le n \\quad\\text{lies on }\\ell_{2},\\\\\na=2 &: (2,b),\\;1\\le b\\le n-1 \\quad\\text{lies on }\\ell_{0},\\\\\na=3 &: (3,b),\\;1\\le b\\le n-2 \\quad\\text{lies on }\\ell_{-2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to one of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L_3\\),\nand exactly three of them are sunny.\n\nIn each construction the lines are distinct, there are precisely \\(n\\) of them,\nand the number of sunny lines equals the prescribed \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe pigeonhole argument forces \\(k\\le 3\\); the explicit families above show that\n\\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are all attainable.  Hence for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of\nadmissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,3\\}} .\n\\]"
