d the expressions (1)\u2013(2) and simplifying gives  \n\n   \\[\n   m^{2}-(2n-3)m+(n^{2}-3n)\\le0 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The quadratic on the left has discriminant \\(9\\) and roots \\(m=n-3\\) and\n   \\(m=n\\).  Hence (4) holds **iff**  \n\n   \\[\n   n-3\\le m\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(m=n-k\\), this is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le 3 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus no configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines can exist.\n\n5. **Construction for the admissible values of \\(k\\).**  \n   For each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) we exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the\n   covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{0}=\\{\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n   Every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\); none of these lines is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and one sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y = x-(n-1) .\n   \\]\n\n   The vertical lines cover all points except \\((n,1)\\); the line \\(\\ell\\) passes\n   through \\((n,1)\\) (and also through \\((n-1,n-2)\\), which is already covered).\n   Hence the set \\(\\mathcal L_{1}= \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\cup\\{\\ell\\}\\) works and\n   contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  \n   Use the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) (they miss the points\n   \\((n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\)).  Cover these three points with two sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{1}:\\; y = x-(n-2),\\qquad\n   \\ell_{2}:\\; y = x-(n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   \\(\\ell_{1}\\) contains \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,2)\\) (the latter is already covered\n   by the vertical line \\(x=n\\) which we do **not** use, so the point is new);\n   \\(\\ell_{2}\\) contains \\((n,1)\\).  All points of \\(T_n\\) are now covered and\n   exactly two lines are sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Take the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  The uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2,1),(n-2,2),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n,1).\n   \\]\n\n   They are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{1}:\\; y = x-(n-2),\\qquad\n   \\ell_{2}:\\; y = x-(n-1),\\qquad\n   \\ell_{3}:\\; y = x-n .\n   \\]\n\n   Each of these lines has slope \\(1\\) (hence is sunny) and together they\n   contain the four remaining points.  Thus \\(\\mathcal L_{3}\\) consists of\n   \\(n-3\\) vertical lines and the three sunny lines above, giving exactly three\n   sunny lines.\n\n   In every case the lines are distinct, there are exactly \\(n\\) of them, and\n   they cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nFrom the pigeonhole bound (5) and the explicit constructions we conclude that\nthe only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(0,1,2,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence it meets each line of \\(\\mathcal V\\) (resp. \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal D\\))\nin at most one point.  If three distinct points of \\(T_n\\) lay on the same\nsunny line, two of them would share either their \\(x\\)-coordinate, their\n\\(y\\)-coordinate or their sum \\(x+y\\), contradicting the definition of a\nsunny line.  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor a fixed integer \\(m\\ge1\\) the largest possible number of points of\n\\(T_n\\) that can be covered by \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nC(m)=(n+1)m-\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or of\nslope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  The numbers of points of \\(T_n\\) lying on such\nlines are respectively  \n\n\\[\nn+1-c,\\qquad n+1-c,\\qquad c-1\\qquad (1\\le c\\le n).\n\\]\n\nThese three multisets together equal \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) taken twice.\nChoosing the \\(m\\) largest elements of this multiset yields the maximum\npossible coverage, and those largest elements are \\(n,n-1,\\dots ,n+1-m\\).\nThey are realised by the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\).  Summing them\ngives the formula for \\(C(m)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Pigeonhole bound)  \nIf a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then \\(0\\le k\\le3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(m=n-k\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  By Lemma\u202f1 the sunny lines\ncover at most \\(2k\\) points, and by Lemma\u202f2 the non\u2011sunny lines cover at most\n\\(C(m)\\) points.  Since every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered,\n\n\\[\n2k+C(m)\\ge|T_n| .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) (formula (1)) and the expression for\n\\(C(m)\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n2(n-m)+(n+1)m-\\frac{m(m+1)}2\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) and simplifying leads to\n\n\\[\nm^{2}-(2n-3)m+(n^{2}-3n)\\le0 .\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant of the quadratic is \\(\\Delta=(2n-3)^{2}-4(n^{2}-3n)=9\\);\nits roots are \\(m=n-3\\) and \\(m=n\\).  Inequality (3.2) therefore holds\niff \\(n-3\\le m\\le n\\).  Because \\(m=n-k\\), this is equivalent to\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le3 .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny part.*  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(m\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  The points of \\(T_n\\) that are not\ncovered by (4.1) are exactly those with abscissa \\(a\\) satisfying\n\\(m+1\\le a\\le n\\); for such an \\(a\\) the admissible ordinates are\n\\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).  Consequently the set of uncovered points is\n\n\\[\nU_k=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid a=m+1,\\dots ,n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\},\n\\]\nand \\(|U_k|=\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{k}i=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nBecause of Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(U_k\\).\nFor \\(k\\le3\\) we have \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le2k\\); therefore the points of\n\\(U_k\\) can be partitioned into \\(k\\) pairs (with one singleton when\n\\(k=1\\)) and each pair can be placed on a distinct sunny line of slope \\(1\\)\n(which is never parallel to the forbidden directions).  Explicitly we may\nchoose the sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y = x-(m+j-1)\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(j=1\\) the line \\(\\ell_1\\) contains the point \\((n,1)\\); for\n\\(j=2\\) it contains \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,2)\\); for \\(j=3\\) it contains\n\\((n-2,1),(n-1,2),(n,3)\\), etc.  In every case all points of \\(U_k\\) lie on\nthe collection \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\), and the \\(\\ell_j\\) are pairwise\ndistinct and sunny.\n\n*Resulting family.*  \n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt consists of exactly \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, contains precisely \\(k\\)\nsunny lines, and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus for each \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemma\u202f3 shows that \\(k\\ge4\\) is impossible, while the construction above\ndemonstrates that \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are all attainable.  Hence the set of\nnon\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the problem admits a solution is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,3\\}} .\n\\]"
