\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Construction of the sunny lines.**  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct real numbers \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) such that \\(\\alpha_i\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) for all \\(i\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y-\\!i = \\alpha_i\\,(x-1)\n   \\]\n\n   be the line of slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) passing through the point \\((1,i)\\).  \n   Because the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(L_i\\) are distinct.  \n   Each \\(L_i\\) contains the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i=\\{(1,i),(2,i-1),\\dots ,(i,1)\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which consists of all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(i+1\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i\\supseteq\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Construction of the non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   For every integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\; x+y=c .\n   \\]\n\n   There are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (n+1)-(k+2)+1 = n-k\n   \\]\n\n   such lines, all of which have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore non\u2011sunny.  \n   By definition each point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) lies on \\(D_c\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigcup_{c=k+2}^{n+1} D_c = \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b\\ge k+2\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n   From (1) and (2) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i\\bigr)\\cup\\bigl(\\bigcup_{c=k+2}^{n+1} D_c\\bigr)=\\mathcal P_n .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) together with the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny diagonals \\(D_{k+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) form a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n\n   By construction precisely the first \\(k\\) lines are sunny, the remaining \\(n-k\\) are non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequences\n\n* Lemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is always possible.  \n* Lemma\u202f2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) is possible.  \n\nHence for any given \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n\\{0\\}\\cup\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe case \\(k=1\\) has not been settled by the arguments above; no construction with exactly one sunny line is known, and a proof that such a configuration cannot exist has not been obtained.  \n\nTherefore the problem is solved up to the single unresolved value \\(k=1\\). \u220e"
