\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition \u201cfor all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) is on at least one of the lines\u2019\u2019 is exactly the requirement that the family of lines covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff its direction vector is parallel to one of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v_1=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf v_2=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf v_3=(1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. the line is horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\).  \nAll other lines are **sunny**.\n\nThe three families of non\u2011sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=b\\mid b\\in\\mathbb Z\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=a\\mid a\\in\\mathbb Z\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb Z\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on exactly one line of each family:\n\\[\ny=b,\\;x=a,\\;x+y=a+b .\n\\]\n\nThe possible values of the sum \\(a+b\\) for points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are the integers  \n\n\\[\n2,3,\\dots ,n+1,\n\\]\nhence there are exactly \\(n\\) distinct \u201cdiagonal\u2019\u2019 sums.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2. Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nAll of them belong to \\(\\mathcal D\\) and therefore are non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in the above range, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(D_c\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contain **no** sunny line.  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3. Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n#### Sunny lines  \nChoose \\(k\\) distinct real numbers \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\neq0,\\;\\alpha_i\\neq\\infty,\\;\\alpha_i\\neq-1\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-i = \\alpha_i\\,(x-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. the line of slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) passing through the point \\((1,i)\\).  \nBecause the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct and all are sunny.\n\nThe set of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with sum \\(i+1\\) is  \n\n\\[\nS_i=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=i+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,i),(2,i-1),\\dots ,(i,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_i\\) satisfy the equation of \\(L_i\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i\\supseteq\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n#### Non\u2011sunny lines  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+2\\le c\\le n+1\\) take the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n+1)-(k+2)+1 = n-k\n\\]\n\nsuch lines; each belongs to \\(\\mathcal D\\) and is therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose sum equals \\(c\\) lies on \\(D_c\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{c=k+2}^{n+1} D_c = \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b\\ge k+2\\}.\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n#### Covering and counting  \nFrom (2.1) and (2.2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i\\Bigr)\\cup\n\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{c=k+2}^{n+1} D_c\\Bigr)=\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nThus the family consisting of the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\) and the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(D_c\\) covers all required points.  \nThe total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nTherefore for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4. Impossibility of \\(k=1\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing **exactly one** sunny line, say \\(L\\).  \nLet the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines be non\u2011sunny; each belongs to one of the families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\).\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nH=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nV=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nD=\\#\\{\\text{diagonal lines in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nClearly  \n\n\\[\nH+V+D = n-1. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2.4.1  \nA horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (or a vertical line \\(x=a\\)) contains points of at most **two** different sums \\(a+b\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nOn the line \\(y=b\\) the sum equals \\(a+b\\); as \\(a\\) varies over the positive integers, the sum increases by one each time \\(a\\) increases by one.  \nSince the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the line can intersect \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) only for those \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\).  \nConsequently the smallest possible sum on the line is \\(1+b\\) and the largest is \\((n+1-b)+b=n+1\\); the interval \\([1+b,n+1]\\) contains at most two integers belonging to the set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\). \u220e\n\nThus each horizontal or vertical line contributes at most two distinct sums, whereas each diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) contributes exactly one sum, namely \\(c\\).\n\nLet \\(S\\) be the set of sums that are covered by the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nUsing Lemma\u202f2.4.1 we obtain the bound  \n\n\\[\n|S|\\le D+2(H+V). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2.3) into (2.4):\n\n\\[\n|S|\\le D+2\\bigl((n-1)-D\\bigr)=2n-2-D\\le 2n-2-(n-1)=n-1.\n\\]\n\nHence the non\u2011sunny lines can cover **at most \\(n-1\\) different sums**.  \nRecall that the sums occurring in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are exactly the \\(n\\) integers  \n\n\\[\n\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore there exists at least one sum, call it \\(c_0\\), that is **not** represented among the non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nThe only line that could contain points with sum \\(c_0\\) is the unique sunny line \\(L\\).  \nBut by Lemma\u202f2.4.1 a single line can contain points of at most two different sums, so \\(L\\) can cover at most two such missing sums.  \nSince at least one sum is missing and the set of missing sums has cardinality at least \\(1\\), we would need \\(L\\) to cover **