\n     \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le (n+1)-2\\).\n\nThe induction yields the desired family of configurations for all \\(n\\ge 3\\) and all\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe integer points that have to lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three\ndirections \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) and \\((1,-1)\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base configurations)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the following families of three distinct lines satisfy the\nrequirements:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) (all non\u2011sunny);\n* \\(k=1\\): the vertical line \\(x=1\\) together with the two lines\n  \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=2\\) (exactly one of them, namely \\(y=2\\), is sunny).\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\) and for \\(k=0,1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer\n\\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le n-2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that\n\n* each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one \\(\\ell_i\\);\n* exactly \\(k'\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.\n\nThen for the integer \\(n+1\\) and every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le (n+1)-2\\) there exist \\(n+1\\) distinct lines\nwith exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones covering \\(T_{\\,n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose the non\u2011sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:=\\{(x,y)\\mid x=1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is vertical and therefore not sunny.  Every point of \\(T_{\\,n+1}\\)\nwith first coordinate equal to \\(1\\) lies on \\(L\\).\n\nRemove from \\(T_{\\,n+1}\\) all points that belong to \\(L\\).  The remaining\nset is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_{\\,n+1}\\setminus L\n&=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+2\\}\\\\\n&=\\{(a',b')\\mid a'=a-1\\ge 1,\\;b'=b\\ge 1,\\;a'+b'\\le n+1\\}\\\\\n&=T_n .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus, after translating the coordinates by \\(( -1,0)\\), the uncovered\npoints form exactly the set \\(T_n\\).\n\nBy the induction hypothesis there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) covering \\(T_n\\) and having any prescribed\nnumber \\(k'\\) of sunny lines, where \\(0\\le k'\\le n-2\\).\nUndoing the translation, we obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(\\ell'_1,\\dots ,\\ell'_n\\) covering the set\n\\(T_{\\,n+1}\\setminus L\\) and possessing the same number \\(k'\\) of sunny\nlines (translation does not change a line\u2019s direction).\n\nNow the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{L,\\ell'_1,\\dots ,\\ell'_n\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers the whole set \\(T_{\\,n+1}\\),\nand contains exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines, because \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny.\nSince \\(k'\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\), the total\nnumber of sunny lines \\(k\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and\n\\((n+1)-2\\).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the induction\n\nThe base case (Lemma\u202f1) provides configurations for \\(n=3\\) and for\n\\(k=0,1\\), i.e. for all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le 3-2\\).\nApplying Lemma\u202f2 repeatedly we obtain, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\nconfigurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every integer\n\\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) contains\nthe whole interval  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThat is, for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.  No statement is made about the values \\(k=n-1\\) or\n\\(k=n\\); the method above does not produce such configurations, and their\npossibility remains open."
