"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to decide whether the values \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) can occur.  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n> **Theorem.**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge 3\\) be an integer.  \n> For every integer \\(k\\) with  \n> \\[\n> 0\\le k\\le n-2\n> \\]\n> there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n> 1. for every pair of positive integers \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n> 2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nThus the admissible set of \\(k\\) always contains the whole interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).  \nThe status of the two largest possible values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) remains open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof is by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n* **Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n  Explicit configurations are exhibited for \\(k=0,1,2\\) (the latter equals \\(n-1\\) but is not needed for the induction). In particular we give constructions with exactly \\(0\\) and exactly \\(1\\) sunny line, thereby establishing the theorem for \\(n=3\\).\n\n* **Inductive step.**  \n  Assume the theorem holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n  From a configuration for \\(n\\) we build one for \\(n+1\\) as follows.\n\n  1. **Add a non\u2011sunny line** \\(L:\\;x=1\\) (vertical, hence parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \n     This line covers all points of the required set whose first coordinate equals \\(1\\).\n\n  2. **Identify the remaining uncovered points.**  \n     After deleting the points on \\(L\\) we obtain the set  \n     \\[\n       \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+2\\}\n       =\\{(a-1,b)\\mid a-1\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;(a-1)+b\\le n+1\\},\n     \\]\n     which is exactly the triangular lattice \\(T_n\\) that appears for the integer \\(n\\).\n\n  3. **Apply the induction hypothesis** to \\(T_n\\).  \n     By hypothesis we can cover \\(T_n\\) with \\(n\\) distinct lines having any prescribed number\n     \\(k'\\) of sunny lines, where \\(0\\le k'\\le n-2\\).\n\n  4. **Translate the obtained lines back** (undo the shift \\(a\\mapsto a-1\\)).  \n     Translation does not change a line\u2019s direction, so the number of sunny lines remains \\(k'\\).\n\n  5. **Combine.**  \n     The total family consists of the new line \\(L\\) together with the \\(n\\) translated lines; it contains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers the whole set required for \\(n+1\\), and has exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines (because \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny).  \n     Since \\(k'\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\), the resulting number of sunny lines can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\((n+1)-2\\).\n\n  This completes the induction and yields the theorem for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Notation\n\nFor an integer \\(m\\ge 2\\) define  \n\\[\nT_m:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\nThus the problem asks for \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction vector is not a scalar multiple of\n\\((1,0)\\) (horizontal), \\((0,1)\\) (vertical) or \\((1,-1)\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\nOtherwise the line is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1 (Base configurations for \\(n=3\\))\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the three vertical lines  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;x=2,\\qquad \\ell_3:\\;x=3 .\n  \\]\n  All three are non\u2011sunny and together they contain the three required points.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the following three distinct lines:\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;x=1\\quad(\\text{non\u2011sunny}),\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;y=1\\quad(\\text{non\u2011sunny}),\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;y=x\\quad(\\text{sunny}).\n  \\]\n  \\(L_1\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); \\(L_2\\) contains \\((2,1)\\); \\(L_3\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\).  \n  Hence every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and exactly one line (\\(L_3\\)) is sunny.\n\nThus the theorem holds for \\(n=3\\) and for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le 3-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following statement holds:\n\n> (**Induction hypothesis**)  \n> For every integer \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le n-2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n> \\(\\mathcal L_{k'}=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one \\(\\ell_i\\);  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k'\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.\n\nWe prove the analogous statement for \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Construction\n\n1. **Add a non\u2011sunny line**  \n   \\[\n   L:\\;x=1 .\n   \\]\n   \\(L\\) is vertical, hence non\u2011sunny, and it contains all points of \\(T_{\\,n+1}\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(1\\).\n\n2. **Describe the uncovered points.**  \n   Removing the points on \\(L\\) leaves\n   \\[\n   U:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{\\,n+1}\\mid a\\ge 2\\}\n      =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+2\\}.\n   \\]\n   Define the translation \\(\\tau:(x,y)\\mapsto (x-1,y)\\).  \n   Applying \\(\\tau\\) to \\(U\\) yields\n   \\[\n   \\tau(U)=\\{(a-1,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+2\\}\n          =\\{(a',b')\\mid a'\\ge 1,\\;b'\\ge 1,\\;a'+b'\\le n+1\\}=T_n .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Apply the induction hypothesis to \\(T_n\\).**  \n   Choose any integer \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le n-2\\).  \n   By the hypothesis there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n   \\(\\ell'_1,\\dots ,\\ell'_n\\) covering \\(T_n\\) and having exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Undo the translation.**  \n   Set \\(\\ell_i:=\\tau^{-1}(\\ell'_i)\\) for each \\(i\\).  \n   Each \\(\\ell_i\\) is a line covering the corresponding points of \\(U\\); the set\n   \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) consists of distinct lines, and the number of sunny lines among them is still \\(k'\\) (translation does not alter direction).\n\n5. **Combine.**  \n   The family\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L:=\\{L,\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n   \\]\n   contains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines.  \n   Every point of \\(T_{\\,n+1}\\) lies either on \\(L\\) (if its first coordinate is \\(1\\)) or on some \\(\\ell_i\\) (otherwise).  \n   Exactly \\(k'\\) of the lines are sunny, because \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny.\n\nSince \\(k'\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\), the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\((n+1)-2\\).\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n+1\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n### Completion of the Induction\n\nThe base case (Lemma\u202f1) establishes the theorem for \\(n=3\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that if the theorem holds for a given \\(n\\) it also holds for \\(n+1\\).  \nBy induction, the theorem is true for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### Final Remark\n\nWe have proved that for each \\(n\\ge 3\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is realizable.  \nThe existence (or impossibility) of configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) has not been settled by the present argument."
