es for \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) and using the fact that each non\u2011sunny line can be counted at most twice (once for a vertical/horizontal line, once for a line of slope \\(-1\\)), we obtain the global lower bound\n   \\[\n   \\#\\{\\text{sunny lines}\\}\\;\\ge\\;\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Construction (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n   *Base configuration.*  \n   For any \\(n\\) we first exhibit a configuration with exactly \\(\\left\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\right\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  \n   Take the \\(\\left\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\right\\rceil\\) lines\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{\\left\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\right\\rceil}\\,x + i,\n   \\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,\\left\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\right\\rceil,\n   \\]\n   which are all sunny (their slopes are distinct and different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)).  \n   One checks directly that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of these lines: solving \\(b = \\frac{i}{m}a + i\\) for \\(i\\) (where \\(m=\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\)) yields an integer \\(i\\) in the required range because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\n   *Adding extra sunny lines.*  \n   If we need more than the minimal number of sunny lines, we simply replace some of the non\u2011sunny lines (which are not used in the base configuration) by arbitrary sunny lines that do not intersect any of the already\u2011present sunny lines at a lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   Because the base configuration already covers all required points, adding further sunny lines never destroys the covering property.  \n   By choosing distinct slopes we keep all lines distinct.  \n   Hence for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) we can obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe two lemmas together give a complete description of all admissible values of \\(k\\) up to the unresolved question of whether the lower bound can ever be improved.  \n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) must contain at least \\(\\displaystyle\\left\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\right\\rceil\\) sunny lines.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nA line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it belongs to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_x=\\{x=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal F_y=\\{y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal F_{-1}=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|S_s|=s-1\\) and the points of \\(S_s\\) lie on the line \\(\\ell_s:x+y=s\\).\n\nA line belonging to \\(\\mathcal F_{-1}\\) coincides with some \\(\\ell_s\\); consequently it can cover **all** points of at most one set \\(S_s\\).  \nA line belonging to \\(\\mathcal F_x\\) (resp. \\(\\mathcal F_y\\)) meets each \\(\\ell_s\\) in exactly one point, so it can cover at most one point of each \\(S_s\\).\n\nHence, to cover the whole set \\(S_s\\) we need at least  \n\n\\[\nc_s:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|S_s|}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n      =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{s-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nlines that are **not** parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. lines from \\(\\mathcal F_x\\cup\\mathcal F_y\\)).  \nIndeed, each such line contributes at most one point of \\(S_s\\); the remaining points must be supplied by a line of family \\(\\mathcal F_{-1}\\), and at most one such line can be used for a given \\(s\\).\n\nSumming (2) over all \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) we obtain a total demand of  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{s-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n    +\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n    +\\cdots\n    +\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach non\u2011sunny line belonging to \\(\\mathcal F_x\\) or \\(\\mathcal F_y\\) can be counted for **at most two** different values of \\(s\\) (once as a vertical line, once as a horizontal line).  \nTherefore the total number of lines from \\(\\mathcal F_x\\cup\\mathcal F_y\\) that appear in the whole family must be at least the right\u2013hand side of (3).  \n\nConsequently the number of sunny lines, i.e. the complement of \\(\\mathcal F_x\\cup\\mathcal F_y\\cup\\mathcal F_{-1}\\), satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines}\\}\\;\\ge\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\qquad\\Box\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*Let \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(\\displaystyle\\left\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\right\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nDefine the \\(m\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{m}\\,x + i ,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,m .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(\\frac{i}{m}\\), which is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\); hence all \\(L_i\\) are sunny, and they are pairwise distinct because their slopes differ.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any integer point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nWrite \\(s:=a+b\\). Since \\(s\\le n+1\\) we have \\(s\\le 2m\\).  \nConsider the equation obtained from (4):\n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{i}{m}a + i \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\ni = \\frac{b}{1+\\frac{a}{m}} = \\frac{mb}{m+a}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\) are positive integers and \\(m+a\\) divides \\(mb\\) exactly when \\(s=m+i\\) for some integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m\\), one checks that the integer  \n\n\\[\ni = s-m \\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,m\\}\n\\]\n\nsatisfies the above equality, i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).  \nThus every required lattice point belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\).\n\nNow we have a configuration of \\(m\\) sunny lines that already fulfills the covering condition.  \nIf \\(k=m\\) we are done.  \n\nIf \\(k>m\\), we need to add \\(k-m\\) further sunny lines while preserving distinctness and the covering property.  \nChoose any \\(k-m\\) distinct slopes different from those already used and different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); for each such slope \\(\\lambda\\) pick a line  \n\n\\[\nM_\\lambda:\\; y = \\lambda x + c_\\lambda ,\n\\]\n\nwhere the intercept \\(c_\\lambda\\) is chosen so that the line does **not** pass through any lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) (this is possible because there are only finitely many such points).  \nAll the added lines are sunny, distinct from each other and from the original \\(L_i\\), and they do not affect the already\u2011satisfied covering condition.\n\nFinally, if \\(k<n\\) we may insert any remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (for instance vertical lines \\(x=0,1,\\dots\\)); they do not interfere with the covering property because it is already guaranteed by the sunny lines.\n\nThus we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that meets the required condition.  \\(\\Box\\)\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f2 shows that this condition is also sufficient: for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nthere exists a suitable configuration.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is contained in, and contains, the interval above.  The exact answer to the original problem is therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\n\\Bigl\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid \n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\Bigr\\}}\n\\]\n\nprovided that no configuration can achieve a smaller number of sunny lines; the proof of the optimality of the lower bound remains an open sub\u2011problem. "
