=0\\) (i.e. lines from \\(\\mathcal F_x\\cup\\mathcal F_y\\)).  \nIndeed, each such line supplies at most one point of \\(S_s\\); the remaining points must be supplied by a line of family \\(\\mathcal F_{-1}\\), and at most one line of that family can be used for a given \\(s\\).\n\nSumming (2) over all \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}c_s\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n    +\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n    +\\cdots\n    +\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach line belonging to \\(\\mathcal F_x\\) or \\(\\mathcal F_y\\) can be counted for at most two different values of \\(s\\) (once as a vertical line, once as a horizontal line).  \nTherefore the total number of lines from \\(\\mathcal F_x\\cup\\mathcal F_y\\) that appear in the whole family must be at least the right\u2013hand side of (3).  \n\nHence the number of sunny lines, i.e. the complement of \\(\\mathcal F_x\\cup\\mathcal F_y\\cup\\mathcal F_{-1}\\), satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines}\\}\\;\\ge\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\qquad\\Box\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*Let \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nPut  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{m}\\,x + i .\n\\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes \\(\\frac{i}{m}\\) are distinct and none equals \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\); consequently the \\(m\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) are sunny and pairwise distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nWrite \\(s:=a+b\\). Because \\(s\\le