nfty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny lines cover at most two points).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\in\\mathcal{P}\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nThe vectors \\((a_{2}-a_{1},b_{2}-b_{1})\\) and \\((a_{3}-a_{1},b_{3}-b_{1})\\) are linearly dependent, so the slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals either \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (the only slopes that make two integer vectors parallel). This contradicts the definition of a sunny line. \u220e\n\nConsequently a family containing \\(k\\) sunny lines covers at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Non\u2011sunny lines cover at most \\(n\\) points).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three directions:\n\n* Horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\mathbb Z\\)).  \n  Points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) on it satisfy \\(b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such points.\n\n* Vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \n  Symmetrically there are at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n* Diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\).  \n  Points satisfy \\(a+b=c\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(b=c-a\\le n+1-a\\).  \n  The maximal possible number of solutions is attained for \\(c=n+2\\) and equals \\(n\\).\n\nThus any non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Extremal inequality).\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThen\n\n\\[\n2k+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;N .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points; by Lemma\u202f2 each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines covers at most \\(n\\) points.  \nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) must lie on at least one line, the total number of points that can be covered is at least \\(N\\), which yields (3). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Corollary 4 (Upper bound for \\(k\\)).\n\nFrom (3) and the value of \\(N\\) in (2),\n\n\\[\n2k+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow\n2k\\,(n-2)\\le n(n-1).\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nK_{\\max}= \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEvery admissible configuration must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Existence for \\(k=0\\)).\n\nThe \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\nare all non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6 (Existence for \\(k=K_{\\max}\\)).\n\nSelect \\(2K_{\\max}\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) and pair them arbitrarily.  \nFor each pair join the two points by the unique line through them; because the two points are distinct, the resulting line cannot be parallel to the three forbidden directions, so it is sunny.  \nThus we obtain \\(K_{\\max}\\) sunny lines covering exactly \\(2K_{\\max}\\) points.\n\nThe remaining set \\(\\mathcal{P}'\\) has size  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}'|=N-2K_{\\max}\\le (n-K_{\\max})\\,n,\n\\]\n\nthe inequality being a rearranged form of (3).  \nConsequently the points of \\(\\mathcal{P}'\\) can be covered by the remaining \\(n-K_{\\max}\\) non\u2011sunny lines: use horizontal, vertical or diagonal lines as needed (Lemma\u202f2 guarantees each such line can accommodate up to \\(n\\) points).  \nAll lines are distinct, so we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(K_{\\max}\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom Corollary\u202f4 we have the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le K_{\\max},\n\\qquad \nK_{\\max}= \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nLemmas\u202f5 and\u202f6 show that the two extremal values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=K_{\\max}\\) are attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is a subset of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,K_{\\max}\\}\\) containing at least \\(\\{0,K_{\\max}\\}\\). \u220e"
