lity (4) shows that **any** admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le K_{\\max}\\).\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}\\).**  \n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 sunny lines.*  \n   Choose arbitrarily \\(2k\\) distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) and pair them.  \n   For each pair join the two points by the unique line through them.  \n   Because the two points are distinct and not aligned with any of the three forbidden directions, each of these lines is sunny.  \n   Thus we obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines covering exactly the chosen \\(2k\\) points.\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 capacity of the remaining lines.*  \n   After Step\u202f1 the set of uncovered points has size  \n   \\[\n   N-2k\\le (n-k)n ,\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   where the inequality is precisely (2) written for the given \\(k\\).  \n   Hence the uncovered points can be partitioned into at most \\(n-k\\) groups, each of size at most \\(n\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   For each group we use a single non\u2011sunny line that contains all its points:\n   * if the group consists of points with a common \\(y\\)-coordinate we take the horizontal line \\(y=c\\);\n   * if they share a common \\(x\\)-coordinate we take the vertical line \\(x=c\\);\n   * otherwise they all satisfy \\(x+y=c\\) and we take the diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\).  \n   Because each group has at most \\(n\\) points, such a line always exists (Lemma\u202f2).  \n   The groups are chosen so that the resulting lines are distinct; this is possible because the groups are disjoint and each line is determined by its constant term (\\(c\\) in the three families).\n\n   Thus we obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n6. **Extremal values.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* is realised by the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\,x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  \n   *\\(k=K_{\\max}\\)* follows from the construction above with the maximal admissible \\(k\\).\n\nHence the necessary condition (4) is also sufficient, and the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,K_{\\max}\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN=|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal P\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nThe vectors \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)\\) and \\((a_3-a_1,b_3-b_1)\\) are integer and linearly dependent, so their slopes are equal.  \nThe only integer slopes that can arise from two non\u2011zero integer vectors are \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); otherwise the two vectors would be linearly independent.  \nThus \\(\\ell\\) would be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, contradicting the definition of a sunny line. \u220e\n\nConsequently a family containing \\(k\\) sunny lines covers at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThree possibilities.\n\n*Horizontal* \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\mathbb Z\\)).  \nPoints satisfy \\(b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such points.\n\n*Vertical* \\(x=c\\).  \nSymmetrically at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Diagonal* \\(x+y=c\\).  \nPoints satisfy \\(a+b=c\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(b=c-a\\le n+1-a\\).  \nThe maximal number of solutions occurs for \\(c=n+2\\) and equals \\(n\\). \