n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P'| = N-2k \\le (n-k)n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Partition \\(\\mathcal P'\\) into at most \\(n-k\\) subsets, each of size at most \\(n\\).  \n   This is always possible: repeatedly take any \\(n\\) (or fewer) remaining points and form a subset until fewer than \\(n\\) points are left; the number of subsets created is at most \\(\\lceil |\\mathcal P'|/n\\rceil\\le n-k\\) because of (5).\n\n3. **Non\u2011sunny part.**  \n   For each subset we select a non\u2011sunny line that contains all its points:\n   * if all points share the same \\(y\\)-coordinate we use the horizontal line \\(y=c\\);\n   * if they share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate we use the vertical line \\(x=c\\);\n   * otherwise they all satisfy a common equation \\(x+y=c\\) and we use the diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\).  \n   Lemma\u202f2 guarantees that a line of the appropriate type can contain up to \\(n\\) points, so each subset fits on a single line.  \n   Because the subsets are disjoint, the constants \\(c\\) obtained are distinct, hence the resulting non\u2011sunny lines are distinct.\n\nThus we have constructed \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines that together cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 7 (Existence for \\(k=K_{\\max}\\))\n\nLemma\u202f6 applied with \\(k=K_{\\max}\\) yields a configuration with the maximal possible number of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}\\,\\}},\\qquad \nK_{\\max}= \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe necessity of the bound follows from Lemma\u202f3 (extremal inequality) and Corollary\u202f4.  \nSufficiency for every integer \\(k\\) in the interval follows from Lem