]\n\nThese three multisets together equal \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) taken twice.\nChoosing the \\(m\\) largest elements of this multiset yields the maximum\npossible coverage, and those largest elements are \\(n,n-1,\\dots ,n+1-m\\).\nThey are realised by the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\).  Summing them\ngives the formula for \\(C(m)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Pigeonhole bound)  \nIf a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then \\(0\\le k\\le3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(m=n-k\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  By Lemma\u202f1 the sunny lines\ncover at most \\(2k\\) points, and by Lemma\u202f2 the non\u2011sunny lines cover at most\n\\(C(m)\\) points.  Since every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered,\n\n\\[\n2k+C(m)\\ge|T_n| .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) (formula (1)) and the expression for\n\\(C(m)\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n2(n-m)+(n+1)m-\\frac{m(m+1)}2\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) and simplifying leads to\n\n\\[\nm^{2}-(2n-3)m+(n^{2}-3n)\\le0 .\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant of the quadratic is \\(\\Delta=(2n-3)^{2}-4(n^{2}-3n)=9\\);\nits roots are \\(m=n-3\\) and \\(m=n\\).  Inequality (3.2) therefore holds\niff \\(n-3\\le m\\le n\\).  Because \\(m=n-k\\), this is equivalent to\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le3 .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny part.*  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(m\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  The points of \\(T_n\\) that are not\ncovered by (4.1) are exactly those with abscissa \\(a\\) satisfying\n\\(m+1\\le a\\le n\\); for such an \\(a\\) the admissible ordinates are\n\\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).  Consequently the set of uncovered points is\n\n\\[\nU_k=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid a=m+1,\\dots ,n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\},\n\\]\nand \\(|U_k|=\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{k}i=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nBecause of Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(U_k\\).\nFor \\(k\\le3\\) we have \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le2k\\); therefore the points of\n\\(U_k\\) can be partitioned into \\(k\\) pairs (with one singleton when\n\\(k=1\\)) and each pair can be placed on a distinct sunny line of slope \\(1\\)\n(which is never parallel to the forbidden directions).  Explicitly we may\nchoose the sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y = x-(m+j-1)\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(j=1\\) the line \\(\\ell_1\\) contains the point \\((n,1)\\); for\n\\(j=2\\) it contains \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,2)\\); for \\(j=3\\) it contains\n\\((n-2,1),(n-1,2),(n,3)\\), etc.  In every case all points of \\(U_k\\) lie on\nthe collection \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\), and the \\(\\ell_j\\) are pairwise\ndistinct and sunny.\n\n*Resulting family.*  \n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt consists of exactly \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, contains precisely \\(k\\)\nsunny lines, and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus for each \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemma\u202f3 shows that \\(k\\ge4\\) is impossible, while the construction above\ndemonstrates that \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are all attainable.  Hence the set of\nnon\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the problem admits a solution is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
