-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following statement holds:\n\n> (**Induction hypothesis**)  \n> For every integer \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le n-2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n> \\(\\mathcal L_{k'}=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one \\(\\ell_i\\);  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k'\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.\n\nWe prove the analogous statement for \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Construction\n\n1. **Add a non\u2011sunny line**  \n   \\[\n   L:\\;x=1 .\n   \\]\n   \\(L\\) is vertical, hence non\u2011sunny, and it contains all points of \\(T_{\\,n+1}\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(1\\).\n\n2. **Describe the uncovered points.**  \n   Removing the points on \\(L\\) leaves\n   \\[\n   U:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{\\,n+1}\\mid a\\ge 2\\}\n      =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+2\\}.\n   \\]\n   Define the translation \\(\\tau:(x,y)\\mapsto (x-1,y)\\).  \n   Applying \\(\\tau\\) to \\(U\\) yields\n   \\[\n   \\tau(U)=\\{(a-1,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+2\\}\n          =\\{(a',b')\\mid a'\\ge 1,\\;b'\\ge 1,\\;a'+b'\\le n+1\\}=T_n .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Apply the induction hypothesis to \\(T_n\\).**  \n   Choose any integer \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le n-2\\).  \n   By the hypothesis there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n   \\(\\ell'_1,\\dots ,\\ell'_n\\) covering \\(T_n\\) and having exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Undo the translation.**  \n   Set \\(\\ell_i:=\\tau^{-1}(\\ell'_i)\\) for each \\(i\\).  \n   Each \\(\\ell_i\\) is a line covering the corresponding points of \\(U\\); the set\n   \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) consists of distinct lines, and the number of sunny lines among them is still \\(k'\\) (translation does not alter direction).\n\n5. **Combine.**  \n   The family\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L:=\\{L,\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n   \\]\n   contains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines.  \n   Every point of \\(T_{\\,n+1}\\)