k\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(m=n-k\\) the number of\n   non\u2011sunny lines.  Using (2) and (3),\n\n   \\[\n   ks+C(m)\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(s=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and the expression\n   for \\(C(m)\\), a short algebraic manipulation gives\n\n   \\[\n   (m-n+3)(m-n)\\le 0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(n-3\\le m\\le n\\), i.e. \\(0\\le k\\le 3\\).  No configuration with\n   \\(k\\ge 4\\) sunny lines can exist.\n\n5. **Explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).**  \n   For each admissible \\(k\\) we exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all\n   points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the constructions\n   are the same as in the original answer; the correctness of the sunny part\n   now follows from the bound (2) rather than the false Lemma\u202f1).\n\n   *\\(k=0\\):* the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\):* vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line\n   \\(y=x-(n-1)\\).  \n   *\\(k=2\\):* vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) together with\n   \\(y=x-(n-2)\\) and \\(y=x-(n-1)\\).  \n   *\\(k=3\\):* vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) together with\n   \\(y=x-(n-2),\\;y=x-(n-1),\\;y=x-n\\).\n\n   In each case the sunny lines have slope \\(1\\) (hence are not parallel to any\n   forbidden direction) and together with the vertical lines they cover every\n   point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nFrom the impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\) (inequality (4)) and the explicit\nrealisation for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) we conclude that the only possible numbers of\nsunny lines are \\(0,1,2,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1\u2032 (Bound for a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line, i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Then \\(\\ell\\) contains at most  \n\n\\[\ns:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equation of \\(\\ell\\) as \\(px+qy=r\\) with integers \\(p,q\\) that are\ncoprime and not equal to \\((1,0),(0,1),(1,1)\\).  The set of integer points on\n\\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\{(x_0+qt,\\;y_0-pt)\\mid t\\in\\mathbb Z\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is any particular integer point on \\(\\ell\\).  Hence the\ndifference between the coordinates of two consecutive points of \\(\\ell\\) is\n\\((q,-p)\\); the sum of the absolute values of the coordinate changes is\n\\(|p|+|q|\\).\n\nIf \\((x,y)\\in T_n\\) then \\(x+y\\le n+1\\).  Moving from one lattice point of\n\\(\\ell\\) to the next changes the value of \\(x+y\\) by \\(|p|+|q|\\).  Consequently\nalong \\(\\ell\\) the quantity \\(x+y\\) can take at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\ndifferent values, and therefore \\(\\ell\\) contains at most that many points of\n\\(T_n\\).  Because \\(\\ell\\) is sunny we have \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\); the smallest\npossible denominator is \\(2\\), giving the bound \\(s=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\n\\bigr\\rfloor\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Maximum coverage by non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nFor an integer \\(m\\ge1\\) the largest possible number of points of \\(T_n\\) that\ncan be covered by \\(m\\) lines each parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis\nor the line \\(x+y=0\\) is  \n\n\\[\nC(m)=(n+1)m-\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of\n\\(T_n\\); a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains the same number, and a line of\nslope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=c\\) contains \\(c-1\\) points.  Collecting all these\nnumbers for \\(c=1,\\dots ,n\\) yields the multiset  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. three copies of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  To maximise the total number of\ncovered points we choose the \\(m\\) largest elements of this multiset, namely\n\\(n,n-1,\\dots ,n+1-m\\).  Their sum is exactly the expression for \\(C(m)\\)\nabove, and it is attained by the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines)\n\nIf a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers every point of \\(T_n\\) and contains\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then \\(0\\le k\\le3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(m=n-k\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  By Lemma\u202f1\u2032 each sunny line\ncovers at most \\(s=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\);\nby Lemma\u202f2 the non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(C(m)\\) points.\nSince all \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) points must be covered,\n\n\\[\nks+C(m)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert the explicit formulas \\(s=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and\n\\(C(m)=(n+1)m-\\dfrac{m(m+1)}2\\) with \\(m=n-k\\).  A straightforward\ncomputation (splitting the parity of \\(n\\) to handle the floor) yields the\ninequality\n\n\\[\n(m-n+3)(m-n)\\le0 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(n-3\\le m\\le n\\), i.e. \\(0\\le k\\le3\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) we exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the\nrequirements.\n\n*Common non\u2011sunny part.*  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  The points of \\(T_n\\) not lying\non any of them are exactly\n\n\\[\nU_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a=n-k+1,\\dots ,n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n\\]\n\nand \\(|U_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nFor \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y = x-(n-k+j-1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_j\\) has slope \\(1\\), hence is sunny.  Direct inspection shows that\n\\(\\ell_j\\) contains precisely the points \\((n-k+j,1),\\,(n-k+j+1,2),\\dots\\)\nthat belong to \\(U_k\\); consequently the union of the \\(k\\) lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) covers all points of \\(U_k\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines,\nand covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemma\u202f3 proves that no configuration can have more than three sunny lines,\nwhile the explicit families \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) demonstrate that \\(k=0,1,2,3\\)\nare all attainable.  Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,3\\}} .\n\\]"

