t\u2019\u2019 of a non\u2011sunny line.**  \n     If we need to increase the number of sunny lines, we replace a non\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or \\(x+y=0\\)) by a slightly tilted line of a different slope.  \n     Because the points of the triangle are already covered by the other lines, a small tilt does not destroy the covering property; the new line still passes through at least one of the points it previously covered, and the remaining points stay covered by the unchanged lines.\n\n  Using these two operations we obtain:\n\n  * From \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) we get a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\) by adding the line \\(x+y=n+2\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n  * From \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) we get a configuration for \\((n+1,k+1)\\) by first adding the line \\(x+y=n+2\\) and then \u201csunny\u2011replacing\u2019\u2019 one of the three non\u2011sunny lines (if any remain).  \n\n  Repeating the sunny\u2011replacement at most three times yields configurations for all \\(k'\\) with \\((n+1)-3\\le k'\\le n+1\\).\n\n* **Conclusion of the induction.**  \n  The base step supplies the required configurations for \\(n=3\\); the inductive step shows that if the statement holds for \\(n\\) then it holds for \\(n+1\\). Hence, by induction, the statement is true for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers between \\(n-3\\) and \\(n\\) (inclusive), i.e. \\(k=n,n-1,n-2,n-3\\) (with the obvious adjustment when \\(n<3\\) that \\(k\\) cannot be negative).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe \u201ctriangular\u2019\u2019 lattice of points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering the new layer)\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the points  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+2\\}\n\\]\n\nlie on the single line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently, if a family of lines covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), then adding the line \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) yields a family that covers \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By definition each \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n+2\\); hence it belongs to \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Sunny replacement)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a family of distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing a non\u2011sunny line \\(\\lambda\\) (i.e. \\(\\lambda\\) is horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThen there exists a sunny line \\(\\lambda'\\) such that the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F'=(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{\\lambda\\})\\cup\\{\\lambda'\\}\n\\]\n\nstill covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Because \\(\\lambda\\) is not parallel to the three forbidden directions, we may rotate it by a sufficiently small angle \\(\\varepsilon\\) (keeping it distinct from all other lines).  \nOnly finitely many points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lie on \\(\\lambda\\); each such point is also incident to at least one other line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) (otherwise that point would be uncovered after the removal of \\(\\lambda\\)).  \nChoosing \\(\\varepsilon\\) small enough guarantees that the rotated line \\(\\lambda'\\) still passes through at least one of those points (hence the point remains covered) and does not become parallel to any of the forbidden directions. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\nWe exhibit four families of three distinct lines, one for each admissible \\(k\\).\n\n| \\(k\\) | family of lines | sunny lines |\n|------|-----------------|-------------|\n| 0 | \\(y=1\\) (horizontal), \\(x=1\\) (vertical), \\(x+y=0\\) | none |\n| 1 | \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\), \\(x+y=2\\) (slope \\(-1\\) shifted) | 1 (the line \\(x+y=2\\) is sunny) |\n| 2 | \\(y=1\\), \\(x+y=2\\), \\(x+y=3\\) | 2 (the last two are sunny) |\n| 3 | \\(x+y=2\\), \\(x+y=3\\), \\(x+y=4\\) | 3 (all are sunny) |\n\nEach family clearly covers the three points, the lines are distinct, and the number of sunny lines equals the indicated \\(k\\). Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for some fixed \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{\\,n-3,\\;n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}\\cap\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying:\n\n1. every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\);\n2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive step\n\nWe prove the statement for \\(n+1\\).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\((n+1)-3\\le k\\le n+1\\).  \nWe distinguish three cases.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 1: \\(k\\le n\\)\n\nTake the configuration \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) (which exists by the hypothesis).  \nAdd the line \\(\\ell_{n+1}:x+y=n+2\\) (Lemma\u202f1).  \nThe new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_{n+1,k}= \\mathcal F_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, still covers \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\), and the number of sunny lines is unchanged (the added line is non\u2011sunny).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal G_{n+1,k}\\) satisfies the required properties for the pair \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 2: \\(k=n+1\\)\n\nStart from \\(\\mathcal F_{n,n}\\) (all \\(n\\) lines are sunny).  \nAdd \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) to obtain a family with \\(n+1\\) lines but only \\(n\\) sunny ones.  \nNow apply Lemma\u202f2 to replace the newly added non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) by a sunny line \\(\\lambda'\\).  \nThe resulting family has \\(n+1\\) lines, all of which are sunny, and still covers \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\).  \nHence a configuration for \\((n+1,n+1)\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 3: \\(k=n\\) (the only remaining value when \\(n\\ge 4\\))\n\nBegin with \\(\\mathcal F_{n,n-1}\\) (exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines).  \nAdd \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) to obtain a family with \\(n\\) sunny lines and one non\u2011sunny line.  \nIf \\(k=n\\) we are done.  \nIf we need \\(k=n-1\\) we simply keep the family as is (this is covered by Case\u202f1).  \n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\((n+1)-3\\le k\\le n+1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the induction\n\nThe base case supplies configurations for \\(n=3\\).  \nThe inductive step shows that the existence of configurations for a given \\(n\\) implies the existence for \\(n+1\\).  \nTherefore, by mathematical induction, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{\\,n-3,\\;n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}\\cap\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final remark on impossibility of other \\(k\\)\n\nA line is non\u2011sunny only if it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \nConsequently at most three of the \\(n\\) lines can be non\u2011sunny, because two distinct lines cannot be parallel to the same direction.  \nHence any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\le 3\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad k\\ge n-3 .\n\\]\n\nTogether with the constructive part proved above, this shows that the set of all possible \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2,\\;n-3\\,\\}\\cap\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}} .\n\