 may rotate it about any point on \\(\\lambda\\) by a sufficiently small angle \\(\\varepsilon>0\\).  \nOnly finitely many points of \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) lie on \\(\\lambda\\); each of those points is also incident to at least one other line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) (otherwise it would become uncovered after the removal of \\(\\lambda\\)).  \n\nChoose \\(\\varepsilon\\) so small that the rotated line \\(\\lambda'\\) still passes through at least one of the points formerly on \\(\\lambda\\) (this is possible because the set of points is discrete).  \nSince the rotation is tiny, \\(\\lambda'\\) cannot become parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, hence it is sunny.  \nAll points that were covered by lines other than \\(\\lambda\\) remain covered, and the points that were on \\(\\lambda\\) remain covered because at least one of them still lies on \\(\\lambda'\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(\\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe following four families of three distinct lines satisfy the covering condition and contain the indicated number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n| \\(k\\) | Family of lines | Sunny lines |\n|------|-----------------|-------------|\n| 0 | \\(y=1\\) (horizontal), \\(x=1\\) (vertical), \\(x+y=0\\) | 0 |\n| 1 | \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\), \\(x+y=2\\) (slope \\(-1\\) shifted) | 1 |\n| 2 | \\(y=1\\), \\(x+y=2\\), \\(x+y=3\\) | 2 |\n| 3 | \\(x+y=2\\), \\(x+y=3\\), \\(x+y=4\\) | 3 |\n\nEach family clearly covers the three points, the lines are distinct, and the number of sunny lines equals the displayed \\(k\\). Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for a fixed \\(n\\ge3\\) and for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{\\,n-3,\\;n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}\\cap\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive step (construction for \\(n+1\\))\n\nLet \\(k'\\) be any integer with \\((n+1)-3\\le k'\\le n+1\\).  \nWe construct a suitable family \\(\\mathcal F_{n+1,k'}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Obtaining a family with the same number of sunny lines\n\nTake \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k'}\\) (which exists because \\(k'\\le n\\) and \\(k'\\ge (n+1)-3\\) implies \\(k'\\ge n-3\\)).  \nAdd the line \\(\\ell_{n+1}:x+y=n+2\\) given by Lemma\u202f1:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_{n+1,k'}:=\\mathcal F_{n,k'}\\cup\\{\\ell_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal G_{n+1,k'}\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\) (Lemma\u202f1), and contains exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines because \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) is non\u2011sunny.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal G_{n+1,k'}\\) works for every \\(k'\\) with \\(k'\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Increasing the number of sunny lines by one\n\nSuppose we need a family with \\(k'+1\\) sunny lines, where \\(k'\\le n\\).  \nStart from \\(\\mathcal G_{n+1,k'}\\) constructed above.  \nIf \\(\\mathcal G_{n+1,k'}\\) already contains a non\u2011sunny line (it does: \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\)), apply Lemma\u202f2 to replace that non\u2011sunny line by a sunny line \\(\\lambda'\\).  \nThe resulting family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{n+1,k'+1}:=(\\mathcal G_{n+1,k'}\\setminus\\{\\ell_{n+1}\\})\\cup\\{\\lambda'\\}\n\\]\n\nhas \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, still covers \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\), and now has \\(k'+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nRepeating this replacement at most three times (there are at most three non\u2011sunny directions) yields families for all \\(k'\\) with \\((n+1)-3\\le k'\\le n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Completion of the induction\n\nThe base case supplies families for \\(n=3\\).  \nThe inductive construction shows that if families exist for a given \\(n\\) for every admissible \\(k\\), then families also exist for \\(n+1\\) for every admissible \\(k\\).  \nTherefore, by mathematical induction, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{\\,n-3,\\;n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}\\cap\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nA line is non\u2011sunny only if it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)).  \nTwo distinct lines cannot be parallel to the same direction, so at most three of the \\(n\\) lines can be non\u2011sunny.  \nHence any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\le3\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad k\\ge n-3 .\n\\]\n\nCombined with the constructive part, this shows that the set of all possible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2,\\;n-3\\,\\}\\cap\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
