oint of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Final family.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k = \\bigl(\\mathcal D\\setminus\\{D_{n+2}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|\\mathcal D|=n\\) and we have added exactly \\(k\\) new lines while discarding none (the line \\(D_{n+2}\\) was not in \\(\\mathcal D\\) to begin with), we have \\(|\\mathcal F_k|=n\\).  \n   By construction exactly \\(k\\) members of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) are sunny.\n\n### Verification of the covering property\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nIf \\(a+b=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(D_c\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) (because we never removed any \\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\le n+1\\)).  \nHence every point of \\(S_n\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, satisfying the required covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines meeting the two stated conditions, with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n* No configuration can have more than \\(n\\) lines, so \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\); trivially \\(k\\) cannot be negative.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
