e non\u2011sunny lines must be chosen among the diagonal family \\(\\{D_c\\}\\); otherwise a point of some \\(D_c\\) would be missed because a non\u2011sunny line with a different direction meets that diagonal in at most one point.  \n  Hence the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines are a sub\u2011family of \\(\\{D_c\\}\\) and together they can cover at most \\((n-k)\\cdot\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points (each diagonal line contains at most \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points of \\(S_n\\)).  \n\n  Adding the contribution of the \\(k\\) sunny lines, which together cover at most \\(k\\cdot n\\) points, we obtain the overall upper bound  \n\n  \\[\n  |S_n|\\le (n-k)\\frac{n+1}{2}+k n .\n  \\]\n\n  Simplifying,\n\n  \\[\n  \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le \\frac{(n-k)(n+1)}2+kn\n  \\Longleftrightarrow\n  0\\le k\\bigl(n-\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\bigr)=k\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n  \\]\n\n  The inequality is always true, but equality can hold only when every estimate above is exact.  \n  Exactness forces:\n\n  1. each non\u2011sunny line to be a diagonal line \\(D_c\\) (otherwise some diagonal would lose a point);\n  2. each sunny line to contain **exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\)**, i.e. one point from every diagonal \\(D_c\\).\n\n  Condition\u202f2 is impossible because a line of slope \\(\\alpha\\neq-1\\) meets the family \\(\\{D_c\\}\\) in at most one point per diagonal, but the \\(n\\) points would have to lie on a single straight line of slope \\(\\alpha\\).  \n  The set of points \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=c\\}\\) for \\(c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) is not collinear unless \\(\\alpha=-1\\), which is forbidden.  \n  Hence a sunny line can never contain one point from each diagonal, so the inequality is strict for any \\(k\\ge1\\), contradicting the required equality \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\n  Therefore \\(k\\) cannot be positive.\n\n* **Conclusion.**  \n  The only possible value is \\(k=0\\); the family \\(\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) provides an explicit construction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad\n|S_n|=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the line  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(c\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) and \\(a+b=c\\), then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(D_c\\).  \nConversely, any point on \\(D_c\\) has sum \\(c\\le n+1\\), hence belongs to \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll lines \\(D_c\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore their slope is \\(-1\\), a forbidden slope. Distinct constants \\(c\\) give distinct lines, so \\(|\\mathcal D|=n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3  \nA sunny line meets each diagonal line \\(D_c\\) in **at most one** point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct lines intersect in at most one point. Since a sunny line is not parallel to any \\(D_c\\) (its slope is not \\(-1\\)), it is not coincident with any \\(D_c\\); hence the intersection with each \\(D_c\\) is either empty or a single point. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 the set \\(\\mathcal D\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and, by Lemma\u202f1, every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of them.  \nThus the required configuration exists with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of a sunny line (\\(k\\ge1\\))\n\nAssume that a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies