is that treats \\(\\mathcal D'\\) as the complete set of non\u2011sunny lines is therefore based on a flawed premise.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Bounding the number of points covered by non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n> \u201cEach diagonal \\(D_c\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\); in particular  \n> \\[\n> |D_c\\cap S_n|=c-1\\le n-1\\le \\frac{n+1}{2}\\quad\\text{for all }c.\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Critical Error:**  \n- The inequality \\(c-1\\le n-1\\) is correct, but the subsequent inequality \\(n-1\\le \\frac{n+1}{2}\\) holds **only** for \\(n\\le3\\). For \\(n\\ge4\\) we have \\(n-1 > \\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  \n- Therefore the claim that *every* diagonal contains at most \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points of \\(S_n\\) is false for the majority of admissible \\(n\\).  \n\nBecause this bound is used to estimate the total number of points that the non\u2011sunny lines can cover, the estimate that follows is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 11. Inequality (1)  \n\n> \u201cHence the total number of points covered by the non\u2011sunny lines is at most  \n> \\[\n> \\sum_{D_c\\in\\mathcal D'}|D_c\\cap S_n|\n> \\le (n-k)\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:** This inequality directly uses the false bound from step\u202f10. Consequently, inequality\u202f(1) is not guaranteed to hold for \\(n\\ge4\\). The argument that the non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most \\((n-k)\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points is therefore unsound.\n\n---\n\n### 12. Claim\u202f2  \n\n> \u201c**Claim\u202f2.** A sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification:** Correct. A sunny line can intersect each of the \\(n\\) diagonals in at most one point, so it can contain at most one point from each diagonal, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Inequality (2)  \n\n> \u201cConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k n\\) points, and together with (1) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> |S_n|\n> \\le (n-k)\\frac{n+1}{2}+k n .\n> \\tag{2}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Ver