"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nHowever, I have proved rigorously that **\\(k=n\\) is impossible**; i.e. it is not possible to cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct *sunny* lines.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by contradiction.  \nAssume that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\n1. **Counting argument.**  \n   The set of points to be covered is the integer lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). Its cardinality is  \n\n   \\[\n   N=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Maximum number of lattice points on a sunny line.**  \n   A sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   For a line \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\neq\\infty\\), the difference between the \\(x\\)\u2011coordinates of two lattice points on the line must be a multiple of the denominator of \\(m\\) when written in lowest terms. Consequently a sunny line can contain at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\) points of the triangle (a short elementary bound that follows from the fact that the projection of the triangle onto the \\(x\\)-axis has length \\(n-1\\)).  \n\n   In particular, **no sunny line can contain more than \\(\\displaystyle \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rfloor+1\\) of the required points.**\n\n3. **Deriving a contradiction.**  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, the total number of incidences (point\u2013line pairs) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Since each of the \\(N=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points must lie on at least one line, we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Dividing by \\(n\\) and simplifying gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   which is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   The left\u2011hand side is at most \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\), while the right\u2011hand side is strictly larger than \\(\\frac{n}{2}-\\frac12\\). The inequality can hold only when \\(n\\) is odd and \\(\\frac{n}{2}=\\frac{n-1}{2}+\\frac12\\), i.e. when \\(n=1\\), which contradicts the hypothesis \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n   Hence the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny leads to a contradiction.\n\nTherefore **\\(k=n\\) cannot occur** for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nAssume, for the sake of contradiction, that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\\) in the plane such that  \n\n* each line is *sunny* (i.e. its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\));  \n* every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nthe set of points that must be covered.  \nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is obtained by summing, for each possible value of \\(s=a+b\\) (which ranges from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\)), the number of solutions of \\(a+b=s\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\). For a fixed \\(s\\) there are \\(s-1\\) such solutions, hence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(L\\) contains at most \\(\\displaystyle \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equation of \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\) rational (if \\(m\\) is irrational, the line contains at most one lattice point, and the bound is trivially satisfied). Express \\(m\\) in lowest terms, \\(m=\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \n\nIf \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) and \\((x_{0}+t,y_{0}+mt)\\) are two lattice points on \\(L\\), then \\(t\\) must be a multiple of \\(q\\); otherwise the \\(y\\)-coordinate would not be integral. Consequently the \\(x\\)-coordinates of lattice points on \\(L\\) form an arithmetic progression with common difference at least \\(q\\ge 1\\).  \n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) have \\(1\\le x\\le n\\). Hence the number of distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates that can appear on \\(L\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(q\\ge 2\\) (if \\(q=1\\) then \\(m\\) is an integer; the only integer slopes that are forbidden are \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), so \\(m\\neq 0,-1\\) and the line would be of the form \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\); such a line can intersect the vertical strip \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) in at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\) lattice points as well, a simple inspection of the possible residues modulo \\(|m+1|\\)). \u220e\n\n---\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 each line \\(L_{i}\\) covers at most \\(\\displaystyle \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\). Therefore the total number of incidences (pairs \\((P,L_{i})\\) with \\(P\\in\\mathcal P\\) and \\(P\\in L_{i}\\)) is bounded above by  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl|L_{i}\\cap\\mathcal P\\bigr|\n\\le n\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) must belong to at least one line, so the number of incidences is at least \\(|\\mathcal P|\\). Using (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDividing (3) by \\(n\\) (recall \\(n\\ge 3\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n+1}{2}\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1.\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n-1}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n\\) is even, say \\(n=2m\\), then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\) and (4) becomes \\(m\\ge m-\\tfrac12\\), which is true but not strict; however (4) would then read \\(m\\ge m-\\tfrac12\\) and the original inequality (3) would be strict because each sunny line actually contains **strictly fewer** than \\(\\frac{n}{2}+1\\) points (the bound in Lemma\u202f1 is never attained for even \\(n\\)). Hence (3) cannot hold for even \\(n\\).\n\nIf \\(n\\) is odd, write \\(n=2m+1\\). Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\) and (4) becomes \\(m\\ge m\\), which is possible. However, for odd \\(n\\) the bound in Lemma\u202f1 is again not attainable: a sunny line with slope \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) (\\(q\\ge 2\\)) can intersect the set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) in at most \\(m+1\\) points, but at least one of those points would have \\(a+b>n+1\\) (a short parity argument shows that a line containing \\(m+1\\) points of the triangle would have to contain a point on the hypotenuse \\(a+b=n+1\\) with integer coordinates, which is impossible because the hypotenuse has slope \\(-1\\), a forbidden slope). Consequently the left\u2011hand side of (3) is strictly larger than the right\u2011hand side, a contradiction.\n\nThus the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny leads to a contradiction for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No configuration with }k=n\\text{ exists.}}\n\\]\n\nHence, among the admissible values of \\(k\\) for a given \\(n\\ge 3\\), the maximal possible value is strictly less than \\(n\\). The complete description of all feasible \\(k\\) remains open."
