"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete answer to the problem.\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, whereas **\\(k=n\\) is impossible**.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   *Assume* that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   \u2013 Count the lattice points that must be covered: they are the integer points of the right\u2011angled triangle  \n     \\[\n     T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n     \\]\n     whose cardinality is \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \n   \u2013 Prove a **lemma**: a sunny line can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n     The proof uses the fact that the slope of a sunny line is a rational number \\(\\frac pq\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(q\\ge 2\\); consequently the \\(x\\)-coordinates of lattice points on the line form an arithmetic progression with step \\(q\\), which limits how many of them can lie in the interval \\([1,n]\\).  \n   \u2013 From the lemma the total number of incidences (point\u2013line pairs) is at most  \n     \\[\n     n\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n     \\]\n     Since every point of \\(T_n\\) must be incident to at least one line, we obtain the inequality  \n     \\[\n     n\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n     \\]\n     which simplifies to \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n-1}2\\). This is false for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (the left\u2011hand side is at most \\(\\frac n2\\) while the right\u2011hand side exceeds \\(\\frac n2-\\frac12\\)). Hence the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny leads to a contradiction, and \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\n2. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   We exhibit, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Base family (no sunny lines).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) lines\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y=0,\\\\\n   L_2 &: x=0,\\\\\n   L_3 &: x+y=n+1,\\\\\n   L_4 &: y=1,\\\\\n   L_5 &: y=2,\\\\\n   &\\ \\vdots\\\\\n   L_{n} &: y=n-2 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   The first three lines are the forbidden (non\u2011sunny) directions; the remaining \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines are also non\u2011sunny (they are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies either on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (if \\(a+b=n+1\\)) or on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (if \\(a+b<n+1\\)). Thus the covering condition is fulfilled and the number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *Increasing the number of sunny lines.*  \n   Starting from the above configuration we replace, one by one, horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 1\\)) by sunny lines that still pass through all points with ordinate \\(c\\) that need to be covered.\n\n   For a given integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-2\\) consider the set\n   \\[\n   P_c=\\{(a,c)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-c\\}.\n   \\]\n   The points of \\(P_c\\) lie on the horizontal line \\(y=c\\). Choose a rational slope \\(m_c\\neq 0,-1\\) and define the line\n   \\[\n   S_c:\\; y=m_c x + b_c,\\qquad b_c=c-m_c\\cdot 1 .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(m_c\\) is rational, the line passes through the lattice point \\((1,c)\\); by construction it also passes through \\((a,c)\\) for every integer \\(a\\) such that\n   \\[\n   a = 1 + \\frac{c-b_c}{m_c}=1+\\frac{c-(c-m_c)}{m_c}=1+\\frac{m_c}{m_c}=2,\n   \\]\n   and, more generally, the set of integer points on \\(S_c\\) with \\(x\\)-coordinate between \\(1\\) and \\(n-c\\) is exactly \\(P_c\\) provided we