            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_n=\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid\\exists\\; \\text{distinct lines }L_1,\\dots ,L_n\n\\text{ with exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines and }T_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^n L_i\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=n\\) is impossible\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equation of \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with slope \\(m\\) not equal to \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\n*If \\(m\\) is irrational*, the line contains at most one lattice point, so the bound holds.\n\n*If \\(m\\) is rational*, write \\(m=\\frac pq\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\).  \nIf \\((x_0,y_0)\\) and \\((x_0+t,y_0+mt)\\) are two lattice points on \\(L\\), then \\(t\\) must be a multiple of \\(q\\); otherwise the \\(y\\)-coordinate would not be integral. Hence the set of \\(x\\)-coordinates of lattice points on \\(L\\) is an arithmetic progression with common difference at least \\(q\\).\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(1\\le x\\le n\\). Consequently the number of distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates that can appear on \\(L\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rfloor+1,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(q\\ge2\\) (the cases \\(q=1\\) correspond to integer slopes; the only integer slopes that are forbidden are \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), so any admissible integer slope satisfies \\(|m|\\ge2\\) and again forces a step of at least \\(2\\) in the \\(x\\)-direction). \u220e\n\nNow suppose that all \\(n\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are sunny.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the total number of incidences (pairs \\((P,L_i)\\) with \\(P\\in T_n\\) and \\(P\\in L_i\\)) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}|L_i\\cap T_n|\n\\le n\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, every point of \\(T_n\\) must belong to at least one line, so the left\u2011hand side of (1) is at least \\(|T_n|\\). The cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is obtained by summing, for each possible sum \\(s=a+b\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)), the number of solutions of \\(a+b=s\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\), namely \\(s-1\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nCombining (1) and (2) gives  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(n\\) (recall \\(n\\ge3\\)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\ge\\frac{n+1}2,\n\\qquad\\text{equivalently}\\qquad\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n-1}2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n\\) is even, say \\(n=2m\\), then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\) and (3) becomes \\(m\\ge m-\\tfrac12\\), which is false because the left\u2011hand side is an integer while the right\u2011hand side is not.  \nIf \\(n\\) is odd, \\(n=2m+1\\), then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\) and (3) reads \\(m\\ge m\\), which holds, but the inequality in (1) is in fact strict: a sunny line with slope \\(\\frac pq\\) (\\(q\\ge2\\)) can have at most \\(m\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the bound \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1=m+1\\) is never attained because attaining \\(m+1\\) points would force the line to contain a point on the hypotenuse \\(a+b=n+1\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\), contradicting the sunny condition). Hence the left\u2011hand side of (1) is at most \\(nm\\), which is strictly smaller than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\nThus (1)\u2013(3) cannot be satisfied, and the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny is impossible. Consequently\n\n\\[\nk=n\\notin\\mathcal K_n\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.1  A base configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y=0,\\\\\nL_2 &: x=0,\\\\\nL_3 &: x+y=n+1,\\\\\nL_{3+i} &: y=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n-3).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe first three lines have slopes \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) respectively, hence are **not** sunny. The remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are horizontal, also non\u2011sunny.  \n\nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have two possibilities:\n\n* If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_3\\).\n* If \\(a+b<n+1\\) then \\(b\\le n-2\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), i.e. on \\(L_{3+b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and the number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Turning a horizontal line into a sunny line\n\nFix an integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-2\\).  \nConsider the set of points of \\(T_n\\) having ordinate \\(c\\):\n\n\\[\nP_c=\\{(a,c)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-c\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose the rational slope  \n\n\\[\nm_c=\\frac{1}{n-c+1},\n\\]\n\nand define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_c:\\; y=m_c x + b_c,\\qquad b_c=c-m_c\\cdot 1 = c-\\frac{1}{n-c+1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the slope is rational with denominator \\(n-c+1>1\\), the distance in the \\(x\\)-direction between two consecutive lattice points on \\(S_c\\) is exactly \\(n-c+1\\). Since the interval of admissible \\(x\\)-coordinates for points of \\(P_c\\) is \\([1,n-c]\\), the only lattice points of \\(S_c\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate lies in this interval are the endpoints \\((1,c)\\) and \\((n-c,c)\\); both belong to \\(P_c\\). No other lattice point of \\(S_c\\) can lie inside the strip \\(1\\le x\\le n\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nS_c\\cap T_n = P_c .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(m_c\\) is positive and different from \\(-1\\); hence \\(S_c\\) is a **sunny** line.\n\nThus we may replace the horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (which is non\u2011sunny) by the sunny line \\(S_c\\) without losing any required point.\n\n#### 2.3  Obtaining any prescribed number of sunny lines\n\nSelect any subset \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \\(|C|=k\\) (possible for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  \nFor each \\(c\\in C\\) replace the horizontal line \\(y=c\\) by the sunny line \\(S_c\\) constructed above; for each \\(c\\notin C\\) keep the original horizontal line \\(y=c\\). Together with the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=0\\), \\(x=0\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\) we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_c\\mid c\\in C\\}\\) are sunny. As argued, the union of all these lines still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThis yields configurations for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 2.4  The case \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nTake \\(C=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\); i.e. replace **all** horizontal lines by the corresponding sunny lines \\(S_c\\). The remaining two non\u2011sunny lines are \\(y=0\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\). The total number of lines is still \\(n\\) and exactly \\(n-1\\) of them are sunny. The covering argument of \u00a72.2 shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies either on the hypotenuse line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (if \\(a+b=n+1\\)) or on the unique sunny line \\(S_c\\) with \\(c=b\\) (if \\(a+b<n+1\\)). Hence the condition is satisfied for \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists (Lemma\u202f1 and the counting argument).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) an explicit construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is possible.\n\nTherefore, for any integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal K_n=\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
