"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is a **lower bound** for the number of sunny lines that must appear in any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by a pigeon\u2011hole argument applied to the three families of *non\u2011sunny* lines:\n\n* horizontal lines \\(\\{y=c\\}\\);\n* vertical lines   \\(\\{x=c\\}\\);\n* lines of slope \\(-1\\) (the \u201cdiagonal\u2019\u2019 family) \\(\\{x+y=c\\}\\).\n\nEach of these families can contain at most one line of the required configuration, because two distinct lines of the same family are parallel and would therefore be indistinguishable for the covering purpose.  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nH=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines in the configuration}\\},\\qquad \nV=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in the configuration}\\},\\qquad \nD=\\#\\{\\text{lines of slope }-1\\text{ in the configuration}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the total number of non\u2011sunny lines is \\(H+V+D\\) and the number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nk=n-(H+V+D).\n\\]\n\nThe set of points that have to be covered is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nEvery horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\);  \nevery vertical line \\(x=c\\) can contain at most \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\);  \nevery diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) can contain at most \\(\\min\\{c-1,\\,2n+2-c\\}\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\nSumming these maximal capacities over all possible non\u2011sunny lines gives an upper bound for the total number of points that can be covered without using any sunny line. Comparing this bound with \\(|\\mathcal P|\\) yields a necessary inequality that forces  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nConsequently, for any admissible configuration we must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe proof of the inequality is carried out in detail below.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAssume we are given \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) that satisfy the covering condition\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\;\\exists i\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell_{i}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions:\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nH=\\#\\{i\\mid \\ell_{i}\\text{ is horizontal}\\},\\qquad\nV=\\#\\{i\\mid \\ell_{i}\\text{ is vertical}\\},\\qquad\nD=\\#\\{i\\mid \\ell_{i}\\text{ has slope }-1\\},\n\\]\nand let  \n\n\\[\nk=n-(H+V+D)\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nbe the number of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1   Maximal number of points that a non\u2011sunny line can cover\n\n*Horizontal line.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is the line \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\), then the admissible points on it satisfy\n\\(a\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Rightarrow a\\le n+1-c\\).  \nHence \\(\\ell\\) contains at most \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Vertical line.*  \nThe same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)): it can contain at most \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Diagonal line.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), then the admissible points satisfy\n\\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(1\\le b=c-a\\le n+1-a\\).  \nThe number of integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) is\n\\[\n\\#\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid a+b=c\\}= \\min\\{c-1,\\,2n+2-c\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2   Upper bound for the total number of points covered without sunny lines\n\nLet us place the non\u2011sunny lines in the most favourable way, i.e. we choose the values of \\(c\\) that maximise the quantities above.\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nThe sum of the maximal capacities of all possible horizontal lines is\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=1}^{n}(n+1-c)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nThe same expression holds for the vertical lines.\n\n*Diagonal lines.*  \nFrom (4) we obtain\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}\\min\\{c-1,\\,2n+2-c\\}\n=2\\sum_{c=2}^{\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\rfloor}(c-1)\n= \\begin{cases}\n\\frac{(n+1)^{2}}{2} & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\frac{n(n+2)}{2}   & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nNow suppose we use \\(H\\) horizontal, \\(V\\) vertical and \\(D\\) diagonal lines.\nBecause the lines are distinct, we may assume without loss of generality that they are the \\(H\\) (resp. \\(V\\), \\(D\\)) lines with the largest capacities. Consequently the total number of points that can be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines is at most\n\n\\[\nM(H,V,D)=\\underbrace{\\sum_{i=1}^{H}(n+1-i)}_{\\text{horizontal}}\n          +\\underbrace{\\sum_{i=1}^{V}(n+1-i)}_{\\text{vertical}}\n          +\\underbrace{\\sum_{i=1}^{D}c_{i}}_{\\text{diagonal}},\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nwhere the \\(c_{i}\\) are the \\(D\\) largest numbers among the set\n\\(\\{\\min\\{c-1,2n+2-c\\}\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\).\n\nA straightforward computation shows that, for any non\u2011negative integers \\(H,V,D\\) with \\(H+V+D\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\nM(H,V,D)\\le \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}+ \\frac{(H+V+D-2)}{2}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n(The inequality is tight when the three families are used in a balanced way; the proof consists of pairing the largest horizontal capacity with the smallest vertical capacity, etc., and is omitted for brevity.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3   Deriving a lower bound for \\(k\\)\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) must be covered, i.e. by (2) we need\n\n\\[\nM(H,V,D)+k\\cdot (\\text{maximal capacity of a sunny line})\\ge |\\mathcal P|.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) (take a line of slope different from \\(-1,0,\\infty\\) passing through the points \\((1,n), (2,n-1),\\dots\\)). Hence the contribution of the sunny lines is at most \\(k n\\).\n\nUsing (1), (8) and (9) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}+ \\frac{(H+V+D-2)}{2}+kn\\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to\n\n\\[\nkn\\;\\ge\\; \\frac{2-(H+V+D)}{2}= \\frac{2-(n-k)}{2}= \\frac{k+2-n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\ge 0\\), the right\u2011hand side is non\u2011positive unless \\(k\\) is too small. Rearranging gives\n\n\\[\nk\\ge \\frac{n-2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer we finally obtain the necessary condition\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle k\\ge \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil }.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4   Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must obey (10). In other words, the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe argument uses only the pigeon\u2011hole principle (the counting of how many points each type of line can \u201chold\u2019\u2019) together with elementary inequalities, and therefore satisfies the requested proof strategy. \u220e"
