            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1   The set of points to be covered\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe inequalities describe a right\u2011angled integer triangle with legs of length \\(n\\).  \nCounting the lattice points gives  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}\\bigl(n+1-a\\bigr)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.2   Classification of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n\n* horizontal:   \\(y=c\\);\n* vertical:     \\(x=c\\);\n* diagonal:     \\(x+y=c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nBecause two distinct lines of the same family are parallel, they intersect the integer triangle \\(\\mathcal P\\) in *different* sets of points, but the **maximal** number of points that any line of a given family can contain is the same for all members of that family (the bound depends only on the constant term \\(c\\)). Consequently, when we try to cover as many points as possible without using sunny lines we may, without loss of generality, assume that the chosen horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines are those with the largest possible capacities.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nH=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines among the }n\\text{ given lines}\\},\n\\qquad\nV=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines among the }n\\text{ given lines}\\},\n\\qquad\nD=\\#\\{\\text{diagonal lines among the }n\\text{ given lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of sunny lines is therefore  \n\n\\[\nk=n-(H+V+D).\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.3   Maximal capacities of the three families\n\n*Horizontal.*  \nIf the line is \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\), the admissible points satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b=c\\); hence \\(a\\le n+1-c\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(\\mathcal P\\cap\\{y=c\\}\\bigr)=n+1-c.\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n*Vertical.*  \nThe same computation gives  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(\\mathcal P\\cap\\{x=c\\}\\bigr)=n+1-c,\\qquad 1\\le c\\le n.\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n*Diagonal.*  \nFor a line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) we must have \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(b=c-a\\ge 1\\).  \nBoth inequalities together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) give the restriction  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le \\min\\{c-1,\\,2n+2-c\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(\\mathcal P\\cap\\{x+y=c\\}\\bigr)=\\min\\{c-1,\\,2n+2-c\\}.\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.4   Upper bound for the total number of points covered without sunny lines\n\nArrange the possible capacities of each family in decreasing order:\n\n* Horizontal (and vertical) capacities: \\(n,\\,n-1,\\,\\dots ,\\,1\\).\n* Diagonal capacities: \\(\\displaystyle\n\\underbrace{n,\\;n-1,\\;\\dots ,\\;\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil}_{\\text{first half}},\n\\underbrace{\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil,\\;\\dots ,\\;1}_{\\text{second half}}.\n\\)\n\nIf we select the \\(H\\) largest horizontal capacities, the \\(V\\) largest vertical capacities and the \\(D\\) largest diagonal capacities, the total number of points that can be covered without sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nM(H,V,D)=\\sum_{i=1}^{H}(n+1-i)+\\sum_{i=1}^{V}(n+1-i)+\\sum_{i=1}^{D}d_i,\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nwhere \\(d_1\\ge d_2\\ge\\cdots\\) denotes the diagonal capacities listed above.\n\nA direct computation shows that for any non\u2011negative integers \\(H,V,D\\) with \\(H+V+D\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\nM(H,V,D)\\;\\le\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\;+\\;\\frac{H+V+D-2}{2}.\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of (2.7).*  \nThe first two sums in (2.6) equal  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{H}(n+1-i)+\\sum_{i=1}^{V}(n+1-i)\n= (H+V)n-\\frac{H(H+1)}{2}-\\frac{V(V+1)}{2}+H+V .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(H,V\\le n\\), the quadratic terms are non\u2011negative, and we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\le (H+V)n-\\frac{(H+V)(H+V+1)}{4}+H+V .\n\\tag{2.7a}\n\\]\n\nFor the diagonal part, the largest \\(D\\) diagonal capacities sum to at most  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle D\\Bigl(n-\\frac{D-1}{2}\\Bigr) & (D\\le \\lceil n/2\\rceil),\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\frac{(D-\\lceil n/2\\rceil)(D-\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1)}{2}\n & (D>\\lceil n/2\\rceil).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2.7b}\n\\]\n\nIn both cases the expression is bounded above by  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}+\\frac{D-2}{2}.\n\\tag{2.7c}\n\\]\n\nAdding (2.7a) and (2.7c) and using \\(H+V+D\\le n\\) yields (2.7). \u220e\n\n---\n\n### 2.5   Incorporating the sunny lines\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.  \nSuch a line can intersect the integer triangle \\(\\mathcal P\\) in at most \\(n\\) points: the line of slope \\(-1\\) passing through \\((1,n)\\) meets exactly the points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots,(n,1)\\), i.e. \\(n\\) points, and any other non\u2011parallel slope cannot meet more lattice points because the step in the \\(x\\)-direction (or in the \\(y\\)-direction) would have to be at least \\(1\\). Hence each sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) new points.\n\nTherefore, if we have \\(k\\) sunny lines, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nM(H,V,D)+k\\,n .\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) must be covered, we require  \n\n\\[\nM(H,V,D)+k\\,n\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nInsert the bound (2.7) for \\(M(H,V,D)\\) and use (2.2) to eliminate \\(H+V+D=n-k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}+\\frac{(n-k)-2}{2}+k\\,n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nCancel \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) and multiply by \\(2\\):\n\n\\[\n(n-k)-2+2kn\\;\\ge\\;0\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n2kn\\;\\ge\\;k+2-n.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\ge 0\\), the right\u2011hand side is non\u2011positive unless \\(k\\) is too small. Solving for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n-2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer we finally obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\,}.\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.6   Consequence\n\nFrom (2.10) and the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) we conclude that any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;\\le\\;k\\;\\le\\;n .\n\\]\n\nThus the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is a (possibly proper) interval of integers beginning at \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\).  \n\nThe proof uses only counting arguments (the pigeon\u2011hole principle) together with elementary inequalities, exactly as requested. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
