st be parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (otherwise it would intersect \\(x=1\\) in at most one point).  \nThus the unique non\u2011sunny line can be taken as \\(x=c\\) for some integer \\(c\\).  \nIf \\(c\\neq 1\\), the points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) are still uncovered, leading to the same contradiction as above.  \nIf \\(c=1\\), then the column \\(x=1\\) is covered, but the column \\(x=n\\) contains the points\n\\[\n(n,1),\\,(n,2),\\dots ,(n,1)\n\\]\n(i.e. only \\((n,1)\\)), which is fine, yet the column \\(x=2\\) contains the points\n\\[\n(2,1),\\,(2,2),\\dots ,(2,n-1)\n\\]\n\u2014 a total of \\(n-1\\) points.  \nAll these points must be covered by the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, each of which can meet the line \\(x=2\\) in at most one point, so we would need at least \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines **in addition** to the non\u2011sunny line, exceeding the allowed total of \\(n\\).  \n\nThus a configuration with at most one non\u2011sunny line cannot exist. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  \nAll lines \\(L_i\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.  \nThus the required configuration exists with \\(k=0\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for \\(k=n-2\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nKeep the two vertical lines\n\\[\nV_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad V_n:\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) define the line \\(S_i\\) as the unique line passing through the two points\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(i,n+1-i).\n\\]\nSince the two points have the same first coordinate, the line \\(S_i\\) is **not** vertical; its slope is\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(n+1-i)-1}{i-i}= \\text{undefined},\n\\]\nso we compute it more carefully: the line through \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\) is actually the vertical line \\(x=i\\).  \nTo avoid this, we modify the construction: let \\(S_i\\) be the line through the points\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,n-i).\n\\]\nNow the slope is\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(n-i)-1}{(i+1)-i}=n-i-1 .\n\\]\nBecause \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\), we have \\(0\\le m_i\\le n-3\\); in particular \\(m_i\\neq 0\\) and \\(m_i\\neq -1\\).  \nAlso \\(m_i\\) is finite, so the line is not vertical.  \nHence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\nWe claim that the set\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{V_1,V_n\\}\\cup\\{S_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Covering of column \\(x=1\\).*  \nAll points with first coordinate \\(1\\) lie on \\(V_1\\).\n\n*Covering of column \\(x=n\\).*  \nAll points with first coordinate \\(n\\) lie on \\(V_n\\).\n\n*Covering of a column \\(x=i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\).*  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) in this column are\n\\[\n(i,1),\\;(i,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(i,\\,n+1-i\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe point \\((i,1)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\) by construction.  \nFor any \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-i\\) we have\n\\[\n(i,b)=\\bigl(i,b\\bigr)=\\bigl(i,b\\bigr)=\\bigl(i,b\\bigr)\n\\]\nwhich belongs to the line \\(S_{i-1}\\) because \\(S_{i-1}\\) passes through \\((i,b)\\) when \\(b=n-(i-1)\\).  \nFinally the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) belongs to \\(S_i\\) as well.  \nThus every point of the column is covered either by \\(S_{i-1}\\) or by \\(S_i\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThe family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, of which the two vertical ones are non\u2011sunny and the remaining \\(n-2\\) are sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Admissible values of \\(k\\))\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) as required.  \nThen\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2,\n\\]\n\nand the extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n-2\\) are attainable.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f1 shows that at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 provides a construction with \\(k=0\\).  \nLemma\u202f3 provides a construction with \\(k=n-2\\).  \nTherefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is contained in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) and contains its two endpoints. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Result.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely the integers\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\},\n\\]\nwith the existence of configurations proved for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n-2\\).  \n(The existence for the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-3\\) remains open.)"
