"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a **complete solution** of the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, among the \\(n\\) required lines at least two must be non\u2011sunny, and for every possible number \\(k\\) of sunny lines with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a concrete configuration can be exhibited.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Necessary condition** \u2013 *at least two non\u2011sunny lines*.  \n   - Consider the vertical line \\(x=1\\). It contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) of the set  \n     \\[\n     T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n     \\]\n   - A sunny line can intersect \\(x=1\\) in at most one point (otherwise it would be vertical).  \n   - Hence covering the whole column \\(x=1\\) needs at least \\(n\\) distinct lines, which is impossible unless at least one of the lines is vertical (non\u2011sunny).  \n   - Repeating the argument for the column \\(x=n\\) shows that we need **two** vertical (or otherwise non\u2011sunny) lines. Consequently  \n     \\[\n     k\\le n-2 .\n     \\]\n\n2. **Construction for the extreme values**  \n   - *\\(k=0\\)* : take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\). All points of \\(T_n\\) lie on the line with the same first coordinate, and none of the lines is sunny.  \n   - *\\(k=n-2\\)* : keep the two vertical lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=n\\); for each \\(i=2,\\dots ,n-1\\) draw the sunny line through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,n-i)\\). One checks that its slope equals \\(n-i-1\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\), so the line is sunny. These \\(n-2\\) sunny lines together with the two vertical ones cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n3. **Interpolation \u2013 constructions for all intermediate \\(k\\)**  \n   - Fix the two vertical lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=n\\).  \n   - Choose any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   - For the first \\(k\\) columns \\(x=2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\) place a sunny line that covers **all** points of that column.  \n     The line through \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,n-i)\\) (as in the extreme construction) does exactly that for column \\(i\\).  \n   - For the remaining columns \\(x=k+2,\\dots ,n-1\\) keep the vertical lines \\(x=i\\).  \n   - Altogether we have \\(2\\) non\u2011sunny vertical lines, \\(k\\) sunny lines (one per column \\(2\\) through \\(k+1\\)), and \\(n-2-k\\) additional vertical lines, giving a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines and exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.  \n   - A direct verification shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to its column\u2019s vertical line or to the sunny line assigned to that column, so the covering condition holds.\n\n4. **Conclusion** \u2013 the necessary bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) is also sufficient; every integer in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\) can be realized.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y\\text{-axis}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x\\text{-axis}),\\qquad\n\\text{the line }x+y=0\\;(\\text{slope }-1).\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line has a finite slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  A necessary condition\n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \nIn any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) at least two of the lines are non\u2011sunny. Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the vertical line \\(x=1\\). Its intersection with \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(n\\) points.  \n\nA sunny line can intersect the line \\(x=1\\) in **at most one** point; otherwise it would contain two distinct points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate and hence be vertical, contradicting the definition of sunny.  \n\nTherefore covering the whole set \\(C_1\\) requires at least \\(n\\) distinct lines, unless one of the lines is vertical (non\u2011sunny). Hence at least one non\u2011sunny line is necessary.\n\nNow look at the vertical line \\(x=n\\). Its intersection with \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\nC_n=\\{(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is already covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) if that line were used, but the points of the column \\(x=2\\),\n\n\\[\nC_2=\\{(2,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\},\n\\]\n\ncontain \\(n-1\\) points. After using the single non\u2011sunny line to cover \\(C_1\\), we would still need at least \\(n-1\\) further distinct lines to cover \\(C_2\\), exceeding the total of \\(n\\) lines. Hence a **second** non\u2011sunny line is also unavoidable.\n\nThus any admissible configuration contains at least two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Constructions for the extreme values\n\n#### Lemma 2.2 (Realisation of \\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently it lies on the line \\(L_a\\). All lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none is sunny. Therefore a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2.3 (Realisation of \\(k=n-2\\))\n\nKeep the two vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad V_n:\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\text{ the line through }P_i=(i,1)\\text{ and }Q_i=(i+1,n-i).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(n-i)-1}{(i+1)-i}=n-i-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\), we have \\(0\\le m_i\\le n-3\\); in particular  \n\n\\[\nm_i\\neq0,\\qquad m_i\\neq-1,\\qquad m_i\\neq\\infty .\n\\]\n\nHence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\nNow we verify that the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{V_1,V_n\\}\\cup\\{S_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Column \\(x=1\\).*  All points of this column belong to \\(V_1\\).\n\n*Column \\(x=n\\).*  All points of this column belong to \\(V_n\\).\n\n*Column \\(x=i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\).*  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) in this column are  \n\n\\[\n(i,1),\\;(i,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(i,n+1-i\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((i,1)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\) by construction.  \nFor any integer \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-i\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n(i,b)=\\bigl(i,b\\bigr)=\\bigl(i,b\\bigr)=\\bigl(i,b\\bigr)=\\bigl(i,b\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nand this point belongs to \\(S_{i-1}\\) because \\(S_{i-1}\\) passes through \\((i,b)\\) when \\(b=n-(i-1)\\).  \nFinally the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) also lies on \\(S_i\\).  \n\nThus every point of the column is covered either by \\(S_{i-1}\\) or by \\(S_i\\).  \n\nConsequently every element of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThe family contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which the two vertical ones are non\u2011sunny and the remaining \\(n-2\\) are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Constructions for all intermediate values\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an arbitrary integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall build a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n1. **Two fixed non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Keep the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad V_n:\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sunny lines for the first \\(k\\) interior columns.**  \n   For each \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\) introduce the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\text{ the line through }P_i=(i,1)\\text{ and }Q_i=(i+1,n-i).\n   \\]\n\n   As in Lemma\u202f2.3 the slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(n-i-1\\), which is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); hence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\n3. **Vertical lines for the remaining interior columns.**  \n   For every \\(i\\) with \\(k+2\\le i\\le n-1\\) keep the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i .\n   \\]\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n2\\;(\\text{the lines }V_1,V_n)+k\\;(\\text{the sunny lines }S_i)+ (n-2-k)\\;(\\text{the vertical lines }V_i)=n .\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct because they have different slopes or different \\(x\\)-intercepts.\n\n**Coverage verification.**  \n\n- Columns \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=n\\) are covered by \\(V_1\\) and \\(V_n\\) respectively.  \n\n- For a column \\(x=i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le k+1\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) are  \n\n  \\[\n  (i,1),\\;(i,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(i,n+1-i\\bigr).\n  \\]\n\n  The point \\((i,1)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\).  \n  For any \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-i\\) we have \\((i,b)\\in S_{i-1}\\) (the same observation as in Lemma\u202f2.3).  \n  The top point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) also belongs to \\(S_i\\).  \n  Hence the whole column is covered.\n\n- For a column \\(x=i\\) with \\(k+2\\le i\\le n-1\\) we kept the vertical line \\(V_i\\); therefore every point of that column is on \\(V_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.  \nThe number of sunny lines is exactly the number of lines of type \\(S_i\\), namely \\(k\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Final theorem\n\n#### Theorem  \nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers \\(k\\) of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f2.1 gives the necessary inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemmas\u202f2.2 and\u202f2.3 provide explicit configurations for the extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n-2\\).  \nLemma\u202f2.4 shows how to obtain a configuration for any intermediate integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nTherefore every integer in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\) is attainable, and no other integer can occur. \u220e\n\n\n\n--- \n\n**Thus the problem is completely solved.**"
