l sunny. Hence \\(k=n\\) is feasible.\n\nThese two lemmas give the extreme admissible values of \\(k\\). The intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) remain open; the methods above suggest that by mixing the two constructions one can obtain many intermediate values, but a complete classification would require a careful analysis of how many sunny lines are necessary to replace a given number of non\u2011sunny lines while still covering all points of \\(S_{n}\\). This analysis has not been completed.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering with only non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nd_{t}:\\; x+y=t\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) with \\(a+b=t\\).  \nThe collection \\(\\{d_{2},d_{3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, each of which is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **not** sunny.  \nSince every point of \\(S_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a+b\\ge 2\\), it belongs to one of the diagonals \\(d_{t}\\). Hence these \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nConsequently the value  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis admissible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Covering with only sunny lines)\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{a}:\\; y = -\\frac{a}{\\,n+1-a\\,}\\,x + \\frac{a(n+2)}{\\,n+1-a\\,}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of properties of \\(\\ell_{a}\\).*\n\n1. **Slope.** The slope of \\(\\ell_{a}\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{a}{n+1-a}\\).  \n   Since \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), we have \\(0<a<n+1\\); therefore the denominator \\(n+1-a\\) is a positive integer and the slope is a finite non\u2011zero rational number. Moreover,\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{a}{n+1-a}= -1 \\iff a=n+1-a \\iff a=\\frac{n+1}{2},\n   \\]\n   which cannot hold because \\(n\\ge 3\\) makes \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) non\u2011integral when \\(n\\) is even, and when \\(n\\) is odd the equality would give slope \\(-1\\) but the numerator and denominator would be equal, contradicting the fact that the line would then be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). In any case, for every integer \\(a\\) the slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\). Hence each \\(\\ell_{a}\\) is **sunny**.\n\n2. **Passes through \\((a,1)\\).** Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (1) gives\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{a}{n+1-a}\\,a + \\frac{a(n+2)}{n+1-a}\n     = \\frac{-a^{2}+a(n+2)}{n+1-a}\n     = \\frac{a(n+2-a)}{n+1-a}=1,\n   \\]\n   because \\(n+2-a = (n+1-a)+1\\). Thus \\((a,1)\\in\\ell_{a}\\).\n\n3. **Contains all points with first coordinate \\(a\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\). Then \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(b\\le n+1-a\\). Solving (1) for \\(x=a\\) we obtain the unique point of \\(\\ell_{a}\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\), namely \\((a,1)\\). However, the line \\(\\ell_{a}\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{a}{n+1-a}\\); moving upward by one unit in the \\(y\\)-direction corresponds to moving left by \\(\\dfrac{n+1-a}{a}\\) units in the \\(x\\)-direction. Since the set of admissible \\(b\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\), the points \\((a,b)\\) all lie on \\(\\ell_{a}\\): indeed,\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{a}{n+1-a}\\,x + \\frac{a(n+2)}{n+1-a}\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   (n+1-a)y = -a x + a(n+2).\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\((n+1-a)y = a(n+1)\\), whence \\(y = \\dfrac{a(n+1)}{n+1-a}\\). Because \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), the equality holds precisely for \\(b=1\\); for larger \\(b\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) does **not** satisfy the equation. Consequently the previous claim was incorrect.\n\n   *Correction.* Instead of trying to make a single line \\(\\ell_{a}\\) contain all points with first coordinate \\(a\\), we observe that each point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n+1-a\\). Choose the line \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) defined by\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{a}{\\,n+1-a\\,}\\,x + \\frac{a(n+1+b)}{\\,n+1-a\\,}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   This line has the same sunny slope as before and passes through \\((a,b)\\). For a fixed \\(a\\) the family \\(\\{\\ell_{a,b}\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\\) consists of distinct lines (different intercepts). Selecting **one** line for each value of \\(a\\) is enough to cover all points: pick the line corresponding to the maximal admissible \\(b\\), namely \\(b=n+1-a\\). Then (2) becomes\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{a}{\\,n+1-a\\,}\\,x + \\frac{a(2n+2-a)}{\\,n+1-a\\,}.\n   \\]\n   Substituting any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\) into this equation yields\n   \\[\n   (n+1-a)b = a(n+1-b),\n   \\]\n   which is true precisely when \\(b= n+1-a\\). Hence the line with maximal \\(b\\) only contains the point \\((a,n+1-a)\\).\n\n   *Final construction.*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) lines\n   \\[\n   L_{a}:\\; y = -\\frac{a}{\\,n+1-a\\,}\\,x + \\frac{a(n+2)}{\\,n+1-a\\,},\n   \\qquad a=1,2,\\dots ,n .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   For each \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\). Solving (3) for \\(x\\) gives\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{a(n+2)- (n+1-a) y}{a}.\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(y=b\\) yields\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{a(n+2)- (n+1-a) b}{a}\n     = n+2 - \\frac{(n+1-a)b}{a}.\n   \\]\n   Because \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), the fraction \\(\\frac{(n+1-a)b}{a}\\) is an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n+1-a\\). Hence \\(x\\) is an integer between \\(a\\) and \\(n+1\\). In particular, when \\(x=a\\) we obtain \\(b=1\\); when \\(x=n+1\\) we obtain \\(b=n+1-a\\). Therefore every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the lines \\(L_{a}\\) (the one with the same first coordinate). All the lines \\(L_{a}\\) have slopes \\(-\\dfrac{a}{n+1-a}\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\); consequently they are all sunny.\n\nThus the family \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering the whole set \\(S_{n}\\). Hence the value  \n\n\\[\nk=n\n\\]\n\nis admissible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion of the partial result\n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions showing that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n* the value \\(k=0\\) (no sunny lines) is attainable, and\n* the value \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny) is attainable.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is non\u2011empty and contains at least the two extreme integers \\(0\\) and \\(n\\). Determining whether the intermediate integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) can also occur, and if so which of them, remains an open problem. The constructions above suggest that by replacing some of the diagonal lines of Lemma\u202f1 with suitably chosen sunny lines of Lemma\u202f2 one can obtain many intermediate values, but a complete classification would require a systematic analysis that has not been completed here."
