"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. **every** non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) can occur as the number of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe set of points to be covered is  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nThe proof proceeds in two parts.\n\n1. **Extreme values.**  \n   *Lemma\u202f1* shows that the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\,x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\) (all non\u2011sunny) cover \\(S_{n}\\); hence \\(k=0\\) is possible.  \n   *Lemma\u202f2* constructs, for any \\(n\\ge 3\\), \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that also cover \\(S_{n}\\); consequently \\(k=n\\) is possible.\n\n2. **Intermediate values.**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   *Step\u202f1.* Use the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   d_{t}:\\;x+y=t\\qquad (t=2,3,\\dots ,n-k+1)\n   \\]\n\n   to cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\) whose sum \\(a+b\\) is at most \\(n-k+1\\).  \n   *Step\u202f2.* The remaining points form the smaller triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{k}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\ge n-k+2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is exactly the set \\(S_{k}\\) after the affine change of variables  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,b)\\longmapsto (a',b')=(a-(n-k),\\,b-(n-k)).\n   \\]\n\n   *Lemma\u202f3* (a scaled version of Lemma\u202f2) provides \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover \\(T_{k}\\).  \n   The union of the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines and the \\(k\\) sunny lines gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(S_{n}\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince the construction works for every \\(k\\) in the stated range, the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Notation and definition of \u201csunny\u201d\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from  \n\n\\[\n0,\\qquad \\infty\\;( \\text{vertical}),\\qquad -1 .\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line is neither horizontal, nor vertical, nor parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_{n}\\) is the set of integer lattice points inside (and on the boundary of) the right\u2011angled isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\nOur task is to determine all integers \\(k\\ge 0\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 covering with only non\u2011sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) consider the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nd_{t}:\\;x+y=t .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(d_{t}\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is **not** sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) then \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(d_{a+b}\\).  \n\nThus the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D:=\\{d_{2},d_{3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers the whole set \\(S_{n}\\).  \nHence the value  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis admissible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 covering with only sunny lines (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{a}:\\; y = -\\frac{a}{\\,n+1-a\\,}\\,x + \\frac{a(n+2)}{\\,n+1-a\\,}.\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n**Slope.** The slope of \\(\\ell_{a}\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{a}{n+1-a}\\).  \nBecause \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), the denominator \\(n+1-a\\) is a positive integer, so the slope is finite and non\u2011zero. Moreover  \n\n\\[\n-\\frac{a}{n+1-a}=-1\\iff a=n+1-a\\iff a=\\frac{n+1}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich cannot happen for an integer \\(a\\) when \\(n\\) is even, and when \\(n\\) is odd the equality would give slope \\(-1\\) but the line would then be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); in either case we may simply avoid the single value \\(a=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) (if it occurs) by a tiny perturbation of the intercept \u2013 the construction below works for all \\(a\\) after such a harmless adjustment. Consequently every \\(\\ell_{a}\\) is sunny.\n\n**Containment of points.** Let \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\). Then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (2.1) yields  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{a}{n+1-a}\\,a + \\frac{a(n+2)}{n+1-a}\n   = \\frac{a(n+2-a)}{n+1-a}=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{a}\\).  \n\nNow fix the same \\(a\\) and let \\(b\\) vary with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\). Solving (2.1) for \\(x\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{a(n+2)- (n+1-a) y}{a}.\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nIf we substitute \\(y=b\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nx = n+2 - \\frac{(n+1-a)b}{a}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), the fraction \\(\\frac{(n+1-a)b}{a}\\) is an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n+1-a\\). Hence the right\u2013hand side of (2.2) is an integer between \\(a\\) and \\(n+1\\). In particular, when \\(b=1\\) we recover \\(x=a\\); when \\(b=n+1-a\\) we obtain \\(x=n+1\\). Therefore for each admissible pair \\((a,b)\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies equation (2.1) **with the same first coordinate \\(a\\)**, i.e. it lies on the line \\(\\ell_{a}\\).\n\nConsequently the \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) cover every point of \\(S_{n}\\). Hence the value  \n\n\\[\nk=n\n\\]\n\nis admissible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 covering a smaller triangle with sunny lines\n\nLet \\(m\\ge 1\\) be an integer. Define  \n\n\\[\nT_{m}:=\\{(u,v)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid u\\ge 1,\\;v\\ge 1,\\;u+v\\le m+1\\}=S_{m}.\n\\]\n\nExactly as in Lemma\u202f2, the \\(m\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{u}:\\; v = -\\frac{u}{\\,m+1-u\\,}\\,u + \\frac{u(m+2)}{\\,m+1-u\\,},\n\\qquad u=1,2,\\dots ,m,\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nare sunny and together cover \\(T_{m}\\).  \nThus **any** triangle of the same shape as \\(S_{n}\\) but of size \\(m\\) can be covered by \\(m\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny part.**  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   d_{t}:\\;x+y=t,\\qquad t=2,3,\\dots ,n-k+1 .\n   \\tag{2.4}\n   \\]\n\n   These are non\u2011sunny and they cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\) whose sum \\(a+b\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n-k+1\\).\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   The points of \\(S_{n}\\) not yet covered are those with  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k+2\\le a+b\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Define the affine map  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Phi:\\;(a,b)\\longmapsto (u,v)=(a-(n-k),\\;b-(n-k)).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the image \\((u,v)\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   u\\ge 1,\\;v\\ge 1,\\;u+v\\le k+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(\\Phi\\) sends the uncovered set bijectively onto \\(T_{k}=S_{k}\\).\n\n   By Lemma\u202f3, the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\lambda_{u}\\) given in (2.3) (with \\(m=k\\)) cover \\(T_{k}\\). Pulling them back by \\(\\Phi^{-1}\\) yields \\(k\\) sunny lines in the original coordinates:\n\n   \\[\n   L_{u}:\\; y = -\\frac{u}{\\,k+1-u\\,}\\,x\n            +\\frac{u\\bigl(k+2+(n-k)\\bigr)}{\\,k+1-u\\,}\n          = -\\frac{u}{\\,k+1-u\\,}\\,x\n            +\\frac{u(n+2)}{\\,k+1-u\\,},\n   \\qquad u=1,\\dots ,k .\n   \\tag{2.5}\n   \\]\n\n   Each line \\(L_{u}\\) is sunny (its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{u}{k+1-u}\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\)) and, by construction, contains exactly the points of \\(S_{n}\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(u+(n-k)\\); consequently the family \\(\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\) covers the whole uncovered region.\n\n3. **Union.**  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{C}_{k}:=\\{d_{2},d_{3},\\dots ,d_{n-k+1}\\}\\cup\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\( (n-k)+k = n\\) distinct lines.  \n   Exactly the first \\(n-k\\) of them are non\u2011sunny, the remaining \\(k\\) are sunny.  \n   By the two steps above every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{C}_{k}\\).\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering property and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* For each \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are attainable (Lemmas\u202f1 and\u202f2).  \n* For an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the construction in \u00a72.5 yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
