au(L_{i})\\le \\tau(L^{*})+(n-1)\\,m,\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   where \\(m\\) denotes the maximal number of required points that can lie on a *non\u2011sunny* line (the same bound holds for any line, but we will use a sharper bound for non\u2011sunny lines).\n\n4. **Bounding \\(m\\).**  \n   - A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets the triangle only for \\(1\\le c\\le n\\); it contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points, so \\(m_{\\text{hor}}=n\\).  \n   - A vertical line \\(x=c\\) is symmetric and also contains at most \\(n\\) points.  \n   - A diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) meets the triangle for \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); it contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points, so the maximum is attained at \\(c=n+1\\) and equals \\(n\\).  \n\n   Hence **any** line, sunny or not, can contain at most \\(n\\) of the required points, i.e. \\(m=n\\).\n\n5. **Deriving the lower bound for \\(k\\).**  \n   Suppose that fewer than \\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) of the lines are sunny. Then at least  \n\n   \\[\n   n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   \\]\n\n   lines are non\u2011sunny. Each non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions. Because there are only three such directions, by the pigeon\u2011hole principle one of the directions is used by at least  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{1}{3}\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\Bigr\\rceil\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines. Two distinct parallel lines intersect the triangle in disjoint sets of lattice points, so the total number of points covered by all lines parallel to that direction is at most the sum of the first \\(p\\) positive integers, where \\(p\\) is the number of such parallel lines. A short calculation shows that for \\(p\\ge\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\) this sum is **strictly smaller** than \\(T\\). Consequently the required points cannot all be covered, contradicting the hypothesis.\n\n   Therefore we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rce