ntersections.\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nA line \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) meets the triangle in the points \\((1,c),(2,c),\\dots ,(n+1-c,c)\\); hence it contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points. The sequence \\((n,\\,n-1,\\,\\dots ,1)\\) is obtained when \\(c\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\). Therefore, if we have \\(p\\) distinct horizontal lines, the total number of points they can cover is at most  \n\n\\[\nn+(n-1)+\\dots +(n-p+1)=p\\bigl(2n-p+1\\bigr)/2.\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Vertical lines* give the same bound as (6) by symmetry.\n\n*Diagonal lines.*  \nA line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) meets the triangle in the points \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); thus it contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points. When \\(c\\) runs from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\) we obtain the sequence \\((1,2,\\dots ,n)\\). Hence \\(p\\) distinct diagonal lines can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n1+2+\\dots +p=p(p+1)/2.\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNow suppose, for a contradiction, that  \n\n\\[\nk<\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nThen the number of non\u2011sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\ns=n-k>\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince there are only three forbidden directions, by the pigeon\u2011hole principle at least one of them is used by at least  \n\n\\[\np:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{s}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\\ge\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{1}{3}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{6}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\n*If the direction is horizontal or vertical*, the maximal number of points covered by those \\(p\\) lines is given by (6). A straightforward inequality shows that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(p\\ge\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{p\\bigl(2n-p+1\\bigr)}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|\\mathcal P|.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\ge\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\) follows from (8), (10) tells us that the horizontal (or vertical) family alone cannot cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\)