            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1. Preliminaries  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.  \nThe points form a right\u2011angled triangular array of side length \\(n\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor a line \\(L\\) write  \n\n\\[\n\\tau(L)=|\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid (a,b)\\in L\\}|\n\\]\n\n(the number of required points that belong to \\(L\\)).  \n\nA line is **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{(i)}\\;x\\text{-axis},\\qquad \n\\text{(ii)}\\;y\\text{-axis},\\qquad \n\\text{(iii)}\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nA line that is parallel to one of these three directions will be called **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2. Necessity  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and let  \n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{L_i\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L_i\\text{ is sunny}\\},\\qquad \ns=n-k\n\\]\n\nbe the numbers of sunny and non\u2011sunny lines, respectively.\n\n#### 2.2.1. An extremal line  \n\nChoose a line \\(L^{*}\\in\\mathcal L\\) with maximal \\(\\tau\\); set  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\tau(L^{*})=\\max_{L\\in\\mathcal L}\\tau(L). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the convex hull of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is the triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\), any straight line can intersect it in at most \\(n\\) lattice points. Hence  \n\n\\[\nM\\le n. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.2.2. How many points can parallel non\u2011sunny lines cover?  \n\nConsider a fixed forbidden direction.\n\n*Horizontal direction* (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)).  \nFor \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P\\) in the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,c),(2,c),\\dots ,(n+1-c,c),\n\\]\n\nhence contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points.  \nIf we have \\(p\\) distinct horizontal lines, after re\u2011ordering the constants we may assume they are \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=p\\); the total number of points they can cover is therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{p}(n+1-i)=p\\bigl(2n-p+1\\bigr)/2. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Vertical direction* gives the same bound (by symmetry).\n\n*Diagonal direction* (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \nFor \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the line \\(x+y=c\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P\\) in  \n\n\\[\n(1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(c-1\\) points.  \nIf we have \\(p\\) distinct diagonal lines we may assume they are \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=p+1\\); the total number of points they can cover is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{p}i=p(p+1)/2. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.2.3. A lower bound for \\(k\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that  \n\n\\[\nk<\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\ns=n-k>\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince there are only three forbidden directions, by the pigeon\u2011hole principle at least one of them is used by  \n\n\\[\np:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{s}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\\ge\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{1}{3}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{6}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\n*If the direction is horizontal or vertical*, the maximal number of points covered by those \\(p\\) lines is given by (4).  \nBecause \\(p\\ge\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\) (which follows from (6)), a short algebraic check shows\n\n\\[\n\\frac{p\\bigl(2n-p+1\\bigr)}{2}\n\\;<\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|\\mathcal P| . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the left\u2013hand side is a concave quadratic in \\(p\\) attaining its maximum at \\(p=n\\), where it equals \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\); for any \\(p\\) strictly between \\(\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\) and \\(n\\) the value is strictly smaller than \\(|\\mathcal P|\\).\n\n*If the direction is diagonal*, the bound (5) applies, and for every \\(p\\ge\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{p(p+1)}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|\\mathcal P| . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus, in all three cases the collection of lines parallel to the same forbidden direction cannot by itself cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\). Adding the remaining \\(n-p\\) lines can increase the total coverage by at most \\((n-p)M\\le (n-p)n\\). Combining this with (8) or (9) yields a total coverage still strictly less than \\(|\\mathcal P|\\), contradicting the hypothesis that every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one line.\n\nConsequently our assumption was false, and we must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe trivial inequality \\(k\\le n\\) holds because there are only \\(n\\) lines. Hence any admissible \\(k\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n } . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.3. Sufficiency  \n\nWe now show that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\displaystyle\\left\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\nFix such a \\(k\\). Write  \n\n\\[\nr:=n-k\\qquad (0\\le r\\le \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor).\n\\]\n\nWe treat two cases.\n\n#### 2.3.1. Construction of the sunny lines  \n\nConsider the \\(k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lying on the anti\u2011diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD:=\\{(i,\\,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = -x + (n+1) + \\varepsilon_i ,\n\\]\n\nwhere the numbers \\(\\varepsilon_1,\\dots ,\\varepsilon_k\\) are distinct, non\u2011zero, and chosen so small that each line still passes through the lattice point \\((i,n+1-i)\\). Because the slope \\(-1\\) is the forbidden direction, adding a non\u2011zero \\(\\varepsilon_i\\) makes \\(\\ell_i\\) **sunny** (it is no longer parallel to any of the three forbidden directions). The lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct, each contains exactly one point of \\(D\\), and none of them is parallel to a forbidden direction. Hence we have produced exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, each covering one of the points of \\(D\\).\n\n#### 2.3.2. Construction of the remaining \\(r\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nWe must now cover the remaining points \\(\\mathcal P\\setminus D\\).  \nObserve that \\(\\mathcal P\\setminus D\\) consists of the points with \\(a+b\\le n\\) together with the points on the anti\u2011diagonal whose index exceeds \\(k\\). The set is a right\u2011angled triangle of side length \\(n-1\\) together with a (possibly empty) \u201ctail\u2019\u2019 on the anti\u2011diagonal; it can be covered by a small number of horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines as follows.\n\n*If \\(r\\) is even*, write \\(r=2t\\).  \nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=t\n\\]\n\nand the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\n\\]\n\nEach of these lines is parallel to a forbidden direction, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) either has \\(a\\le t\\) (so it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(b\\le t\\) (so it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)). Points on the anti\u2011diagonal with index \\(>k\\) have both coordinates \\(\\ge t+1\\); they are covered because for such a point we have \\(a+b\\le n\\) and therefore at least one of \\(a,b\\) is \\(\\le t\\). Thus all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\setminus D\\) are covered.\n\n*If \\(r\\) is odd*, write \\(r=2t+1\\).  \nProceed as in the even case with the \\(t\\) horizontal and \\(t\\) vertical lines, and add the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nx+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe diagonal line is parallel to the third forbidden direction, so it is non\u2011sunny.  \nNow any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) is covered as before.  \nA point on the anti\u2011diagonal with index \\(>k\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\) and therefore lies on the added diagonal line. Hence again every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered.\n\nIn both sub\u2011cases we have used exactly \\(r\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all distinct, and together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering property.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\displaystyle\\left\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4. Conclusion  \n\nThe necessary condition (11) and the explicit constructions above show that the condition is also sufficient. Therefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;\\le\\;k\\;\\le\\;n } .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
