            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad\n|S_n|=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the line  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(c\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) and \\(a+b=c\\), then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(D_c\\).  \nConversely, any point on \\(D_c\\) has sum \\(c\\le n+1\\), hence belongs to \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll lines \\(D_c\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore their slope is \\(-1\\), a forbidden slope. Distinct constants \\(c\\) give distinct lines, so \\(|\\mathcal D|=n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3  \nA sunny line meets each diagonal line \\(D_c\\) in **at most one** point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct lines intersect in at most one point. Since a sunny line is not parallel to any \\(D_c\\) (its slope is not \\(-1\\)), it is not coincident with any \\(D_c\\); hence the intersection with each \\(D_c\\) is either empty or a single point. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 the set \\(\\mathcal D\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and, by Lemma\u202f1, every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of them.  \nThus the required configuration exists with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of a sunny line (\\(k\\ge1\\))\n\nAssume that a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines.  \nLet the sunny lines be \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) and the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non\u2011sunny.\n\n**Claim 1.** Every non\u2011sunny line must belong to \\(\\mathcal D\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \nIf it is not parallel to the direction of the diagonals (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)), then it meets a diagonal \\(D_c\\) in at most one point (by Lemma\u202f3). Consequently some point of that diagonal would be uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis. Hence each non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the diagonals, i.e. it is of the form \\(x+y=c\\); by Lemma\u202f1 the only such lines that intersect \\(S_n\\) are precisely the \\(D_c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nThus the non\u2011sunny part of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is a sub\u2011family of \\(\\mathcal D\\); denote it by \\(\\mathcal D'\\) with \\(|\\mathcal D'|=n-k\\).\n\nEach diagonal \\(D_c\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\); in particular  \n\n\\[\n|D_c\\cap S_n|=c-1\\le n-1\\le \\frac{n+1}{2}\\quad\\text{for all }c.\n\\]\n\nHence the total number of points covered by the non\u2011sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{D_c\\in\\mathcal D'}|D_c\\cap S_n|\n\\le (n-k)\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n**Claim 2.** A sunny line contains at most \\(