uting into \\(a_c+b_c=c\\) gives \\(b_c = (1-\\lambda)c -\\mu\\). The slope of the line through \\((a_c,b_c)\\) is then \\(\\frac{b_{c+1}-b_c}{a_{c+1}-a_c}= \\frac{1-\\lambda}{\\lambda}\\). For this to equal \\(\\alpha\\neq -1\\) we need \\(\\lambda\\neq \\tfrac12\\). However the integer constraints \\(a_c,b_c\\ge1\\) and \\(a_c+b_c=c\\) force \\(\\lambda=\\tfrac12\\), which yields slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the assumption that the line is sunny. Hence no sunny line can meet every diagonal.\n\nThus the inequality in (2) is strict for any \\(k\\ge1\\), contradicting the fact that all \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points must be covered. Therefore \\(k\\) cannot be positive.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nThe covering condition can be satisfied with \\(n\\) distinct lines, but **all such lines must be non\u2011sunny**. Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
