ed line \\(\\lambda'\\) still passes through at least one of the points formerly on \\(\\lambda\\)\u2026\u201d\n\n#### 3.1 Contradictory premise\n\n- The lemma assumes \\(\\lambda\\) is a **non\u2011sunny** line, i.e. it *is* parallel to one of the forbidden directions.  \n- The proof begins with \u201cBecause \\(\\lambda\\) is **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions\u2026\u201d, which directly contradicts the hypothesis.  \n- **Critical Error:** The argument proceeds on a false premise; the rotation idea is not justified for a line that *is* parallel to a forbidden direction.\n\n#### 3.2 Unjustified assumption about coverage redundancy\n\n- The proof claims: \u201ceach of those points is also incident to at least one other line of\u202f\\(\\mathcal F\\) (otherwise it would become uncovered after the removal of\u202f\\(\\lambda\\)).\u201d  \n- The problem only requires that each point be covered by **at least one** line; it does **not** guarantee that any point is covered by more than one line.  \n- Hence it is possible that a point lies **solely** on the non\u2011sunny line \\(\\lambda\\). Removing \\(\\lambda\\) would then leave that point uncovered, contradicting the claim.  \n- **Critical Error:** The proof assumes a property (every point on \\(\\lambda\\) has a backup line) that need not hold, making the conclusion of Lemma\u202f2 invalid.\n\n#### 3.3 Failure of the rotation argument\n\n- Even if the above redundancy held, rotating \\(\\lambda\\) about a single point would keep that point on the new line, but would generally move the line away from the other points originally on \\(\\lambda\\). The proof asserts that \u201c\\(\\lambda'\\) still passes through at least one of the points formerly on \\(\\lambda\\)\u201d, which is insufficient: all points formerly on \\(\\lambda\\) must remain covered, not just one.  \n- No rigorous argument is provided that a *single* small rotation can preserve coverage of **all** points formerly on \\(\\lambda