eed the denominator \\(p+q\\) to divide the numerator \\(q(c-b)\\).  \nSince \\(\\gcd(p+q,q)=\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), the condition reduces to\n\\[\np+q\\mid (c-b).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line can meet a diagonal \\(D_c\\) in a lattice point **iff** the integer \\(c\\) satisfies (3).  \nThe numbers \\(c\\) that appear in \\(S_n\\) form the consecutive set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); consequently the set\n\\[\n\\{c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid p+q\\mid (c-b)\\}\n\\]\ncontains at most two elements, because any two distinct integers in a length\u2011\\(n\\) interval that are congruent modulo \\(|p+q|\\) differ by at least \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\).  \nHence **a sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).**  \n(If \\(|p+q|>n\\) it may contain none; if \\(|p+q|=1\\) the line would be horizontal, which is excluded.)\n\n*Step\u202f3 (counting argument).*\nSuppose a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nFrom Step\u202f2 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points of \\(S_n\\); from the obvious observation that any non\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or diagonal) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\), we obtain\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le 2k+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nUsing \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) this becomes\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k+(n-k)n\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nk\\le 1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Step\u202f4 (existence of a configuration with \\(k=1\\)).*  \nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) we exhibit an explicit example with exactly one sunny line.\n\nTake the sunny line \\(L:\\;y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \nIt passes through the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\); only \\((1,1)\\) belongs to \\(S_n\\) (the point \\((2,2)\\) lies in \\(S_n\\) iff \\(2+2\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(n\\ge 3\\), but it is not needed).  \nNow cover the remaining points of \\(S_n\\) by the following \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n* for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n+1\\) use the diagonal \\(D_c:x+y=c\\);\n* additionally use the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (or the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)) to cover the points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) that are not on any of the diagonals \\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\ge 3\\).\n\nAltogether we have:\n* the sunny line \\(y=x\\);\n* the vertical line \\(x=1\\);\n* the \\(n-2\\) diagonals \\(x+y=c\\) for \\(c=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\).\n\nThese are \\(1+(1)+(n-2)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them. (A brief verification:  \n\u2013 \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(y=x\\);  \n\u2013 \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) lie on \\(x=1\\);  \n\u2013 any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\ge 3\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\).)\n\nHence a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n*Conclusion.*  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(\\boxed{0\\text{ or }1}\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (A configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(D_c\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in the above range, hence lies on exactly one of the \\(D_c\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (At most two points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  