"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI agree with the bug\u2011report\u2019s assessment that Lemma\u202f2 (and consequently Lemma\u202f4) in the previous answer is incorrect.  \nA sunny line can contain more than two points of the set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nso the bound \\(k\\le 1\\) derived from the false statement \u201ca sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\)\u201d is not justified.  \nConsequently the previous answer does **not** give a complete and rigorous solution of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch for a Correct Solution**  \n\nThe correct solution proceeds in three stages.\n\n1. **Upper bound on the number of points of \\(S_n\\) that a sunny line can contain.**  \n   Let a sunny line be written as \\(y=mx+b\\) with rational slope \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms, \\(q>0\\), and \\(p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).  \n   Intersecting this line with the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) gives the lattice point  \n\n   \\[\n   (x,y)=\\Bigl(\\frac{q(c-b)}{p+q},\\;\\frac{p(c-b)}{p+q}+b\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a diagonal contributes a point of \\(S_n\\) to the line **iff**  \n\n   \\[\n   p+q\\mid (c-b). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The integers \\(c\\) that occur in \\(S_n\\) form the consecutive set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \n   Condition (2) therefore says that the admissible values of \\(c\\) form an arithmetic progression with common difference \\(|p+q|\\).  \n   Consequently the number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can lie on a sunny line is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p+q|}\\bigr\\rfloor\\;+\\;\\varepsilon,\n   \\qquad\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\},\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. at most \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) because \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) for a sunny line (the cases \\(|p+q|=0,1\\) correspond to the forbidden slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   In particular a sunny line can contain **no more than \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).**  \n   This bound is sharp: for even \\(n\\) the line \\(y=-\\frac12x+\\frac{n+2}{2}\\) contains exactly \\(\\frac{n}{2}+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n2. **Counting argument.**  \n   Suppose a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points (Lemma\u202f1).  \n   Any non\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or diagonal) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Therefore\n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;\\le\\;\n   k\\Bigl(\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (4) for \\(k\\) yields\n\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\le\\;\n   \\frac{n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{\\,n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor-1\\,}\n   \\;=\\;\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **any admissible configuration satisfies**  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sharpness \u2013 constructions attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).**  \n\n   *Base construction (no sunny lines, \\(k=0\\)).*  \n   The \\(n\\) diagonals \\(x+y=c\\) for \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) are all parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); they cover \\(S_n\\) and contain no sunny line.\n\n   *Adding sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) we replace the two diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{2t}\\colon x+y=2t,\\qquad D_{2t+1}\\colon x+y=2t+1\n   \\]\n\n   by the single sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t\\colon y=-\\frac12x+\\bigl(t+1\\bigr).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_t\\) passes through the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,2t),\\;(3,2t-1),\\;(5,2t-2),\\dots ,\\bigl(2t-1,\\,t+1\\bigr),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. exactly the \\(\\displaystyle t+1=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2t}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on the two diagonals \\(D_{2t}\\) and \\(D_{2t+1}\\).  \n   Thus the replacement does not lose any point of \\(S_n\\).  \n\n   Performing this replacement for any chosen set of \\(k\\) distinct values of \\(t\\) (with \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covering all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   Consequently **every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) is attainable.**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Maximum number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nWrite a sunny line as  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+b,\\qquad m=\\frac{p}{q}\\;(p,q)=1,\\;q>0,\\;p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIntersect it with a diagonal \\(x+y=c\\).  Solving the system gives  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=\\Bigl(\\frac{q(c-b)}{p+q},\\;\\frac{p(c-b)}{p+q}+b\\Bigr).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus a diagonal contributes a lattice point of \\(S_n\\) to the line **iff**  \n\n\\[\np+q\\mid (c-b). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe admissible values of \\(c\\) in \\(S_n\\) are the consecutive integers  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nso the set \\(\\{c\\in\\mathcal C\\mid (11)\\text{ holds}\\}\\) is an arithmetic progression with step \\(|p+q|\\).  \nHence its cardinality is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{|\\mathcal C|}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is sunny we have \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) (the cases \\(|p+q|=0,1\\) correspond to slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \nTherefore a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).  The bound is attained, for example, by the line  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac12x+\\frac{n+2}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers every point of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nA non\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or diagonal) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nHence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|\n   \\le k\\Bigl(\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (14) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\frac{n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{\\,n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor-1\\,}\n=\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for any admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n1. **Start** with the \\(n\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\;x+y=c,\\qquad c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\tag{17}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover \\(S_n\\) and are all non\u2011sunny.\n\n2. **Replace** for each chosen integer \\(t\\) (\\(1\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\)) the two consecutive diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{2t}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad D_{2t+1}\n   \\]\n\n   by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\;y=-\\frac12x+(t+1).\n   \\tag{18}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_t\\) passes through exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on \\(D_{2t}\\) or on \\(D_{2t+1}\\); indeed, solving \\(x+y=2t\\) or \\(x+y=2t+1\\) together with (18) yields the integer solutions  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,2t),\\;(3,2t-1),\\dots ,\\bigl(2t-1,\\,t+1\\bigr),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. all points of those two diagonals that belong to \\(S_n\\).  \n   Consequently the replacement does not lose any point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. After performing the replacement for exactly \\(k\\) distinct values of \\(t\\) we have removed \\(2k\\) diagonals and added \\(k\\) sunny lines, leaving a total of  \n\n   \\[\n   n-2k+k = n-k\n   \\]\n\n   non\u2011sunny lines together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines, i.e. exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n   By construction every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them, and precisely \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Answer to the problem)\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\; } .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that no configuration can have more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f3 provides, for each \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\), an explicit construction meeting all requirements. \u220e"

