ion for \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) that already has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (which exists by the induction hypothesis).  \nAdd the line \\(\\ell_{*}\\) (which is *not* sunny because its slope is \\(-1\\)).  \nThe total number of lines becomes \\(n+1\\) and the number of sunny lines stays \\(k\\).  \nSince \\(k'\\le k\\) we can then replace \\(k-k'\\) of the existing sunny lines by non\u2011sunny lines of slopes \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\) (vertical or horizontal) without destroying the covering property (each replacement is performed on a line that already contains at least two of the required points, so the points it loses are still covered by the other lines).  \nThus we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Case 2.** \\(k'>k\\).  \n\nStart again from a configuration for \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) that has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nNow we **replace** the line \\(\\ell_{*}\\) (which we would have added in Case\u202f1) by a *sunny* line \\(\\ell\\) that passes through **all** the new points \\((a,n+2-a)\\) except possibly one of them.  \nBecause the points \\((a,n+2-a)\\) are collinear only on \\(\\ell_{*}\\), a sunny line can contain at most one of them.  \nChoose \\(\\ell\\) to pass through the point \\((1,n+1)\\) and to have a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); then \\(\\ell\\) covers that point and none of the others.  \nAll remaining new points are covered by the original non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_{*}\\) which we keep in the family.  \nConsequently we have added **two** new lines: the sunny line \\(\\ell\\) and the non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_{*}\\).  \nThe total number of lines is now \\(n+2\\); to return to exactly \\(n+1\\) lines we delete one of the old non\u2011sunny lines that is *redundant* (i.e. whose set of covered points is a subset of the union of the other lines).  \nSuch a redundant line always exists: among the original \\(n\\) lines at least one 