e \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following statement \\(\\mathcal H(n)\\) holds:\n\n> For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive step\n\nWe prove \\(\\mathcal H(n+1)\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n+1}= \\mathcal P_{n}\\cup S,\\qquad \nS:=\\{(a,n+2-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S\\) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{*}:x+y=n+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is non\u2011sunny (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nTake a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) for \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) that has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) (such a configuration exists by the hypothesis).  \nWe shall modify \\(\\mathcal C\\) to obtain a configuration for \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\) with any prescribed number \\(k'\\) of sunny lines, where \\(0\\le k'\\le n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a given \\(k'\\)\n\n1. **Add the line \\(\\ell_{*}\\).**  \n   The set \\(\\mathcal C\\cup\\{\\ell_{*}\\}\\) now consists of \\(n+1\\) lines and covers \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\).  \n   The number of sunny lines is still \\(k\\).\n\n2. **If \\(k'\\le k\\).**  \n   Choose any \\(k-k'\\) sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal C\\) and replace each of them by a non\u2011sunny line (vertical or horizontal) that passes through at least one point already covered by the line being replaced.  \n   Because each replaced line contains at least two points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\), after the replacement every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) remains covered.  \n   The total number of sunny lines becomes \\(k'\\) while the total number of lines stays \\(n+1\\).\n\n3. **If \\(k'>k\\).**  \n   Perform the following elementary operation \\(k'-k\\) times.\n\n   *a.* Choose a point \\(P\\in S\\) that is not yet on a sunny line (for instance \\(P=(1,n+1)\\)).  \n   *b.* Intro