ng two families of sunny lines:\n\n* vertical lines \\(V_i:\\;x=i\\) for \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,n\\);\n* horizontal lines \\(H_j:\\;y=j\\) for \\(j=2,3,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nEach vertical line \\(V_i\\) contains exactly the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); each horizontal line \\(H_j\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,j)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\).\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 Construction for any prescribed \\(k\\ge 2\\).*  \n\n1. **Start** with the non\u2011sunny family (1).  \n2. **Choose** \\(k-2\\) distinct indices among \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   * For each chosen index \\(i\\) replace the line \\(L_{i+1}\\) by the vertical line \\(V_i\\).  \n   * For each remaining index \\(j\\) (if any) replace the line \\(L_{j+1}\\) by the horizontal line \\(H_j\\).  \n\n   Because the sets \\(\\{V_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{H_j\\}\\) are pairwise distinct, after the replacement we still have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n   The two lines \\(L_2\\) and \\(L_{n+1}\\) are left unchanged; they are non\u2011sunny.  \n   Consequently the total number of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k = (k-2)+2 = k .\n   \\]\n\n   All points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) remain covered: a point \\((a,b)\\) lies either on a vertical line \\(V_a\\) (if \\(a\\) was selected) or on a horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (if \\(b\\) was selected) or on one of the untouched lines \\(L_{a+b}\\).\n\n   This yields a valid configuration for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Step\u202f5 \u2013 The case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the whole family (1); all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n*Step\u202f6 \u2013 Impossibility of \\(k=1\\).*  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists.  \nLet the sunny line be \\(S\\). All other \\(n-1\\) lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions: horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\).  \n\nConsider the three points \\((1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1)\\).  \nNo two of them lie on a line of slope