*  \nThe equations are of the forms \\(x=\\text{const}\\) and \\(y=\\text{const}\\), which are not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, hence sunny. The bounds on the second coordinate follow from the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Impossibility of a single sunny line)  \nThere is no set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition (2) that contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume such a set exists and denote the unique sunny line by \\(S\\).  \nAll other \\(n-1\\) lines are parallel to one of the directions in (4).  \n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,2),\\qquad P_3=(2,1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nNo line parallel to \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) contains any pair of these points, because the slope of the segment joining any two of them is either \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\).  \nConsequently each of the three points must lie either on \\(S\\) or on a line parallel to \\(\\ell_{x}\\) or \\(\\ell_{y}\\).\n\nIf two of the three points were on distinct non\u2011sunny lines, those two lines would be parallel (both horizontal or both vertical) and would therefore coincide, contradicting the distinctness of the lines.  \nHence at least two of the points must belong to the same line, which can only be \\(S\\).  \n\nIf \\(S\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) it is vertical; if it contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) it is horizontal. In either case \\(S\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell_{x}\\) or \\(\\ell_{y}\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line.  \nThus a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq 1\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny