*Derive a contradiction** from the counting: with at most two points per line, \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2n\\) points, but \\(|\\mathcal{T}_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). Therefore the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny is impossible.\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least one non\u2011sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nAssume, for the sake of contradiction, that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\\) in the plane such that  \n\n* every point \\((a,b)\\) with integers \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* each line is sunny (i.e. none is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 1.  Description of a sunny line\n\nA line that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) has a finite, non\u2011zero slope different from \\(-1\\). Hence it can be written uniquely as  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = m_i x + c_i ,\\qquad m_i\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 2.  Cardinality of the required point set\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{T}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there are exactly \\(s-1\\) pairs \\((a,b)\\) satisfying \\(a+b=s\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{T}_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 3.  A sunny line meets \\(\\mathcal{T}_n\\) in at most two points\n\nSuppose a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_n\\), say  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(a_1,b_1),\\;P_2=(a_2,b_2),\\;P_3=(a_3,b_3),\