 are distinct.  \n   * The construction works for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\nThe two parts together give the exact set of possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{T}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there are exactly \\(s-1\\) pairs \\((a,b)\\) satisfying \\(a+b=s\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{T}_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (At most two points per sunny line)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is **sunny** (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)), then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap\\mathcal{T}_n|\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line has a finite slope \\(m\\) with  \n\n\\[\nm\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence it can be written uniquely as  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y=m x +c ,\\qquad c\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\]\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(L\\) contains three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_n\\),\n\n\\[\nP_1=(a_1,b_1),\\;P_2=(a_2,b_2),\\;P_3=(a_3,b_3),\n\\]\n\nwith \\(a_i,b_i\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}\\) and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\).  \nCollinearity gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}=m . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the two integer vectors  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v}_1=(a_2-a_1,\\;b_2-b_1),\\qquad \n\\mathbf{v}_2=(a_3-a_1,\\;b_3-b_1)\n\\]\n\nare parallel. Let \\((p,q)\\) be a primitive integer vector (i.e. \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)) parallel to them; then there exist non\u2011zero integers \\(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v}_1=\\lambda_1(p,q),\\qquad \\mathbf{v}_2=\\lambda_2(p,q).\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{q}{p}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause each coordinate of the points lies between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\), we have \\(|p|,|q|\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(|p|>1\\) and \\(|q|>1\\) then the step \\((p,q)\\) would move a point outside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) after at most two steps, contradicting the existence of three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_n\\) on the same line. Hence one of \\(|p|,|q|\\) must be \\(1\\).\n\n* If \\(|p|=1\\) then \\(m=q\\) is an integer. The only integer slopes that allow three lattice points inside the triangle are \\(0\\) (horizontal) and \\(\\infty\\) (vertical). Both are excluded for a sunny line.\n\n* If \\(|q|=1\\) then \\(m=1/p\\). The only reciprocal slopes that admit three lattice points in the triangle are \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)). Again this slope is forbidden.\n\nThus the assumption that three points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_n\\) lie on a sunny line leads to a prohibited slope, a contradiction. Hence \\(|L\\cap\\mathcal{T}_n|\\le 2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Necessity: \\(k\\neq n\\)\n\nSuppose there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) satisfying the covering condition and such that **all** of them are sunny.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each line contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_n\\); therefore the total number of points that can be covered is at most \\(2n\\).\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(|\\mathcal{T}_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n .\n\\]\n\nHence the \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot cover all points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_n\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least one non\u2011sunny line, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Sufficiency: construction for every \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.2.1  The set of points to be covered\n\nList the points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_n\\) in lexicographic order:\n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\dots ,(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1).\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) of them.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.2.2  The two fixed non\u2011sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL^{(v)}:\\; x=1 \\qquad\\text{(vertical, parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis)},\n\\]\n\\[\nL^{(d)}:\\; x+y=n+1 \\qquad\\text{(parallel to the line }x+y=0\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nBoth are non\u2011sunny.  \n\\(L^{(v)}\\) contains the points \\((1,b)\\) for \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \n\\(L^{(d)}\\) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\); these are exactly the points on the \u201chypotenuse\u2019\u2019 of the triangle.\n\nThus after adding these two lines, all points with either \\(a=1\\) or \\(a+b=n+1\\) are already covered.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.2.3  Adding \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nWe need to cover the remaining points\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{R}= \\mathcal{T}_n\\setminus\\bigl(L^{(v)}\\cup L^{(d)}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nObserve that \\(\\mathcal{R}\\) consists of the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\).\n\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct slopes  \n\n\\[\nm_1,m_2,\\dots ,m_k\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\n\\]\n\n(e.g. \\(m_i=i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)).  \nFor each \\(i\\) we will construct a line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=m_i x +c_i\n\\]\n\nthat passes through **exactly one** point of \\(\\mathcal{R}\\) and through no other point of \\(\\mathcal{R}\\).  \n\nProceed inductively:\n\n*Let* \\(\\mathcal{R}_0=\\mathcal{R}\\).  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) do:\n\n1. Pick the lexicographically smallest point \\((a_i,b_i)\\) in \\(\\mathcal{R}_{i-1}\\).  \n2. Set \\(c_i=b_i-m_i a_i\\) and define \\(L_i\\) by \\(y=m_i x +c_i\\).  \n3. Remove from \\(\\mathcal{R}_{i-1}\\) every point that lies on \\(L_i\\); denote the remainder by \\(\\mathcal{R}_i\\).\n\nBecause the slope \\(m_i\\) is not \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\), the line \\(L_i\\) cannot be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover, by construction each \\(L_i\\) contains the chosen point \\((a_i,b_i)\\) and, by Lemma\u202f1, at most one further point of \\(\\mathcal{R}_{i-1}\\).  \nSince we always delete all points of \\(\\mathcal{R}_{i-1}\\) that lie on \\(L_i\\), the process terminates after at most \\(k\\) steps with a set \\(\\mathcal{R}_k\\) that may be empty or may still contain points.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.2.4  Filling the remaining points with horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines\n\nAll points that are still uncovered after the previous step have coordinates satisfying \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n-1\\) define the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_j\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b=j\\) lies on \\(H_j\\); consequently the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\{H_j\\mid 2\\le j\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(\\mathcal{R}_k\\).  \n\nThe number of horizontal lines used is \\(n-2\\).  \n\n---\n\n#### 2.2.5  Counting the total number of lines\n\nWe have assembled the following family of lines:\n\n* the vertical line \\(L^{(v)}\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n* the diagonal line \\(L^{(d)}\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\);\n* the \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines \\(H_2,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) (non\u2011sunny).\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n1+1+k+(n-2)=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, the remaining \\(n-k\\) are non\u2011sunny.  \nAll lines are distinct because:\n\n* the vertical line has undefined slope, while all other lines have finite slope;\n* the diagonal line has slope \\(-1\\), which none of the sunny lines possess;\n* the horizontal lines have slope \\(0\\), distinct from the slopes \\(m_i\\) (chosen away from \\(0\\));\n* the sunny lines have distinct slopes \\(m_i\\), hence are distinct from each other.\n\nFinally, by construction every point of \\(\\mathcal{T}_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, so the covering condition is satisfied.\n\nThus for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Conclusion\n\nFrom the necessity part we have proved that \\(k=n\\) is impossible, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nFrom the construction we have shown that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
