"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution, but I have rigorously proved a necessary condition on the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines that can appear in a configuration satisfying the two requirements of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by a counting argument based on the pigeon\u2011hole principle.\n\n* Let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that have to be covered.  \nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n* A line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n  * vertical lines \\(x=c\\);\n  * horizontal lines \\(y=c\\);\n  * diagonal lines \\(x+y=d\\).\n\n  For each of these three families we compute the maximal number of points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) that a single line can contain:\n\n  * If the line is \\(x=c\\) (vertical), then the admissible points are \\((c,b)\\) with\n    \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points.\n  * If the line is \\(y=c\\) (horizontal), the same bound \\(n\\) holds.\n  * If the line is \\(x+y=d\\) (diagonal), the admissible points are the solutions of\n    \\(a+b=d\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\); there are exactly \\(d-1\\le n\\) such points.\n\n  Consequently **every non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).**  \n\n  Denote by \\(t=n-k\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines in a configuration.\n\n* Let \\(M\\) be the maximal number of points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) that a single sunny line can contain.  \n  Since a sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, it meets each\n  vertical line \\(x=c\\) in at most one point, each horizontal line \\(y=c\\) in at most one point,\n  and each diagonal line \\(x+y=d\\) in at most one point.  \n  Therefore a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) (otherwise two of its\n  points would share the same \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate, the same \\(y\\)\u2011coordinate, or the same sum\n  \\(x+y\\), forcing the line to be parallel to one of the forbidden directions). Hence we may take  \n\n  \\[\n  M=2. \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n\n* Using the pigeon\u2011hole principle, the total number of points that can be covered by the\n  \\(n\\) lines is bounded above by  \n\n  \\[\n  \\underbrace{t\\cdot n}_{\\text{non\u2011sunny lines}}+\\underbrace{k\\cdot M}_{\\text{sunny lines}}\n  = (n-k)n+2k = n^{2}-kn+2k. \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  This quantity must be at least \\(|\\mathcal{P}|\\) from (1). Hence\n\n  \\[\n  n^{2}-kn+2k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n  \\]\n\n  Rearranging gives the necessary inequality  \n\n  \\[\n  k\\;\\le\\;\\frac{n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}}{\\,n-2\\,}\n  =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}. \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n  Since \\(k\\) is an integer, (4) yields the explicit bound  \n\n  \\[\n  k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n  \\]\n\nThus **any admissible configuration must satisfy**  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThis is a rigorous necessary condition on the possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) are exactly those required to lie on at least one of the\n\\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \n\\(|\\mathcal{P}|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has\n\\(s-1\\) positive integer solutions \\((a,b)\\). Summing over all admissible \\(s\\),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nA non\u2011sunny line (i.e. a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) (\\(c\\) integer, \\(1\\le c\\le n\\)).  \nPoints of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) on it are \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most\n\\(n+1-c\\le n\\) such points.  \n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\) is symmetric and yields the same bound.  \n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=d\\) (\\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\)).  \nThe admissible points satisfy \\(a+b=d\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\); there are exactly \\(d-1\\le n\\)\nsolutions. \u220e\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 3.**  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2}), (a_{3},b_{3})\\in\\mathcal{P}\\).\nIf two of these points share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, then \\(L\\) is vertical,\ncontradicting sunniness.  \nIf two share the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, then \\(L\\) is horizontal, again a contradiction.  \nIf two share the same sum \\(x+y\\), then \\(L\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\), also a\ncontradiction.  \nSince among three numbers at least two are equal in one of the three categories\n\\(\\{x\\},\\{y\\},\\{x+y\\}\\), a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\nLet the configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(t=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\nBy Lemma\u202f2 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\);\nby Lemma\u202f3 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points. Hence the total number of\ncovered points satisfies\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}|\\le tn+2k=(n-k)n+2k=n^{2}-kn+2k. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nUsing Lemma\u202f1, \\(|\\mathcal{P}|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\). Substituting into (5) gives\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-kn+2k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}}{\\,n-2\\,}\n   =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer, we obtain the necessary condition\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\; }.\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible set of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most\n\\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n*The inequality above is a direct consequence of the pigeon\u2011hole principle applied to\nthe counting of points that must be covered by the given lines.*"
