\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) must be covered, (2) must be at least \\(|\\mathcal P|\\) from (1).  \n   This yields the necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Solving for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\le\\;\n   \\frac{n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{\\,n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor}\n   \\;=\\;\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\lceil n/2\\rceil}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\nThus we have a rigorously proved necessary condition on the possible numbers \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the points that have to lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has \\(s-1\\) positive\ninteger solutions. Summing over all admissible \\(s\\),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA line that is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. a\nnon\u2011sunny line) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\): admissible points are \\((c,b)\\) where\n\\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) of them.  \n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\) is symmetric and yields the same bound.  \n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\): the admissible points satisfy\n\\(a+b=d\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\); there are exactly \\(d-1\\le n\\) such points. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap\\mathcal P|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equation of \\(L\\) as \\(b=ma+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nFor a lattice point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) we have \\(b=ma+c\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Substituting \\(b\\),\n\n\\[\na+ma+c\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{1+m}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\) is integer, the number of admissible \\(a\\)\u2019s is at most the\ninteger part of the right\u2011hand side. The worst case (largest possible number of\nsolutions) occurs when the denominator \\(1+m\\) is as small as possible while staying\npositive; the minimal positive value of \\(|1+m|\\) for \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) is \\(1\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n|L\\cap\\mathcal P|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThe bound is attained, for instance, by the line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)):\nits intersection with \\(\\mathcal P\\) consists of the points\n\\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots,\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor,\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main counting argument\n\nAssume that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the two conditions of the\nproblem. Let  \n\n* \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines,\n* \\(t=n-k\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\); by\nLemma\u202f3 each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\npoints. Therefore the total number of points that can be covered is bounded above by  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot n\\;+\\;k\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n=(n-k)n+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) must be covered, so using Lemma\u202f1 we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\;\n\\frac{n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{\\,n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor}\n=\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\lceil n/2\\rceil } .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer we finally have  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\lceil n/2\\rceil}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\; } .\n\\]\n\nConsequently, **any admissible set of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most**\n\\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\lceil n/2\\rceil}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) **sunny lines**.\nThis inequality is a necessary condition on the possible values of \\(k\\). \u220e"
