* **Vertical**: \\(x=c\\).  The admissible \\(y\\)-values satisfy\n  \\(1\\le y\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them, with equality only\n  for \\(c=1\\) (the left side of the triangle).\n\n* **Horizontal**: \\(y=c\\).  Analogously we obtain at most \\(n\\) points,\n  equality only for \\(c=1\\) (the bottom side).\n\n* **Slope \\(-1\\)**: \\(x+y=c\\).  Integer solutions with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and\n  \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) exist for \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); the number of solutions is at\n  most \\(n\\), and it equals \\(n\\) precisely when \\(c=n+1\\) (the hypotenuse).\n\nHence the claim. \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 4 (Incidence upper bound)  \nLet the configuration consist of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny\nlines.  Denote by \\(I\\) the total number of incidences \u201cpoint \\(\\in\\) line\u201d.\nThen  \n\n\\[\nI\\le 2k+(n-2)(n-k)+2n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) incidences by Lemma\u202f2, giving the\nterm \\(2k\\).\n\nAmong the non\u2011sunny lines, at most two can be the sides of the triangle;\neach such side contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (Lemma\u202f3), contributing\n\\(2n\\) incidences in total.\n\nAll remaining \\(n-k-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines are not sides, so each contains at\nmost \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (again Lemma\u202f3).  Their total contribution is\n\\((n-1)(n-k-2) = (n-2)(n-k)+2n -2n\\).  Adding the contributions yields (3). \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 5 (Incidence lower bound)  \nBecause every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines,\n\n\\[\nI\\ge |S_n| = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Immediate from the hypothesis of the problem and Lemma\u202f1. \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n---\n\n### Theorem  \nIn any admissible configuration one must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nCombine the upper bound (3) with the lower bound (4):\n\n\\[\n2k+(n-2)(n-k)+2n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding the left\u2013hand side,\n\n\\[\n2k + n^{2}-2n -nk +2k +2n\n   = n^{2} - nk + 2k .\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nn^{2} - nk + 2k \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n2n^{2} - 2nk + 4k \\;\\ge\\; n^{2}+n .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\nn^{2} - 2nk + 4k - n \\ge 0\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n(n-2)k \\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\), the factor \\(n-2\\) is positive, and division yields\n\n\\[\nk \\le n-2 .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Corollary  \nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe combinatorial incidence argument shows that any collection of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\)\nmust contain at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  Determining whether every\ninteger \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can actually be realized, or whether\nadditional restrictions apply, remains an open problem.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
