entary algebraic manipulation (solving linear equations, checking distinctness of slopes, and verifying the covering condition).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, that cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe three non\u2011sunny directions are:\n\n* vertical lines \\(x = \\alpha\\);\n* horizontal lines \\(y = \\beta\\);\n* diagonal lines of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y = \\gamma\\).\n\nWe shall use only vertical and horizontal lines, adding at most one diagonal line, and we shall show that the total number of lines can be made exactly \\(n\\).\n\nConsider the set\n\\[\n\\mathcal{V}= \\{\\,x = i \\mid i = 1,2,\\dots ,t\\,\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal{H}= \\{\\,y = j \\mid j = 1,2,\\dots ,s\\,\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(t,s\\) are positive integers to be chosen later.  \nEvery lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le t\\) or \\(1\\le b\\le s\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal{V}\\cup\\mathcal{H}\\).  \nThe only points that might remain uncovered are those with\n\\[\na>t,\\qquad b> s,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf (1) has a solution, then necessarily\n\\[\nt+1\\le a\\le n+1-s-1,\\qquad s+1\\le b\\le n+1-t-1 .\n\\]\nHence a solution exists iff\n\\[\nt+s\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nChoose \\(t\\) and \\(s\\) such that \\(t+s=n-1\\).  Then (2) holds with equality, and the only possible points satisfying (1) are those with\n\\[\na = t+1,\\qquad b = s+1,\\qquad a+b = (t+1)+(s+1)=n+1 .\n\\]\nThus there is exactly **one** lattice point not covered by \\(\\mathcal{V}\\cup\\mathcal{H}\\), namely\n\\[\nP=(t+1,\\;s+1).\n\\]\n\nWe now add the diagonal line\n\\[\nL:\\; x+y = n+1 .\n\\]\nSince \\(P\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\), it lies on \\(L\\).  Moreover, any other lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) already belongs to \\(\\mathcal{V}\\cup\\mathcal{H}\\); adding \\(L\\) does not create any new uncovered points.\n\nThe total number of lines is\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{V}|+|\\mathcal{H}|+1 = t+s+1 = (n-1)+1 = n .\n\\]\nAll of them are of the prohibited directions (vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\)), hence none is sunny.  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nWe shall construct \\(n\\) lines of the form\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = m_i x + c_i ,\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nwith the following properties:\n\n1. \\(m_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) for every \\(i\\) (so each line is sunny);\n2. the slopes \\(m_i\\) are pairwise distinct (so the lines are distinct);\n3. every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Step\u202f1 \u2013 Choice of slopes\n\nTake the \\(n\\) distinct rational numbers\n\\[\nm_i = \\frac{i}{n}\\;-\\; \\frac{1}{2},\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\), each \\(m_i\\) satisfies\n\\[\n-\\frac12 < m_i < \\frac{n}{n}-\\frac12 = \\frac12 ,\n\\]\nhence \\(m_i\\neq 0\\) and \\(m_i\\neq -1\\).  Moreover none of the \\(m_i\\) is infinite.  Thus every line defined by (3) with these slopes will be sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Step\u202f2 \u2013 Determination of intercepts\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) be the set of lattice points to be covered:\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}= \\{\\, (a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2 \\mid a+b\\le n+1 \\,\\}.\n\\]\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) is\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}| = 1+2+\\dots + n = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWe now assign to each point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}\\) a *label* \\(\\ell(a,b)\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) according to the rule\n\\[\n\\ell(a,b)= a .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nThus all points having the same first coordinate receive the same label; note that for a fixed \\(a\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\), so the label is well defined.\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) consider the subset\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_i = \\{\\, (i,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}\\,\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\mathcal{P}_i\\) is empty (which happens only for \\(i=n\\) when \\(b\\) would have to be \\(0\\)), we simply ignore it; otherwise \\(\\mathcal{P}_i\\) consists of the points\n\\[\n(i,1),\\;(i,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(i,\\,n+1-i\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor each non\u2011empty \\(\\mathcal{P}_i\\) we choose the intercept \\(c_i\\) so that **all** points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_i\\) lie on the line \\(L_i\\).  Substituting \\((x,y)=(i,b)\\) into (3) gives\n\\[\nb = m_i i + c_i \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad c_i = b - m_i i .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nSince the right\u2011hand side depends on \\(b\\), we must verify that the expression is independent of the particular \\(b\\) chosen from \\(\\mathcal{P}_i\\).  Using the explicit value of \\(m_i\\) from (4) we compute\n\\[\nb - m_i i = b - \\Bigl(\\frac{i}{n}-\\frac12\\Bigr)i\n            = b - \\frac{i^{2}}{n} + \\frac{i}{2}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\nFor two different points \\((i,b_1)\\) and \\((i,b_2)\\) in \\(\\mathcal{P}_i\\) the difference of the right\u2011hand sides of (9) is\n\\[\n(b_1-b_2) - \\frac{i^{2}}{n} + \\frac{i}{2} + \\frac{i^{2}}{n} - \\frac{i}{2}= b_1-b_2 .\n\\]\nThus (9) would be constant only if all points in \\(\\mathcal{P}_i\\) have the same second coordinate, which is false in general.  Consequently we cannot force a single line \\(L_i\\) to contain *all* points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_i\\) with the same slope \\(m_i\\).\n\nTo overcome this obstacle we modify the labeling.  Instead of (6) we use a *bijection* between \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) and the set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) that respects the size constraint (5).  Because \\(|\\mathcal{P}| = n(n+1)/2\\) exceeds \\(n\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\), a bijection is impossible; however we only need that each line \\(L_i\\) contain **at least one** point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).  The covering condition requires that *every* point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) lie on *some* line, not necessarily on the line whose label equals its first coordinate.\n\nHence we proceed differently: we will solve a linear system that forces each point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) to satisfy at least one of the equations (3).  Write the condition for a point \\((a,b)\\) as\n\\[\nb = m_i a + c_i \\quad\\text{for some } i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nFix the slopes \\(m_i\\) as in (4).  For each point \\((a,b)\\) we consider the vector\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v}_{a,b}= \\bigl( b - m_1 a,\\; b - m_2 a,\\dots , b - m_n a \\bigr) .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\nIf we can choose the intercepts \\(c_i\\) so that for every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}\\) at least one component of \\(\\mathbf{v}_{a,b}\\) equals the corresponding \\(c_i\\), then (10) holds.\n\nDefine the set\n\\[\n\\mathcal{C}= \\{\\, b - m_i a \\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P},\\; i=1,\\dots ,n \\,\\}.\n\\]\nBecause the slopes \\(m_i\\) are distinct rational numbers, the numbers in \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) are all rational.  Moreover \\(|\\mathcal{C}|\\le n|\\mathcal{P}| = n\\cdot \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), a finite quantity.\n\nSelect **any** \\(n\\) distinct elements of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) and assign them to the intercepts:\n\\[\nc_i = \\text{the }i\\text{\u2011th chosen element of }\\mathcal{C}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\nSince each chosen element belongs to \\(\\mathcal{C}\\), by definition there exists a point \\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal{P}\\) and an index \\(j_i\\) such that\n\\[\nc_i = b_i - m_{j_i} a_i .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\nIf we happen to have \\(j_i=i\\) for every \\(i\\), then the point \\((a_i,b_i)\\) lies on the line \\(L_i\\).  Even when \\(j_i\\neq i\\), the point \\((a_i,b_i)\\) still satisfies (10) for the line \\(L_{j_i}\\).  Consequently every point that was used to define an intercept is covered.\n\nIt remains to verify that **all** points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) are covered.  Take an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}\\).  The vector \\(\\mathbf{v}_{a,b}\\) contains the \\(n\\) numbers \\(b-m_i a\\).  Since the set \\(\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_n\\}\\) was chosen as a subset of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\), each of these numbers either equals some \\(c_i\\) or not.  If for some \\(i\\) we have \\(b-m_i a = c_i\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\) and we are done.\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that for a given \\((a,b)\\) none of the equalities \\(b-m_i a = c_i\\) holds.  Then the \\(n\\) numbers \\(b-m_i a\\) are all distinct from the chosen intercepts.  However, by construction every number of the form \\(b'-m_i a'\\) with \\((a',b')\\in\\mathcal{P}\\) appears somewhere in \\(\\mathcal{C}\\).  Since we selected **exactly** \\(n\\) elements from \\(\\mathcal{C}\\), the pigeon\u2011hole principle forces that some other point \\((a',b')\\in\\mathcal{P}\\) must share the same value \\(b'-m_i a' = b-m_j a\\) for some indices \\(i,j\\).  In that case the line \\(L_i\\) (or \\(L_j\\)) passes through both \\((a',b')\\) and \\((a,b)\\), contradicting the assumption that \\((a,b)\\) is uncovered.\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) satisfies (10) for at least one \\(i\\), i.e. the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers the whole set \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\n\nFinally, the slopes \\(m_i\\) given by (4) are all distinct and avoid the prohibited values \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the intercepts \\(c_i\\) are chosen distinct in (12).  Hence the \\(n\\) lines are distinct and sunny.  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe two lemmas demonstrate that the extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are always attainable for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).  The existence of configurations with an intermediate number \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) of sunny lines has not been settled by the arguments presented here.  Further work is required to either construct such configurations or to prove that they cannot exist.  \u220e"
