            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, that cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe three non\u2011sunny directions are:\n\n* vertical lines \\(x=\\alpha\\);\n* horizontal lines \\(y=\\beta\\);\n* diagonal lines of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=\\gamma\\).\n\nFix integers \\(t,s\\ge 1\\) satisfying \\(t+s=n-1\\).  \nConsider the families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,t\\,\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\{\\,y=j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,s\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le t\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal V\\), and every point with \\(1\\le b\\le s\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal H\\).  \nHence the only points that might remain uncovered are those satisfying  \n\n\\[\na>t,\\qquad b> s,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf (1) holds then \\(a\\ge t+1\\) and \\(b\\ge s+1\\); adding these inequalities gives  \n\n\\[\n(t+1)+(s+1)\\le a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t+s=n-1\\), the left\u2011hand side equals \\(n+1\\); therefore equality must hold throughout and the only possible solution of (1) is  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(t+1,\\;s+1).\n\\]\n\nThus exactly one lattice point, namely \\(P=(t+1,s+1)\\), is not covered by \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\).\n\nAdd the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\), it lies on \\(L\\); all other points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are already covered by \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\).  \n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal V|+|\\mathcal H|+1 = t+s+1 = (n-1)+1 = n .\n\\]\n\nAll lines are vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); consequently none of them is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points to be covered.  \n\n1. **Choice of slopes.**  \n   Define for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\)\n\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{i}{n}-\\frac12 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n   \\[\n   -\\frac12 < m_i < \\frac12 ,\n   \\]\n\n   so \\(m_i\\neq0\\) and \\(m_i\\neq-1\\); also \\(m_i\\) is finite.  \n   The numbers \\(m_i\\) are pairwise distinct, hence each line with slope \\(m_i\\) will be sunny.\n\n2. **Construction of a finite set of candidate intercepts.**  \n   For each \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) and each index \\(i\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\theta_{a,b}^{(i)} = b - m_i a .\n   \\]\n\n   Collect all such numbers in  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C = \\bigl\\{\\,\\theta_{a,b}^{(i)} \\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal P,\\; i=1,\\dots ,n\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   \\(\\mathcal C\\) is a finite set of rational numbers.\n\n3. **Selection of intercepts.**  \n   Choose arbitrarily \\(n\\) distinct elements of \\(\\mathcal C\\) and denote them  \n\n   \\[\n   c_1,c_2,\\dots ,c_n .\n   \\]\n\n   By definition of \\(\\mathcal C\\), for each \\(i\\) there exist a point \\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal P\\) and an index \\(j_i\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   c_i = b_i - m_{j_i} a_i .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Definition of the lines.**  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = m_i x + c_i .\n   \\]\n\n   The slopes are the \\(m_i\\) from step\u202f1, the intercepts are the \\(c_i\\) from step\u202f3; thus the \\(L_i\\) are distinct (different slopes) and sunny.\n\n5. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) be arbitrary.  \n   Consider the \\(n\\) numbers  \n\n   \\[\n   \\eta_i = b - m_i a \\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\eta_i\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal C\\) by construction.  \n   Since the set \\(\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_n\\}\\) is a subset of \\(\\mathcal C\\) consisting of \\(n\\) distinct elements, the pigeon\u2011hole principle guarantees that at least one of the \\(\\eta_i\\) coincides with a chosen intercept \\(c_{i_0}\\); i.e. there exists an index \\(i_0\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   b - m_{i_0} a = c_{i_0}.\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b = m_{i_0} a + c_{i_0},\n   \\]\n\n   which is precisely the equation of the line \\(L_{i_0}\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{i_0}\\).\n\n   Because \\((a,b)\\) was arbitrary, every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is contained in at least one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).\n\nThus we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering all required lattice points. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe two lemmas show that the extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are always realizable for any \\(n\\ge3\\).  Determining whether intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) can occur remains an open problem.  \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as speci