mid (b_{2}-b_{1})\\).  \n    Because \\(|a_{2}-a_{1}|\\le n-1\\) and \\(|b_{2}-b_{1}|\\le n-1\\), the only possibilities for \\((p,q)\\) are \\((\\pm1,0)\\) (horizontal), \\((0,\\pm1)\\) (vertical) or \\((\\pm1,\\pm1)\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n    All three are excluded by the definition of a sunny line.  \n    Hence a sunny line cannot contain three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\); it can contain at most two.  \n\n*   **Step\u202f3 \u2013 Deriving the inequality for \\(k\\).**  \n    The set \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) has \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.  \n    Suppose exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n    The \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points (Lemma\u202f2).  \n    The remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines each lie in one of the three forbidden directions; each such line can contain arbitrarily many points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).  \n    In the most economical situation each non\u2011sunny line would cover exactly the same number of points, namely \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}{2k}\\).  \n    Since a line can contain at most \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points (the diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(t=\\lceil\\frac{n+2}{2}\\rceil\\) attains this maximum), we must have  \n    \\[\n    \\frac{n(n+1)}{2k}\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n    \\]  \n    Because \\(k\\) is an integer, this yields the bound \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines that satisfy the covering condition.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider the family of lines\n\\[\nL_{t}:\\;x+y=t\\qquad (t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\nEach line \\(L_{t}\\) has slope \\(-1\\); therefore it is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and consequently **not** sunny.  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a+b\\ge 2\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\).  \nThe lines are distinct because they have different constant terms. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(\\ell\\) contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) in the form \\(y=mx+c\\) with real slope \\(m\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), we have\n\\[\nm\\notin\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAssume that \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\in\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\),\n\\[\nb_{i}=ma_{i}+c .\n\\]\nSubtracting the equations for \\(i=1,2\\) gives\n\\[\nb_{2}-b_{1}=m(a_{2}-a_{1}) .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nBecause the coordinates are integers, the left\u2013hand side of (1) is an integer; hence \\(m\\) must be rational.  \nWrite \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  \nEquation (1) becomes\n\\[\nq\\,(b_{2}-b_{1})=p\\,(a_{2}-a_{1}) .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\in\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\), we have \\(1\\le a_{i},b_{i}\\le n\\) and \\(a_{i}+b_{i}\\le n+1\\).  \nConsequently\n\\[\n|a_{2}-a_{1}|\\le n-1,\\qquad |b_{2}-b_{1}|\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) we see that \\(q\\) divides \\(a_{2}-a_{1}\\) and \\(p\\) divides \\(b_{2}-b_{1}\\).  \nBecause of (3) the only possibilities compatible with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) are\n\\[\n(q,p)=(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,1),\\;(1,-1).\n\\]\nThese correspond respectively to slopes \\(m=0\\) (horizontal), \\(m=\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(m=\\pm1\\).  \nAll three slopes are excluded by the hypothesis that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \nHence our assumption that three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\) leads to a contradiction.  \nTherefore a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f3  \n*If exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, then \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe set \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) consists of\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\npoints.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line covers at most two points, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.  \nConsequently the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines must cover at least\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\npoints.\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \nAmong all such lines the one that contains the largest number of points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) is a diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) with\n\\(t=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\); it contains exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.  \nHence each non\u2011sunny line can cover **at most** \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\n\nTherefore\n\\[\n(n-k)\\,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k .\n\\]\nSince \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}-\\frac12\\), a short calculation yields\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the claimed bound\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\qquad\\qquad\\Box\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Consequences  \n\n*   Lemma\u202f1 shows that the value \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.  \n*   Lemma\u202f3 gives a necessary condition for any admissible \\(k\\):\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus the problem reduces to determining, for each integer \\(n\\ge3\\), which integers in the interval \\([0,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor]\\) can actually be realized. The present work establishes the lower endpoint and a universal upper bound, but does not settle the existence of configurations for the intermediate values of \\(k\\). \u220e"
