            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines that cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider the family of lines  \n\\[\nL_{t}:\\;x+y=t\\qquad (t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]  \nEach line has slope \\(-1\\); hence it is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **not** sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\) then \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines \\(L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\), which are clearly distinct. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(\\ell\\) contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) with real slope \\(m\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), we have  \n\\[\nm\\notin\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAssume that three distinct points \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\),\n\\[\nb_{i}=ma_{i}+c .\n\\]  \nSubtracting the equations for \\(i=1,2\\) yields\n\\[\nb_{2}-b_{1}=m(a_{2}-a_{1}) . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince the coordinates are integers, the left\u2013hand side of (1) is an integer; therefore \\(m\\) must be rational.  \nWrite \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  \nEquation (1) becomes\n\\[\nq\\,(b_{2}-b_{1})=p\\,(a_{2}-a_{1}) . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\) we have\n\\[\n1\\le a_{i},b_{i}\\le n,\\qquad a_{i}+b_{i}\\le n+1,\n\\]\nhence\n\\[\n|a_{2}-a_{1}|\\le n-1,\\qquad |b_{2}-b_{1}|\\le n-1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) we see that \\(q\\mid (a_{2}-a_{1})\\) and \\(p\\mid (b_{2}-b_{1})\\).  \nTogether with (3) and the coprimality of \\(p,q\\) the only possibilities are  \n\n* \\(q=1,\\;p=0\\)   (horizontal line, \\(m=0\\));  \n* \\(q=0,\\;p=1\\)   (vertical line, undefined slope);  \n* \\(q=1,\\;p=\\pm1\\) (slope \\(\\pm1\\), in particular \\(m=-1\\)).  \n\nAll three cases are excluded by the definition of a sunny line.  \nThus our assumption that three points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\) leads to a contradiction, and a sunny line can contain at most two such points. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f3  \n*If exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, then*\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\;\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe set \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) has\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\npoints.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line covers at most two points, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.  \nConsequently the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines must cover at least\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_{n}|-2k=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\npoints.\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n\n* a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\);\n* a vertical line \\(x=a\\) is analogous and also contains at most \\(n\\) points;\n* a line of slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=t\\)) contains exactly \\(t-1\\) points, and the maximum of \\(t-1\\) over all admissible \\(t\\) is \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le n\\).\n\nHence **every** non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\nTherefore we must have\n\\[\n(n-k)\\,n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k .\n\\]  \nExpanding and rearranging,\n\\[\nn^{2}-nk\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}-2k\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n2n^{2}-2nk\\;\\ge\\;n^{2}+n-4k\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nn^{2}-2nk+n\\;\\ge\\;-4k\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n4k\\;\\le\\;2nk-n^{2}+n .\n\\]  \nDividing by \\(4\\) gives\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\;\\frac{2nk-n^{2}+n}{4}\n      \\;=\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)} .\n\\]  \nSince \\(k\\) is an integer, inequality (4) follows. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Consequences  \n\n* Lemma\u202f1 shows that the lower bound \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.  \n* Lemma\u202f3 provides a universal upper bound for the number of sunny lines; in particular it implies \\(k\\le n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nThus any admissible value of \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\\[\n0\\;\\le\\;k\\;\\le\\;\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]  \nThe problem of deciding whether every integer in this interval can actually be realized by a suitable configuration of \\(n\\) lines remains open. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
