r of lines is \\(n\\), the number of sunny lines\nsatisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).  The inequality \\(k\\ge 0\\) is trivial, so (1) holds as a\nnecessary condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n1. **Diagonal lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\) take the diagonal line\n   \\(d_{c}: x+y=c\\).  There are \\(n-2\\) of them; all are non\u2011sunny.\n\n2. **Vertical and horizontal lines.**  \n   Add the vertical line \\(v:x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(h:y=1\\).  These\n   two lines are also non\u2011sunny.  At this stage we have exactly\n   \\((n-2)+2=n\\) lines, all non\u2011sunny.\n\n3. **Insertion of sunny lines.**  \n   To obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we replace \\(k\\) of the diagonal lines\n   \\(d_{c}\\) by \\(k\\) arbitrary sunny lines.  For each replaced diagonal line\n   \\(d_{c}\\) choose a point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) that is not on any\n   of the remaining lines (such a point exists because the points on a\n   diagonal have distinct abscissae and ordinates).  Through this point draw\n   a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); this line is sunny\n   and does not coincide with any line already present.  Performing the\n   replacement for \\(k\\) distinct values of \\(c\\) yields a family of exactly\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n**Verification of coverage.**  \n\n* Points with sum \\(c\\) for \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\): if the diagonal line\n  \\(d_{c}\\) was kept, the whole set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=c\\}\\) is covered.\n  If \\(d_{c}\\) was replaced, the chosen sunny line passes through at least\n  one point of that diagonal, and the remaining points of the same diagonal\n  are covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) or the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)\n  (because on the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) the coordinates range from\n  \\((1,c-1)\\) to \